import React, { useState, useMemo, useReducer, useEffect } from "react";

const TOPICS_DATA = [{"id":"bonds","name":"Bonds & Long-Term Debt","icon":"💵","group":"Liabilities","overview":"Why a bond's price moves opposite to market rates, and how the effective-interest method makes the income statement tell the economic truth.","lesson":[{"h":"The core tension: a fixed promise in a moving market","p":["A bond is a contract to pay a fixed stream: periodic interest at the STATED (coupon) rate on the face amount, plus the face amount at maturity. Those cash flows never change once the bond is printed.","But the market's required return — the MARKET (effective, yield) rate — moves constantly. The only thing that can adjust to reconcile a fixed promise with a moving required return is the PRICE an investor will pay today.","So the issue price is whatever makes the bond's fixed cash flows yield exactly the market rate. That is why price = present value of all cash flows discounted at the MARKET rate — never the stated rate. The stated rate only sizes the cash coupons; the market rate does the discounting."]},{"h":"Premium, discount, par — and why","p":["If stated > market: the bond pays more cash interest than investors currently demand. They bid the price UP above face to compete for it. The excess paid is a PREMIUM. Effectively, the issuer 'pre-collects' the above-market coupons.","If stated < market: the bond pays too little. Nobody pays face for an under-paying bond, so the price falls BELOW face — a DISCOUNT. The discount compensates the investor for the skimpy coupons.","If stated = market: price = face, issued at PAR.","Memory anchor: the bond account always carries FACE. The premium or discount is a separate valuation account that bridges face to the actual issue price, and it amortizes to zero by maturity."]},{"h":"Effective-interest method: making expense reflect economics","p":["Cash interest paid each period is fixed: face × stated rate. But the true economic cost of borrowing is the market rate applied to what you actually owe right now (the carrying value).","So interest EXPENSE = beginning carrying value × market rate (per period). The difference between expense and cash is the amortization of the premium/discount.","Discount case: expense > cash, so the shortfall is added to carrying value — it climbs toward face. Premium case: expense < cash, so carrying value is written down toward face.","By maturity, the carrying value equals face exactly, and the premium/discount account is fully amortized. The straight-line method is simpler but GAAP only permits it when results aren't materially different; effective-interest is the conceptually correct method."]},{"h":"Worked example (read every line)","p":["$100,000 face, 6% stated, 8% market, 5 years, annual interest. Stated < market → discount.","Cash interest = 100,000 × 6% = $6,000 per year (fixed forever).","Issue price = 100,000 × PV-single(8%,5) + 6,000 × PV-annuity(8%,5) = 100,000 × 0.68058 + 6,000 × 3.99271 ≈ 68,058 + 23,956 = $92,015.","Year 1 expense = 92,015 × 8% = $7,361. Amortization = 7,361 − 6,000 = $1,361. New carrying value = 92,015 + 1,361 = $93,376.","Entry: Dr Interest Expense 7,361 / Cr Cash 6,000 / Cr Discount on Bonds Payable 1,361. Repeat each year; carrying value reaches $100,000 at maturity."]},{"h":"Straight-line amortization (the simpler alternative)","p":["The effective-interest method is required under GAAP, but many courses also teach straight-line amortization because it's simpler and still appears on exams. Straight-line spreads the discount or premium evenly across the life of the bond — same amount every period.","Example: a $7,985 discount on a 5-year bond amortizes at $7,985 ÷ 5 = $1,597 per year. So each year's interest expense is the cash coupon ($6,000) plus that flat $1,597 = $7,597, every single year.","The contrast: under effective-interest, expense changes each period (it tracks the carrying value); under straight-line, expense is identical every period. Effective-interest is more accurate and GAAP-preferred; straight-line is allowed only when the difference isn't material."]},{"h":"Bonds issued between interest dates","p":["Bonds are often sold partway through an interest period. The rule: the buyer pays the issuer the accrued interest since the last payment date, then gets it all back at the next full coupon. This keeps things fair — the buyer holds the bond only part of the period but receives a full coupon, so they pre-pay the part they didn't earn.","Example: a $100,000, 12% bond (paid semiannually) is issued two months after the last interest date. Accrued interest collected at issuance = $100,000 × 12% × 2/12 = $2,000. At the next semiannual payment the company pays the full $6,000, but $2,000 of that just returns the buyer's own money — so true interest expense for the period is only $4,000.","The trap: the cash paid at the next coupon ($6,000) is not all expense. Subtract the accrued interest you collected up front."]},{"h":"Retiring bonds early (extinguishment of debt)","p":["Companies often buy back (call) their bonds before maturity. When they do, the difference between the reacquisition price and the bond's carrying value at that moment is a gain or loss, recognized immediately in income.","If you pay MORE than carrying value to retire the debt, that's a loss; if you pay LESS, that's a gain. Example: a bond with a $96,000 carrying value called at 102 (i.e., $102,000) produces a $6,000 loss. A premium bond carried at $104,000 retired for $101,000 produces a $3,000 gain.","Watch the direction — it's the opposite of intuition for some students. Paying more to get rid of a debt is a loss because you gave up more than the debt was worth on your books."]},{"h":"Notes payable: zero-interest and below-market notes","p":["Long-term notes work like bonds but often have a missing or unrealistic interest rate, so you have to find the real economics using present value. A zero-interest-bearing note pays no stated interest — you record it at the present value of the future payment, and the difference between face and PV is a discount that becomes interest expense over time.","Example: issue a 3-year, $50,000 note with no stated interest when the market rate is 10%. Cash received = $50,000 ÷ 1.10³ = $37,566. The $12,434 difference is a discount; year-one interest expense = $37,566 × 10% = $3,757. You're paying interest — it's just buried in the face amount.","When a note's stated rate is unrealistic (say 3% when the market wants 10%), you 'impute' the market rate and record the note at the present value computed with that rate, not the face. The stated rate sizes the small cash coupons; the market rate does the discounting — the same split you learned with bonds."]},{"h":"Convertible bonds and bonds with warrants","p":["Convertible bonds let the holder swap the bond for stock. Under current U.S. GAAP, a plain convertible bond is recorded entirely as debt — no portion is split off to equity at issuance. (This is the simplified treatment; some textbooks still show an older split method, so follow your course.)","Bonds issued WITH detachable warrants are different — here you DO split the proceeds, because the warrant is a separate instrument that can trade on its own. You allocate the proceeds between the bonds and the warrants based on their relative fair values, with the warrant portion going to equity (paid-in capital).","Example: $100,000 of proceeds from bonds-with-warrants where the bonds' fair value is $97,000 and warrants' is $5,000. Allocate proportionally: bonds get 100,000 × (97/102) = $95,098; warrants get 100,000 × (5/102) = $4,902 to equity."]},{"h":"Bond issue costs","p":["Issuing bonds isn't free — there are underwriting fees, legal costs, and printing. Under current GAAP, these issue costs reduce the carrying value of the bond (they're treated like an additional discount), not recorded as a separate asset.","Example: $500,000 of bonds issued at par with $15,000 of issue costs. The net carrying value is $485,000. Those costs are then amortized to interest expense over the life of the bond — under straight-line, $15,000 ÷ 5 years = $3,000 per year added to interest expense.","The effect: issue costs raise the effective interest rate slightly, because you received less cash ($485,000) but still must repay the full $500,000."]},{"h":"Troubled debt restructuring","p":["When a borrower is in financial trouble, a creditor may grant concessions — this is a troubled debt restructuring (TDR). There are two forms: settlement (the debt is satisfied now) and modification (the terms are changed going forward).","Settlement: the debtor transfers an asset (or stock) to satisfy the debt. Two gains can arise. Example: debt with a $200,000 carrying value is settled by transferring land (book value $120,000, fair value $150,000). Gain on settlement = $200,000 − $150,000 = $50,000; plus a $30,000 gain on the asset itself ($150,000 fair value − $120,000 book). They're recorded separately.","Modification of terms: the creditor reduces the principal, rate, or extends the due date. The debtor's rule: if the total future cash flows under the new terms are LESS than the carrying value, recognize a gain for the difference. If the new total future flows still equal or exceed carrying value, no gain is recorded — instead a new lower effective rate is computed and interest is recognized going forward."]}],"keyFormulas":["Issue price = PV(face, market rate) + PV(coupons annuity, market rate)","Interest expense = beginning carrying value × market rate per period","Cash interest = face × stated rate per period","Amortization = interest expense − cash interest"],"bank":[{"d":"easy","q":"A bond's stated rate is 7% and the market rate is 9%. The bond is issued at:","choices":["A premium","Par","A discount","Cannot be determined"],"answer":2,"why":["Premium requires stated > market.","Par requires stated = market.","Stated (7%) < market (9%): the coupons are below market demand, so price falls below face → discount.","It can be determined: compare the two rates."]},{"d":"easy","q":"The issue price of a bond is the present value of its cash flows discounted at the:","choices":["Stated rate","Market (effective) rate","Prime rate","Average of stated and market"],"answer":1,"why":["The stated rate only sizes the coupons; it does not discount.","Correct — discounting always uses the market/effective rate, which is what makes the bond yield the market return.","The prime rate is unrelated to bond valuation here.","No averaging is involved."]},{"d":"easy","q":"A $200,000 bond with a 6% stated rate pays interest semiannually. Each cash interest payment is:","choices":["$12,000","$6,000","$3,000","$24,000"],"answer":1,"why":["$12,000 would be the full annual amount.","Correct: 200,000 × 6% × 6/12 = $6,000 per semiannual payment.","$3,000 uses the wrong rate.","$24,000 is double the annual interest."]},{"d":"easy","q":"Over its life, a bond issued at a premium has a carrying value that:","choices":["Increases toward face","Decreases toward face","Stays constant","Equals zero at maturity"],"answer":1,"why":["Increasing toward face describes a discount bond.","Correct: premium amortization writes the carrying value down until it equals face at maturity.","It changes each period as the premium amortizes.","It equals face, not zero, at maturity."]},{"d":"medium","q":"A bond is issued at a discount. In year 2 versus year 1, the interest expense will be:","choices":["Lower","Higher","The same","Equal to cash interest"],"answer":1,"why":["Expense rises, not falls, for a discount bond.","Correct: carrying value grows each period for a discount bond, and expense = carrying value × market rate, so expense increases.","It changes because carrying value changes.","Expense never equals cash interest except at par."]},{"d":"medium","q":"A bond is issued at a premium. The amount of premium amortized each period under effective interest:","choices":["Decreases over time","Increases over time","Stays constant","Equals the cash interest"],"answer":1,"why":["Amortization grows, not shrinks: as carrying value declines, expense falls, so the gap between fixed cash and falling expense widens.","Correct: premium amortization = cash interest − interest expense. As carrying value declines each period, expense falls, so the amortization amount increases over the bond's life.","Effective-interest amortization is never constant — that pattern describes the straight-line method.","Amortization is the difference between cash and expense, not the cash interest itself."]},{"d":"medium","q":"A $100,000, 5-year bond with a 10% stated rate is issued at 8% market, interest annual. The issue price is approximately:","choices":["$92,015","$100,000","$107,985","$108,000 exactly"],"answer":2,"why":["$92,015 would be a discount (stated < market).","Par requires stated = market.","Correct: stated (10%) > market (8%) → premium. PV at 8% of $10,000 coupons + $100,000 face ≈ $107,985.","The premium is not a round $8,000; it must be present-valued."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Bond issue costs (e.g., underwriting fees) under current U.S. GAAP are:","choices":["Expensed immediately","Recorded as a separate asset and amortized","Netted against the carrying value of the debt (a debt discount)","Added to interest expense each period in full"],"answer":2,"why":["They are not expensed up front.","The old 'deferred charge asset' treatment was eliminated.","Correct: issue costs reduce the carrying amount of the liability, like an additional discount, and amortize via effective interest.","They are amortized, not expensed in full each period."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A bond with a $96,000 carrying value (face $100,000, unamortized discount $4,000) is retired early at 102 (i.e., 102% of face). The gain or loss on extinguishment is:","choices":["$2,000 gain","$6,000 loss","$4,000 loss","$2,000 loss"],"answer":1,"why":["A gain would require the reacquisition price to be below carrying value.","Correct: reacquisition price = 102% × 100,000 = $102,000. Loss = carrying value 96,000 − 102,000 = −$6,000 loss.","$4,000 ignores the call premium paid.","$2,000 miscomputes the reacquisition price."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A bond is issued BETWEEN interest dates. The issuer receives the issue price PLUS accrued interest. The accrued interest received is:","choices":["Additional bond premium","A liability that is returned to the buyer at the next interest date","Interest revenue to the issuer","Recorded as a discount"],"answer":1,"why":["It is not premium; premium relates to the rate differential.","Correct: the buyer pre-pays interest accrued since the last coupon date; the issuer holds it and effectively returns it by paying a full coupon at the next date.","The issuer pays interest, it does not earn revenue here.","It is not a discount."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Under the effective-interest method, if a bond is issued at par, the periodic amortization of premium/discount is:","choices":["Equal to the cash interest","Zero","Equal to the market rate times face","Increasing over time"],"answer":1,"why":["Cash interest is paid, but there is no premium/discount to amortize.","Correct: at par there is no premium or discount, so amortization is zero and expense equals cash interest every period.","That describes interest expense, not amortization.","There is nothing to amortize, so it cannot increase."]},{"d":"easy","q":"The account 'Discount on Bonds Payable' is best described as:","choices":["An asset","A contra-liability that reduces the carrying value of the bond","An expense","A contra-asset"],"answer":1,"why":["It is not an asset; it relates to a liability.","Correct: a discount is a contra-liability — it's subtracted from Bonds Payable so the net carrying value is below face.","It is amortized to interest expense over time but is not itself an expense account.","It offsets a liability, not an asset."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Under the effective-interest method, as a DISCOUNT bond approaches maturity, the annual interest expense:","choices":["Decreases each year","Increases each year","Stays constant","Equals zero"],"answer":1,"why":["Expense rises, not falls, for a discount bond.","Correct: carrying value climbs each year for a discount bond, and expense = carrying value × market rate, so expense increases annually.","Only the straight-line method gives constant expense.","Expense is positive every period."]},{"d":"medium","q":"A bond sold at par on an interest date has total interest expense over its life equal to:","choices":["Total cash interest paid","Total cash interest plus the premium","Total cash interest minus the discount","Zero"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: at par there's no premium or discount to amortize, so lifetime interest expense exactly equals the total cash coupons paid.","There is no premium at par.","There is no discount at par.","Interest expense is substantial, not zero."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Two bonds have identical face, term, and market rate, but Bond A has a higher stated rate than Bond B. Compared to Bond B, Bond A's issue price is:","choices":["Lower","Higher","The same","Cannot be compared"],"answer":1,"why":["A higher coupon makes the bond more valuable, not less.","Correct: a higher stated rate means larger coupons, so investors pay more — Bond A has the higher issue price (more likely a premium).","Different coupons produce different prices.","They can be compared; the higher coupon commands the higher price."]},{"d":"hard","q":"In a troubled-debt restructuring SETTLEMENT, a debtor transfers an asset (book value $120,000, fair value $150,000) to settle debt with a carrying value of $200,000. The debtor recognizes:","choices":["A $50,000 gain on debt settlement and a $30,000 gain on the asset","An $80,000 gain only","A $50,000 loss","No gain or loss"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: gain on settlement = debt CV 200,000 − asset FV 150,000 = $50,000; separately, the asset is revalued to fair value, giving a $30,000 gain (150,000 − 120,000 book).","The two gains are recognized separately, not merged into $80,000 as a single line.","It is a gain, not a loss — the debt exceeded the asset's value.","Gains arise because the debt settled for less than its carrying value."]},{"d":"hard","q":"In a TDR by MODIFICATION of terms, if the total future undiscounted cash flows under the new terms ($520,000) EXCEED the loan's carrying value ($500,000), the debtor recognizes:","choices":["A $20,000 gain immediately","No gain; a new lower effective interest rate is computed","A $20,000 loss","A $500,000 gain"],"answer":1,"why":["No immediate gain arises when future flows exceed carrying value.","Correct: when total future cash flows ≥ carrying value, no gain is recorded; instead a new effective interest rate is found that equates the flows to the carrying value.","There is no loss in this case.","The carrying value is not a gain."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Under STRAIGHT-LINE amortization, a $9,000 discount on a 6-year bond amortizes how much per year?","choices":["$1,500","$1,800","$900","$1,000"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: straight-line spreads it evenly — $9,000 ÷ 6 years = $1,500 per year, every year.","$1,800 would be over 5 years.","$900 halves it incorrectly.","$1,000 would be over 9 years."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Compared to effective-interest, straight-line amortization produces interest expense that is:","choices":["The same total over the bond's life but flat each period","Higher in total","Lower in total","Always larger each period"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: both methods amortize the same total discount/premium, so lifetime expense matches; straight-line just makes each period's expense identical instead of changing.","Totals are equal, not higher.","Totals are equal, not lower.","Per-period amounts differ but neither is always larger."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A $100,000, 12% bond paying semiannual interest is issued 2 months after the last interest date. How much accrued interest does the issuer collect from the buyer at issuance?","choices":["$2,000","$6,000","$4,000","$1,000"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: $100,000 × 12% × 2/12 = $2,000 — the buyer pre-pays the interest for the period before they owned it.","$6,000 is the full semiannual coupon, not the accrued portion.","$4,000 is the net expense after the next coupon, not the amount collected.","$1,000 uses the wrong fraction."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A bond with a carrying value of $96,000 is called early at 102 (i.e., $102,000). The result is:","choices":["A $6,000 loss on extinguishment","A $6,000 gain","A $2,000 loss","No gain or loss"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: paying $102,000 to retire debt carried at $96,000 means giving up $6,000 more than the debt was worth — a loss.","Paying more than carrying value is a loss, not a gain.","$2,000 ignores the carrying value.","There is a difference, so a gain/loss exists."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A 3-year, $50,000 zero-interest-bearing note is issued when the market rate is 10%. The note is recorded initially at:","choices":["$37,566 (the present value of $50,000)","$50,000 (the face amount)","$45,000","$35,000"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: with no stated interest, you record the present value: $50,000 ÷ 1.10³ ≈ $37,566. The $12,434 gap is a discount amortized to interest expense.","Face value ignores the time value of money — you didn't receive $50,000 today.","$45,000 isn't the PV at 10%.","$35,000 over-discounts."]},{"d":"medium","q":"When a note's stated rate is unrealistically low versus the market rate, GAAP requires you to:","choices":["Impute the market rate and record the note at present value","Record it at face value","Ignore the difference","Use the stated rate for everything"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: you impute (substitute) the market rate, record the note at the PV computed with that rate, and recognize the difference as a discount.","Face value would overstate the liability and understate interest.","The difference is the whole point — it can't be ignored.","The stated rate sizes the cash coupons, but the market rate does the discounting."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Bonds are issued WITH detachable warrants for $100,000. The bonds' fair value is $97,000 and the warrants' is $5,000. How much is allocated to the warrants (equity)?","choices":["$4,902","$5,000","$3,000","$0"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: allocate proportionally — $100,000 × (5,000 / 102,000) = $4,902 to the warrants in equity.","$5,000 is the fair value, but proceeds are allocated by relative fair value since total FV ($102,000) exceeds proceeds.","$3,000 isn't the proportional share.","Detachable warrants DO get an allocation, unlike plain convertibles."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Under current U.S. GAAP, a plain convertible bond (no detachable warrants) is recorded at issuance as:","choices":["Entirely debt, with no amount split to equity","Split between debt and equity","Entirely equity","A contingent liability"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: current U.S. GAAP records a plain convertible bond wholly as debt — no equity split at issuance.","The split method applies to detachable warrants, not plain convertibles under current rules.","It's a liability until converted, not equity.","It's a real liability, not contingent."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Under current GAAP, bond issue costs (underwriting, legal fees) are:","choices":["A reduction of the bond's carrying value, amortized over its life","Recorded as a separate intangible asset","Expensed immediately in full","Added to the bond's face value"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: issue costs reduce carrying value (like an added discount) and are amortized to interest expense over the bond's life.","The old separate-asset treatment is no longer current GAAP.","They're spread over the life, not expensed at once.","They reduce, not increase, the net carrying amount."]},{"d":"medium","q":"$500,000 of bonds is issued at par with $15,000 of issue costs (5-year life, straight-line). Annual issue-cost amortization is:","choices":["$3,000","$15,000","$1,500","$7,500"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: $15,000 ÷ 5 years = $3,000 added to interest expense each year.","$15,000 is the total, not the annual amount.","$1,500 halves it incorrectly.","$7,500 would be over 2 years."]},{"d":"hard","q":"In a TDR settlement, debt with a $200,000 carrying value is settled by transferring land (book $120,000, fair value $150,000). The total gains recognized are:","choices":["$50,000 on debt settlement plus $30,000 on the asset","$80,000 as a single gain","$50,000 only","$30,000 only"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: gain on settlement = $200,000 − $150,000 fair value = $50,000; separately, the land is revalued to fair value for a $30,000 gain ($150,000 − $120,000 book).","The two gains are recognized separately, not merged.","$50,000 omits the asset gain.","$30,000 omits the settlement gain."]},{"d":"hard","q":"In a TDR by modification, if total future cash flows under the new terms are LESS than the loan's carrying value, the debtor:","choices":["Recognizes a gain equal to the difference","Recognizes no gain and computes a new effective rate","Recognizes a loss","Writes the debt to zero"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: when future flows fall below carrying value, the debtor recognizes a gain for that difference and records the debt at the new (lower) total.","That's the rule for when flows still equal or exceed carrying value, not below.","It's a gain to the debtor, not a loss.","The debt is reduced to the new total flows, not zero."]}],"worked":{"title":"Issuing a bond at a discount and amortizing it for one year","setup":"On January 1, a company issues a $100,000 face value bond. It promises to pay 6% interest once a year, and the bond matures in 5 years. On the day it's issued, investors in the market are demanding an 8% return for bonds of this risk. We need to find the price the bond sells for and record the first year of interest.","steps":[{"do":"Identify the two interest rates and what each one does. Stated rate = 6% (printed on the bond). Market rate = 8% (what investors currently demand).","why":"The stated rate ONLY tells you the cash coupon: face × stated. It never changes. The market rate is what investors actually require, so it's the rate used to discount (find present value). Mixing these up is the #1 beginner mistake."},{"do":"Decide premium vs. discount. Stated (6%) < market (8%), so the bond sells at a DISCOUNT — below its $100,000 face.","why":"The bond only pays 6% cash but investors want 8%. Nobody pays full price for an under-paying bond, so the price drops below face. That price cut is what bumps the investor's real return up from 6% to 8%."},{"do":"Compute the annual cash interest: $100,000 × 6% = $6,000 per year.","why":"This is the fixed coupon the company is contractually locked into paying every year, no matter what the market does. It's based on FACE and STATED rate."},{"do":"Compute the issue price = present value of everything the bond pays, discounted at 8%. PV of the $100,000 face received in 5 years + PV of the five $6,000 coupons. Price ≈ $100,000 × 0.68058 + $6,000 × 3.99271 ≈ $68,058 + $23,956 = $92,015.","why":"An investor will pay today exactly what the future cash flows are worth today at their required 8% return. The 0.68058 and 3.99271 are present-value factors for 8% over 5 years (from PV tables). The bond is worth $92,015 now — the $7,985 gap below face is the 'discount.'"},{"do":"Record the issuance. Dr Cash $92,015; Dr Discount on Bonds Payable $7,985; Cr Bonds Payable $100,000.","why":"Cash is what you actually received. Bonds Payable is ALWAYS recorded at full face ($100,000) — that's what you must repay at maturity. The $7,985 'Discount' is a contra-liability that plugs the gap; it sits against Bonds Payable so the net 'carrying value' starts at $92,015."},{"do":"Compute Year 1 interest EXPENSE using the effective-interest method: carrying value × market rate = $92,015 × 8% = $7,361.","why":"Your true economic cost of borrowing is 8% applied to what you actually owe right now ($92,015), not the 6% printed coupon. This is why expense ($7,361) is higher than the cash you pay ($6,000) — you're slowly paying for having borrowed at a discount."},{"do":"Find the discount amortized: interest expense − cash paid = $7,361 − $6,000 = $1,361.","why":"You expensed $7,361 but only paid out $6,000 in cash. The $1,361 difference isn't cash — it's the portion of the discount being 'used up' this year. It gets added to the bond's carrying value."},{"do":"Record Year 1 interest. Dr Interest Expense $7,361; Cr Cash $6,000; Cr Discount on Bonds Payable $1,361.","why":"You recognize the full economic cost ($7,361), pay the contractual cash ($6,000), and shrink the discount account by $1,361. Crediting (reducing) the discount raises the net carrying value."},{"do":"Update the carrying value: $92,015 + $1,361 = $93,376.","why":"The bond is now carried at $93,376. Each year it climbs a little closer to $100,000, and by maturity the discount is fully amortized — carrying value will equal face exactly, which is what you repay."}],"takeaway":"A discount bond sells below face because its coupon is below market. Bonds Payable always holds face; the discount bridges face to the real price and melts away over time. Interest expense (carrying value × market rate) exceeds the cash coupon, and the difference amortizes the discount, pushing carrying value up toward face."},"worked2":{"title":"Issuing a bond at a PREMIUM (the mirror image of a discount)","setup":"A company issues a $100,000 face bond paying 9% annual interest, maturing in 4 years, when the market rate is only 7%. We'll see how a premium behaves opposite to a discount.","steps":[{"do":"Compare rates: stated 9% > market 7% → PREMIUM (price above face).","why":"The bond pays MORE cash interest than the market demands, so investors compete and bid the price up above $100,000. They're paying extra now to get the fat coupons later."},{"do":"Cash coupon = 100,000 × 9% = $9,000/yr.","why":"Fixed by face and stated rate, exactly as with a discount bond — the coupon doesn't care about the market rate."},{"do":"Issue price = 100,000 × PV-single(7%,4) + 9,000 × PV-annuity(7%,4) = 100,000 × 0.76290 + 9,000 × 3.38721 ≈ 76,290 + 30,485 = $106,774.","why":"Discount at the 7% market rate. The price ($106,774) exceeds face by $6,774 — that's the premium investors paid for the above-market coupon."},{"do":"Record issuance. Dr Cash 106,774; Cr Bonds Payable 100,000; Cr Premium on Bonds Payable 6,774.","why":"Bonds Payable still holds face ($100,000). The Premium is an ADD-ON to the liability (the opposite of a discount), so carrying value starts ABOVE face at $106,774."},{"do":"Year-1 interest expense = carrying value × market = 106,774 × 7% = $7,474.","why":"Here's the twist: expense ($7,474) is LESS than the cash paid ($9,000). With a premium, your true cost is below the coupon because you collected extra cash up front."},{"do":"Premium amortized = cash − expense = 9,000 − 7,474 = $1,526.","why":"You paid $9,000 cash but only $7,474 was true interest cost. The extra $1,526 is you 'using up' the premium you collected — it reduces the liability."},{"do":"Record interest. Dr Interest Expense 7,474; Dr Premium on Bonds Payable 1,526; Cr Cash 9,000. New carrying value = 106,774 − 1,526 = $105,249.","why":"Debiting (reducing) the Premium lowers the carrying value toward face. A premium bond's carrying value DECLINES to $100,000 by maturity — exactly opposite a discount bond, which rises to face."}],"takeaway":"A premium is the discount's mirror image. Price starts above face; the premium is added to the liability; interest expense is BELOW the cash coupon; and amortizing the premium pulls carrying value DOWN to face by maturity. Same machinery, opposite direction."},"practice":[{"prompt":"A company issues a $200,000 face bond with a 5% annual stated rate, maturing in 4 years, when the market rate is 6%. Interest is paid annually. (a) Is it a premium or discount? (b) Find the issue price. (c) Compute year-1 interest expense, cash paid, and discount amortized, and the new carrying value.","solution":["(a) Stated 5% < market 6% → DISCOUNT (the coupon is below what the market demands, so price falls below face).","(b) Cash coupon = 200,000 × 5% = $10,000/yr. Issue price = PV of face + PV of coupons at 6%: 200,000 × 0.79209 + 10,000 × 3.46511 ≈ 158,419 + 34,651 = $193,070.","(c) Year-1 interest expense = carrying value × market = 193,070 × 6% = $11,584. Cash paid = $10,000. Discount amortized = 11,584 − 10,000 = $1,584. New carrying value = 193,070 + 1,584 = $194,654.","Entry: Dr Interest Expense 11,584 / Cr Cash 10,000 / Cr Discount on Bonds Payable 1,584."]},{"prompt":"A company issues $500,000 of bonds with an 8% stated rate, paying interest SEMIANNUALLY, maturing in 3 years, when the market rate is 6%. (a) Premium or discount? (b) Find the issue price. (c) Compute the first period's interest expense and premium amortization.","solution":["Convert to per-period: market rate r = 6%/2 = 3%; periods n = 3 × 2 = 6; coupon = 500,000 × 8% × 6/12 = $20,000 per period.","(a) Stated 8% > market 6% → PREMIUM.","(b) Issue price = 500,000 × PV-single(3%,6) + 20,000 × PV-annuity(3%,6) = 500,000 × 0.83748 + 20,000 × 5.41719 ≈ 418,740 + 108,344 = $527,086.","(c) Period-1 interest expense = 527,086 × 3% = $15,813. Cash = $20,000. Premium amortized = 20,000 − 15,813 = $4,187 (premium amortization REDUCES carrying value toward face)."]},{"prompt":"A bond with a carrying value of $96,500 (face $100,000) is retired early at a call price of 101 (101% of face). Compute the gain or loss on extinguishment and state where it's reported.","solution":["Reacquisition price = 101% × 100,000 = $101,000.","Gain/loss = carrying value − reacquisition price = 96,500 − 101,000 = −$4,500 → a $4,500 LOSS.","The loss is reported in income (income from continuing operations). Entry: Dr Bonds Payable 100,000 / Dr Loss on Extinguishment 4,500 / Cr Discount on Bonds Payable 3,500 / Cr Cash 101,000."]}],"entries":{"w1":[{"caption":"At issuance (Jan 1) — record the bond sold at a discount","lines":[{"account":"Cash","dr":92015},{"account":"Discount on Bonds Payable","dr":7985},{"account":"Bonds Payable","cr":100000}]},{"caption":"End of Year 1 — record interest expense (effective-interest method)","lines":[{"account":"Interest Expense","dr":7361},{"account":"Cash","cr":6000},{"account":"Discount on Bonds Payable","cr":1361}]}],"w2":[{"caption":"At issuance (Jan 1) — record the bond sold at a premium","lines":[{"account":"Cash","dr":106774},{"account":"Bonds Payable","cr":100000},{"account":"Premium on Bonds Payable","cr":6774}]},{"caption":"End of Year 1 — record interest expense (effective-interest method)","lines":[{"account":"Interest Expense","dr":7474},{"account":"Premium on Bonds Payable","dr":1526},{"account":"Cash","cr":9000}]}],"w3":[{"caption":"Retire the premium bond early (carrying value $103,000, paid $101,000)","lines":[{"account":"Bonds Payable","dr":100000},{"account":"Premium on Bonds Payable","dr":3000},{"account":"Cash","cr":101000},{"account":"Gain on Extinguishment of Debt","cr":2000}]}]},"comptable":null,"aids":{"mnemonics":[{"tag":"DISCOUNT vs PREMIUM","text":"\"Stated LOW, price LOW\" → stated rate below market = DISCOUNT (price below face). \"Stated HIGH, price HIGH\" → premium. The price always moves the SAME direction as how the coupon compares to the market."},{"tag":"Which rate does what","text":"\"Stated Sizes, Market Measures.\" The STATED rate sizes the cash coupon (face × stated). The MARKET rate measures present value (does the discounting)."},{"tag":"Carrying value drift","text":"\"Discount climbs, Premium dips.\" A discount bond's carrying value rises toward face; a premium bond's falls toward face. Both arrive at face by maturity."}],"watch":["Bonds Payable is ALWAYS recorded at face — never net it with the discount/premium in the bond account itself.","Interest EXPENSE uses the market rate × carrying value; cash PAID uses the stated rate × face. Mixing these is the #1 error.","On a discount bond, expense RISES each year (carrying value grows); on a premium bond, expense FALLS each year.","Issue costs reduce the carrying value (like an extra discount) — they are NOT a separate asset anymore."],"cheat":"Think of a bond as a loan where the lender's real yield must equal the market rate. The price is whatever forces that to be true. Once you see price = 'whatever makes the yield = market,' premium/discount stops being memorization — a fat coupon must cost more (premium), a skimpy coupon must cost less (discount)."},"plain":{"plain":"A bond is just an IOU a company sells to borrow money. It promises to pay a fixed bit of interest each year and pay the full amount back at the end. Here's the only tricky part: the interest rate printed on the bond is fixed, but the rate investors actually want changes. So the PRICE adjusts to make up the difference. If the bond pays more than people currently demand, they'll pay extra for it (a premium). If it pays less, they'll only buy it for a discount. Over the bond's life, that premium or discount slowly melts away until, at the end, the books show exactly what you owe back.","terms":[{"term":"Face value","plain":"the amount printed on the bond — what you must pay back at the end"},{"term":"Stated (coupon) rate","plain":"the fixed interest rate written on the bond; it sets the cash payment"},{"term":"Market (effective) rate","plain":"the interest rate investors actually want right now"},{"term":"Carrying value","plain":"what the bond is worth on your books at this moment"},{"term":"Discount / Premium","plain":"the gap between what investors paid and the face value — below face is a discount, above is a premium"},{"term":"Amortization","plain":"slowly closing that gap a little each year until it's gone"}]},"lecture":[{"beat":"Let's start with what a bond actually is","text":"Imagine your company needs to borrow a million dollars. One option is a bank loan. Another is to break that borrowing into thousands of identical IOUs and sell them to investors — those IOUs are bonds. Each one says: 'I'll pay you a fixed amount of interest every period, and return the full face amount on a set maturity date.' The key word is fixed — once a bond is printed, those promised cash payments never change."},{"beat":"Now here's the tension that drives everything","text":"The interest rate printed on the bond — the stated rate — is locked in. But the rate investors demand in the open market moves around every day based on risk and the economy. So what happens when a bond promises 6% but investors now want 8%? Nobody will pay full price for a bond that under-pays. The only thing that can flex is the price. Investors simply pay less than face value, and that lower price is what bumps their actual return up to the 8% they wanted."},{"beat":"That gives us premium, discount, and par","text":"So the rule falls out naturally. If the bond's stated rate is below what the market wants, it sells below face — a discount. If the stated rate is above what the market wants, investors compete for that fat coupon and bid the price above face — a premium. And if the two rates happen to match, it sells right at face — par. You never have to memorize this; just ask 'is the coupon generous or stingy compared to what investors want today?'"},{"beat":"Pricing it is just present value","text":"How much exactly will investors pay? Whatever the future cash flows are worth today, discounted at the rate they actually demand — the market rate. The bond pays two streams: the face amount at the end, and the string of interest coupons along the way. Present-value the face, present-value the coupons, add them up. That sum is the issue price. Notice the stated rate only sizes the coupons; the market rate does all the discounting. Mixing those two up is the single most common mistake."},{"beat":"The effective-interest method, and why it makes sense","text":"Here's the elegant part. Each period, your real interest expense isn't the cash coupon — it's the market rate applied to what you actually owe right now, the carrying value. The cash you pay is fixed (face times stated rate), but the true economic cost floats on the carrying value. The gap between the two slowly closes the discount or premium. For a discount bond, expense runs higher than the cash, and the difference pushes the carrying value up toward face. For a premium, expense runs lower than the cash, pulling carrying value down toward face. Either way, by maturity the books show exactly the face amount you must repay — it all reconciles perfectly."}],"worked3":{"title":"Worked example — retiring a bond early","setup":"A company has bonds with a carrying value of $103,000 (a premium bond). It calls them early at 101 — that is, for 101% of the $100,000 face, or $101,000. Record the retirement.","steps":[{"do":"Find the reacquisition price.","why":"Called at 101 means 101% × $100,000 face = $101,000 cash paid."},{"do":"Compare to carrying value: $103,000 carrying − $101,000 paid = $2,000.","why":"You retired a debt worth $103,000 on your books by paying only $101,000 — you came out $2,000 ahead."},{"do":"Because you paid LESS than carrying value, recognize a $2,000 gain.","why":"Settling a liability for less than its book value is a gain on extinguishment, recognized immediately in income."},{"do":"Remove the bonds and the related premium from the books, record the cash, and book the gain.","why":"The entire carrying value (face plus unamortized premium) comes off; the difference is the gain."}],"takeaway":"Gain or loss on early retirement = carrying value − price paid. Pay less than carrying value → gain; pay more → loss. The carrying value already includes any unamortized premium or discount."},"advanced":{"intro":"You've mastered the core. This advanced tier covers the harder edge cases: semiannual effective-interest schedules, the fair value option, and deeper extinguishment scenarios.","lesson":[{"h":"Semiannual compounding done precisely","p":["Most real bonds pay interest semiannually, which means you must convert BOTH the rates and the periods. A 10% stated / 12% market, 5-year bond paying semiannually becomes: stated 5% per period, market 6% per period, 10 periods. Never discount an annual rate over annual periods when the bond pays semiannually.","Example: $100,000 face, 5% coupon per period, discounted at 6% over 10 periods → price ≈ $92,640. The effective-interest schedule then runs period-by-period at 6% on the carrying value, not 12% on an annual basis.","The classic error is mixing an annual market rate with semiannual coupons. Halve the rates, double the periods — every time."]},{"h":"The fair value option for liabilities","p":["GAAP lets a company elect to report certain financial liabilities at fair value instead of amortized cost. When elected, the bond is remeasured to fair value each period, and most of the change flows through net income.","The subtle twist: the portion of the fair value change caused by the company's OWN credit risk is reported in OCI, not net income. This prevents the counterintuitive result where a company's deteriorating credit would otherwise book a 'gain' in earnings as its debt's value falls."]}],"bank":[{"d":"hard","q":"A $100,000 bond pays a 10% annual coupon SEMIANNUALLY; the market rate is 12% annual; term 5 years. The correct inputs for pricing are:","choices":["5% per period, 6% discount, 10 periods","10% coupon, 12% discount, 5 periods","5% per period, 12% discount, 5 periods","10% coupon, 6% discount, 10 periods"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: halve both rates (5% coupon, 6% market) and double the periods (10) for semiannual compounding.","That ignores semiannual compounding entirely.","Mixes a per-period coupon with an annual discount rate.","Mixes an annual coupon with a per-period discount rate."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Under the fair value option for a bond liability, the portion of the fair value change due to the company's OWN credit risk is reported in:","choices":["Net income","Other comprehensive income (OCI)","Retained earnings directly","Not reported"],"answer":1,"why":["Most of the change goes to net income, but not the own-credit portion.","Correct: the own-credit-risk portion is isolated in OCI, to avoid booking earnings 'gains' when the company's credit worsens.","It routes through OCI, not directly to retained earnings.","It is reported, in OCI."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A company elects the fair value option on its debt. Its credit deteriorates, lowering the debt's fair value. The effect on NET income from this own-credit change is:","choices":["A gain in net income","Nothing — it goes to OCI","A loss in net income","It increases the liability"],"answer":1,"why":["Booking a gain as your own credit worsens is exactly the distortion the rule prevents.","Correct: the own-credit portion of the change is reported in OCI, keeping it out of net income.","It's neither a gain nor loss in net income — it's in OCI.","A falling fair value decreases the liability."]}]}},{"id":"leases","name":"Leases (ASC 842)","icon":"🏢","group":"Liabilities","overview":"Why ASC 842 dragged nearly every lease onto the balance sheet, the five classification tests, and how finance vs. operating leases hit the income statement differently.","lesson":[{"h":"The problem ASC 842 solved","p":["Under old rules, 'operating leases' were off-balance-sheet: companies controlled billions in assets (aircraft, stores, equipment) with matching obligations that never appeared on the balance sheet. Analysts had to guess at the hidden debt.","ASC 842's core principle: if you have the right to use an asset for a period in exchange for payment, you control an asset and owe a liability — so recognize both. Lessees now record a Right-of-Use (ROU) asset and a lease liability for essentially all leases over 12 months."]},{"h":"The five classification tests (any ONE triggers finance)","p":["A lease is a FINANCE lease (economically a purchase financed over time) if ANY of these are met: (1) ownership transfers at the end; (2) there is a purchase option the lessee is reasonably certain to exercise; (3) the lease term is a MAJOR PART of the asset's remaining economic life (~75%+); (4) the present value of payments is SUBSTANTIALLY ALL of the asset's fair value (~90%+); (5) the asset is so specialized only this lessee can use it.","If NONE are met, it is an OPERATING lease. Note: under ASC 842 the lessee still capitalizes an ROU asset and liability even for operating leases — the classification only changes the income-statement pattern, not whether it's on the balance sheet."]},{"h":"Two different income-statement stories","p":["FINANCE lease: treated like a financed purchase. You record (a) interest expense on the lease liability (front-loaded, since the liability is largest early) and (b) straight-line amortization of the ROU asset. Total expense is HIGHER in early years, declining over time.","OPERATING lease: a single, straight-line 'lease expense' each period — even though the underlying liability still uses interest accretion internally, the asset amortization is 'plugged' so the total nets to a flat line.","This is why classification matters: same cash payments, very different early-year expense and the split between operating vs. financing on the cash-flow statement."]},{"h":"Measuring the numbers","p":["Lease liability = present value of remaining lease payments, discounted at the rate implicit in the lease (or, if not determinable, the lessee's incremental borrowing rate).","ROU asset = lease liability + initial direct costs + prepaid lease payments − lease incentives received.","Each period for a finance lease: interest = beginning liability × rate; principal reduction = payment − interest; the liability amortizes exactly like a loan."]},{"h":"Residual values and purchase options","p":["Many leases include a residual value — the asset's expected worth at lease end. If the lessee guarantees that residual, the present value of the guaranteed amount is added to the lease liability. Example: $20,000 annual payments for 5 years at 8% gives a $79,854 payment PV; a $10,000 guaranteed residual adds its PV ($6,806), for an $86,660 liability.","A bargain purchase option (a price so low the lessee is almost certain to buy) is treated the same way — its present value is included, and it also signals a finance lease."]},{"h":"Payments at the beginning vs end of the period","p":["Whether payments are due at the start of each period (annuity due) or the end (ordinary annuity) changes the present value. Beginning-of-period payments are discounted one less period, so they produce a larger liability. The same $20,000/5-year/8% lease is $79,854 if paid in arrears but $86,243 if paid in advance.","Read the problem carefully — 'payments begin immediately' or 'at the inception' signals an annuity due."]},{"h":"The lessor's side: sales-type and direct-financing leases","p":["So far we've taken the lessee's view. The lessor (the owner renting out the asset) classifies the lease too. In a sales-type lease, the lessor recognizes a gain or loss at inception — gross profit equal to the asset's selling price (its fair value) minus its cost. Example: fair value $79,854, cost $65,000 → $14,854 gross profit recognized up front, plus interest income over the term.","A direct-financing lease recognizes only interest income over time, no upfront profit. The distinction mirrors the lessee's finance-vs-operating split."]}],"keyFormulas":["Lease liability = PV of remaining lease payments (implicit or incremental borrowing rate)","ROU asset = liability + initial direct costs + prepayments − incentives","Finance: interest = beginning liability × rate; total expense front-loaded","Operating: single straight-line lease expense"],"bank":[{"d":"easy","q":"Under ASC 842, a lessee with an operating lease (over 12 months) records on the balance sheet:","choices":["Nothing","Only a footnote","An ROU asset and a lease liability","Only the asset"],"answer":2,"why":["The off-balance-sheet treatment was eliminated.","Footnote-only was the old rule.","Correct: ASC 842 puts BOTH the ROU asset and lease liability on the balance sheet for operating leases too.","Both the asset and the liability are recorded, not just the asset."]},{"d":"easy","q":"How many of the five classification criteria must be met for a finance lease?","choices":["All five","At least three","Any one","Exactly two"],"answer":2,"why":["All five is far too strict.","Three is not the threshold.","Correct: meeting ANY single criterion makes it a finance lease.","Two is not the rule; one is enough."]},{"d":"easy","q":"The lease liability at commencement is measured at:","choices":["Total undiscounted payments","Present value of lease payments","Fair value of the asset","The first payment only"],"answer":1,"why":["Undiscounted overstates the obligation.","Correct: PV of the remaining lease payments at the appropriate discount rate.","Fair value is used in a classification test, not as the liability measure.","The liability reflects all payments, not just the first."]},{"d":"medium","q":"A lease term is 7 years; the asset's remaining economic life is 9 years. Ownership does not transfer and there is no purchase option. Which classification test should you check first, and what is the result?","choices":["90% PV test; likely operating","75% economic-life test; 7/9 = 78% → finance","Specialized-asset test; operating","Ownership test; finance"],"answer":1,"why":["The PV test is separate; the life ratio is the direct one to check here.","Correct: 7/9 = 78% exceeds ~75%, so the economic-life criterion is met → finance lease.","Nothing indicates specialization.","Ownership does not transfer, so that test fails."]},{"d":"medium","q":"For a finance lease, the pattern of TOTAL expense (interest + amortization) over the lease term is:","choices":["Flat (straight-line)","Higher in early years, declining","Lower early, rising","Zero until the end"],"answer":1,"why":["Flat describes an operating lease.","Correct: interest is largest when the liability is largest (early), and amortization is straight-line, so the sum is front-loaded.","It declines, not rises.","Expense is recognized throughout, not deferred."]},{"d":"medium","q":"The ROU asset is initially measured as the lease liability PLUS:","choices":["Expected residual value","Initial direct costs and prepayments, minus incentives","Total interest over the term","The asset's fair value"],"answer":1,"why":["Residual value affects the liability calculation, not this add-on.","Correct: ROU asset = liability + initial direct costs + prepaid payments − incentives received.","Interest is recognized over time, not capitalized into the asset.","Fair value is not the measurement basis for the ROU asset."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A finance lease has an $80,000 liability at 8%, with a $20,037 annual payment (ordinary annuity). After the first payment, the liability balance is:","choices":["$66,363","$59,963","$60,000","$73,600"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: interest = 80,000 × 8% = $6,400; principal = 20,037 − 6,400 = $13,637; new balance = 80,000 − 13,637 = $66,363.","$59,963 subtracts the full payment as if it were all principal.","$60,000 ignores the interest portion of the payment.","$73,600 subtracts only the interest, not the principal."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Under ASC 842, on the statement of cash flows, principal payments on a FINANCE lease are classified as:","choices":["Operating outflow","Investing outflow","Financing outflow","Not reported"],"answer":2,"why":["Only the interest portion of a finance lease is operating.","Investing relates to asset purchases, not lease principal.","Correct: finance-lease principal repayments are financing outflows; the interest portion is operating.","They are reported, in financing."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A lessee leases highly specialized equipment that only it can use; the term is short (30% of life) and there is no purchase option. The lease is:","choices":["Operating, because the term is short","Finance, because of the specialized-asset criterion","Operating, because PV is low","Indeterminate"],"answer":1,"why":["A short term does not override a met criterion.","Correct: criterion (5), a specialized asset with no alternative use to the lessor, is met → finance lease regardless of term.","Even low PV does not matter once any single criterion is met.","It is determinate: criterion 5 is satisfied."]},{"d":"easy","q":"Under ASC 842, the Right-of-Use asset represents:","choices":["The lessee's right to use the leased asset over the term","Ownership of the underlying asset","A cash deposit","The lessor's profit"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: the ROU asset is the lessee's right to use the asset for the lease term — not legal ownership.","The lessee does not own the asset (unless title transfers).","It is not a deposit.","It reflects the lessee's right, not the lessor's profit."]},{"d":"medium","q":"For a FINANCE lease, the interest portion of each payment over time:","choices":["Increases","Decreases","Stays constant","Equals the whole payment"],"answer":1,"why":["Interest falls as the balance is paid down, it does not rise.","Correct: interest = liability × rate, and the liability shrinks each period, so the interest portion decreases (and principal grows).","Only straight-line patterns are constant; this is not one.","Interest is only part of the payment, declining over time."]},{"d":"medium","q":"On the cash flow statement, the INTEREST portion of a finance lease payment is classified as:","choices":["Investing","Financing","Operating","Not reported"],"answer":2,"why":["Investing covers asset purchases, not lease interest.","The principal portion is financing, but the question asks about interest.","Correct: the interest portion of a finance lease is an operating cash outflow under U.S. GAAP (the principal is financing).","It is reported, in operating."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A company prefers operating-lease treatment to keep reported expenses smooth early on. Which single factor would push a borderline lease into FINANCE classification?","choices":["A lease term of 60% of the asset's life","A PV of payments equal to 95% of fair value","A 2-year term on a 10-year asset","Payments made at year-end"],"answer":1,"why":["60% is below the ~75% economic-life threshold.","Correct: PV of payments at 95% exceeds the ~90% 'substantially all of fair value' test, forcing finance classification.","A short 2-year term on a long-lived asset points toward operating.","Payment timing (ordinary vs. due) affects PV slightly but is not a classification test."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A 5-year lease has $20,000 annual payments (8%, ordinary) plus a $10,000 guaranteed residual value. The lease liability is approximately:","choices":["$86,660","$79,854","$90,000","$73,048"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: PV of payments ($79,854) + PV of the guaranteed residual ($6,806) = $86,660.","$79,854 omits the residual.","$90,000 just adds the undiscounted residual.","$73,048 subtracts instead of adds."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Lease payments due at the BEGINNING of each period (annuity due) versus the end produce a lease liability that is:","choices":["Larger","Smaller","Identical","Zero"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: beginning-of-period payments are discounted one fewer period, so their present value — and the liability — is larger.","It's larger, not smaller.","The timing changes the PV, so not identical.","There is still a liability."]},{"d":"hard","q":"In a sales-type lease, the lessor's gross profit at inception equals:","choices":["The asset's fair value (selling price) minus its cost","Total lease payments minus cost","Interest income only","Zero"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: sales-type leases recognize upfront gross profit = fair value − cost, plus interest income over the term.","That overstates profit by including interest.","A direct-financing lease recognizes only interest; sales-type also recognizes upfront profit.","Sales-type leases do recognize upfront profit."]}],"worked":{"title":"Classifying a lease and recording the first year (finance lease)","setup":"A company leases a machine on January 1. It will pay $20,000 at the END of each year for 5 years. The machine's total useful life is 6 years. The interest rate built into the lease is 8%. Ownership does not transfer and there's no bargain purchase option. We need to classify the lease and record year 1.","steps":[{"do":"Run the 5 classification tests. (1) ownership transfer? No. (2) bargain purchase option? No. (3) lease term a major part of life? 5 of 6 years = 83%, which is ≥ ~75% — YES, this one is met.","why":"Under ASC 842, meeting ANY ONE of the five tests makes it a FINANCE lease (economically you're buying the asset on installments). Test 3 is met at 83%, so we can stop — it's a finance lease. We don't need the other tests."},{"do":"Compute the lease liability = present value of the 5 payments at 8%. $20,000 × PV-annuity factor(8%, 5 years) = $20,000 × 3.99271 = $79,854.","why":"You're promising five future $20,000 payments. Their worth TODAY (discounted at the 8% lease rate) is the liability you owe right now. The factor 3.99271 comes from a present-value-of-annuity table for 8%/5 years."},{"do":"Record the lease at commencement. Dr Right-of-Use (ROU) Asset $79,854; Cr Lease Liability $79,854.","why":"ASC 842's core idea: you control an asset (the right to use the machine for 5 years) and you owe a liability (the payments). Both go on the balance sheet at the same present value. The ROU asset is your right to use; the liability is your obligation to pay."},{"do":"Split the first $20,000 payment into interest and principal. Interest = beginning liability × rate = $79,854 × 8% = $6,388. Principal = $20,000 − $6,388 = $13,612.","why":"A lease payment works like a loan payment: part covers the interest accruing on what you owe, and the rest pays down the balance. Early on, the balance is big, so most of the payment is interest."},{"do":"Record the payment. Dr Interest Expense $6,388; Dr Lease Liability $13,612; Cr Cash $20,000.","why":"You recognize the interest cost of financing ($6,388), reduce the liability by the principal portion ($13,612), and pay $20,000 cash. The liability is now $79,854 − $13,612 = $66,242."},{"do":"Separately, amortize the ROU asset. Straight-line over the 5-year lease term: $79,854 ÷ 5 = $15,971. Dr Amortization Expense $15,971; Cr ROU Asset (or Accumulated Amortization) $15,971.","why":"In a FINANCE lease you record TWO separate expenses: interest on the liability AND amortization of the asset (like depreciation). This is the key difference from an operating lease, which would show just ONE straight-line lease expense instead."},{"do":"Add up year-1 expense: interest $6,388 + amortization $15,971 = $22,359.","why":"Notice total expense ($22,359) is higher than the $20,000 cash paid, and it's front-loaded — interest is biggest early when the liability is largest. Over the lease, total expense declines. That front-loading is the signature of a finance lease."}],"takeaway":"If any one of the five tests is met, it's a finance lease. You record an ROU asset and lease liability at the present value of payments. Each payment splits into interest (liability × rate) and principal. You also amortize the asset separately — so a finance lease produces two expenses and a front-loaded total, unlike an operating lease's single flat expense."},"worked2":{"title":"The SAME lease as an OPERATING lease (one flat expense)","setup":"Suppose a 5-year lease with $12,000 annual payments at 6% meets NONE of the five finance-lease tests (short term relative to life, no transfer, no bargain option, PV well under 90%). It's an operating lease. We'll see how differently it hits the income statement.","steps":[{"do":"Confirm classification: none of the five tests met → OPERATING lease.","why":"Even though it's operating, ASC 842 still puts it on the balance sheet — the difference is purely in how expense is recognized over time."},{"do":"Record the ROU asset and liability at PV of payments = 12,000 × PV-annuity(6%,5) = 12,000 × 4.21236 = $50,548.","why":"Just like a finance lease, you capitalize the right-to-use asset and the obligation at present value. The balance sheet treatment is the same; only the income statement differs."},{"do":"Recognize a SINGLE straight-line lease expense each year = total payments ÷ term = (12,000 × 5) ÷ 5 = $12,000/yr.","why":"This is the defining feature of an operating lease: one flat 'lease expense' equal to the average annual payment — no separate interest and amortization lines. The income statement shows a smooth $12,000 every year."},{"do":"Behind the scenes, that $12,000 is split into interest on the liability (declining) and a 'plug' to the ROU asset (rising) so the two always sum to $12,000.","why":"The bookkeeping still accrues interest internally, but the asset amortization is deliberately set so the TOTAL stays flat. You don't report the split — you report the single number."},{"do":"Compare to the finance version: a finance lease would show front-loaded expense (high early, low late); this operating lease shows a flat $12,000 line every year.","why":"Same cash payments, same balance-sheet capitalization — but the expense PATTERN differs. That's the entire practical consequence of lease classification under ASC 842."}],"takeaway":"An operating lease is still capitalized (ROU asset + liability), but it produces ONE straight-line lease expense rather than separate interest and amortization. The total expense is flat across the term, versus a finance lease's front-loaded pattern."},"practice":[{"prompt":"A company signs a finance lease: $15,000 paid at the END of each year for 6 years, interest rate 7%. (a) Compute the lease liability at commencement. (b) Split the first payment into interest and principal and give the liability balance after year 1. (c) Compute the annual straight-line ROU amortization.","solution":["(a) Liability = PV of payments = 15,000 × PV-annuity(7%, 6) = 15,000 × 4.76654 = $71,498.","(b) Year-1 interest = 71,498 × 7% = $5,005. Principal = 15,000 − 5,005 = $9,995. Balance after year 1 = 71,498 − 9,995 = $61,503.","(c) ROU asset is recorded at $71,498 and amortized straight-line over the 6-year term: 71,498 ÷ 6 = $11,916/yr.","Year-1 entries: at commencement Dr ROU Asset 71,498 / Cr Lease Liability 71,498. Payment: Dr Interest Expense 5,005, Dr Lease Liability 9,995 / Cr Cash 15,000. Amortization: Dr Amortization Expense 11,916 / Cr ROU Asset 11,916."]},{"prompt":"A lease has a 4-year term; the leased asset's economic life is 5 years. Ownership doesn't transfer, there's no purchase option, and the PV of payments is about 88% of fair value. Classify the lease and justify which test decides it.","solution":["Check the economic-life test: term ÷ life = 4 ÷ 5 = 80%, which is ≥ ~75% → this criterion IS met.","Because ANY single criterion being met forces finance classification, this is a FINANCE lease (decided by the 75% economic-life test).","Note the PV test (88%) is just under the ~90% threshold and would NOT have triggered finance on its own — but it doesn't matter, since the life test already did."]},{"prompt":"A finance lease requires $25,000 at the BEGINNING of each year (annuity due) for 4 years at 8%. Compute the initial lease liability.","solution":["Annuity due: multiply the ordinary-annuity factor by (1 + r). Liability = 25,000 × PV-annuity(8%, 4) × 1.08 = 25,000 × 3.31213 × 1.08 = $89,427.","The 'due' adjustment matters: because payments arrive at the start of each period, they're discounted one period less, making the liability larger than an ordinary annuity ($82,803) would give."]}],"entries":{"w1":[{"caption":"At commencement — recognize the right-of-use asset and lease liability","lines":[{"account":"Right-of-Use Asset","dr":79854},{"account":"Lease Liability","cr":79854}]},{"caption":"End of Year 1 — record the lease payment (split into interest and principal)","lines":[{"account":"Interest Expense","dr":6388},{"account":"Lease Liability","dr":13612},{"account":"Cash","cr":20000}]},{"caption":"End of Year 1 — amortize the right-of-use asset (straight-line over 5 years)","lines":[{"account":"Amortization Expense","dr":15971},{"account":"Right-of-Use Asset","cr":15971}]}],"w2":[{"caption":"At commencement — operating lease also capitalizes the ROU asset and liability","lines":[{"account":"Right-of-Use Asset","dr":50548},{"account":"Lease Liability","cr":50548}]},{"caption":"End of Year 1 — single straight-line lease expense (operating lease)","lines":[{"account":"Lease Expense","dr":12000},{"account":"Lease Liability","dr":8967},{"account":"Cash","cr":12000},{"account":"Right-of-Use Asset","cr":8967}]}]},"comptable":null,"aids":{"mnemonics":[{"tag":"The 5 finance tests — \"OWNES\"","text":"Ownership transfers · Written bargain purchase option · Net present value ≥ 90% of fair value · Economic life ≥ 75% · Specialized asset. ANY one = finance lease."},{"tag":"Finance vs operating expense","text":"\"Finance = Front-loaded, Operating = One flat line.\" Finance splits into interest + amortization (high early); operating is a single straight-line expense."}],"watch":["Even an OPERATING lease goes on the balance sheet under ASC 842 (ROU asset + liability). Classification only changes the income-statement pattern, not whether it's capitalized.","Only ONE of the five tests has to be met — stop checking once you hit one.","On the cash flow statement, finance-lease PRINCIPAL is financing but the INTEREST portion is operating.","The liability is the PV of payments; the ROU asset is that liability plus initial direct costs and prepayments, minus incentives — they're not always equal."],"cheat":"Ask one question: 'Is this really a disguised purchase?' If the lessee keeps the asset for most of its life, will own it, or is paying ~its whole value, it's economically a financed purchase → finance lease. Operating leases are true rentals. The five tests are just five ways of detecting a disguised purchase."},"plain":{"plain":"A lease is renting something big — a building, a truck, a machine — for a long time. The new rule says: if you're going to control that thing for years, you can't pretend it's invisible. You have to put it on your books as both something you have the right to use (an asset) and money you still owe (a liability). The big question is whether the lease is really just a sneaky way of buying the thing on installments. If it basically is — you'll keep it most of its life, or end up owning it — it's a 'finance' lease and you treat it like a purchase. If it's a genuine rental, it's an 'operating' lease and you just record a steady rent expense.","terms":[{"term":"Lessee","plain":"the one renting (paying to use the thing)"},{"term":"Right-of-use (ROU) asset","plain":"your right to use the rented thing, treated as something you have"},{"term":"Lease liability","plain":"the money you still owe on the lease"},{"term":"Finance lease","plain":"a lease that's really a disguised purchase on installments"},{"term":"Operating lease","plain":"a true rental — you give it back at the end"}]},"lecture":[{"beat":"Why this topic exists at all","text":"For decades, companies played a trick. They'd rent buildings and aircraft and equipment on long leases, control those assets for years, owe billions in lease payments — and keep all of it off the balance sheet by calling it a 'rental.' Investors couldn't see the real debt. ASC 842 closed that loophole with a simple principle: if you control the use of an asset for a meaningful period in exchange for payments, you have an asset and a liability, so show both."},{"beat":"The one question that classifies every lease","text":"Once a lease is on the books, you still have to decide what kind it is, and the whole thing reduces to one question: is this really a disguised purchase? If you'll end up owning the asset, or keep it for most of its useful life, or pay nearly its whole value, then economically you bought it on installments — that's a finance lease. If it's a genuine rental you'll hand back, it's an operating lease. The five formal tests are just five different ways of detecting a disguised purchase."},{"beat":"Measuring what goes on the books","text":"The liability is the present value of the lease payments you've promised — same present-value logic as a bond. The right-of-use asset starts from that same liability, adjusted for things like prepayments and setup costs. So at day one, asset and liability are roughly mirror images."},{"beat":"Where the two lease types part ways","text":"The difference is entirely on the income statement. A finance lease is treated like a financed purchase: you book interest on the shrinking liability plus straight-line amortization of the asset — two separate expenses that total more in the early years and taper off. An operating lease instead shows a single, flat lease expense every year. Same cash payments, same balance-sheet footprint — but a finance lease front-loads the expense, while an operating lease keeps it level. That pattern difference is the entire practical stake of the classification."}],"advanced":{"intro":"Advanced lease territory: the lessor's net investment computation with residual values, and the treatment of initial direct costs across lease types.","lesson":[{"h":"The lessor's net investment with residual values","p":["A lessor's net investment in a lease equals the present value of the lease payments PLUS the present value of any residual value (guaranteed or unguaranteed). Both are discounted at the rate implicit in the lease.","Example: payments of $25,000/year for 5 years at 8% (PV ≈ $99,818) plus an $8,000 unguaranteed residual (PV ≈ $5,445) gives a net investment of about $105,262. Unlike the lessee — who only includes a residual if it's guaranteed — the lessor includes the residual either way, because the lessor owns it."]},{"h":"Initial direct costs by lease type","p":["Initial direct costs (commissions, legal fees to set up the lease) are handled differently depending on classification. For a lessee, they're added to the ROU asset. For a lessor's sales-type lease where fair value equals carrying amount, they're expensed at commencement; in a direct-financing lease, they're deferred and included in the net investment, reducing the effective rate.","The logic: in a sales-type lease you're essentially making a sale, so setup costs are period costs; in a direct-financing arrangement you're making a loan, so the costs are capitalized into the yield."]}],"bank":[{"d":"hard","q":"A lessor's net investment in a lease includes the PV of lease payments PLUS:","choices":["The PV of the residual value (guaranteed OR unguaranteed)","Only a guaranteed residual","Nothing beyond the payments","The asset's original cost"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: the lessor includes the PV of the residual whether guaranteed or not, because the lessor owns the residual.","That's the lessee's rule — the lessee includes only guaranteed residuals.","The residual is part of the lessor's net investment.","Net investment is a present value, not original cost."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Payments of $25,000/year for 5 years at 8% (PV ≈ $99,818) plus an $8,000 unguaranteed residual (PV ≈ $5,445). The lessor's net investment is approximately:","choices":["$105,262","$99,818","$107,818","$94,373"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: net investment = PV of payments ($99,818) + PV of residual ($5,445) ≈ $105,262.","$99,818 omits the residual.","$107,818 adds the undiscounted residual.","$94,373 subtracts the residual."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Initial direct costs in a lessor's SALES-TYPE lease (fair value = carrying amount) are:","choices":["Expensed at commencement","Capitalized into the ROU asset","Always deferred over the term","Ignored"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: in a sales-type lease (essentially a sale), initial direct costs are expensed at commencement.","The ROU asset is the lessee's account, not the lessor's.","Deferral applies to direct-financing leases, not sales-type with FV = carrying amount.","They are recognized, just expensed immediately."]}]}},{"id":"income-tax","name":"Accounting for Income Taxes","icon":"🧾","group":"Liabilities","overview":"Why book income and taxable income diverge, how temporary differences create deferred tax assets and liabilities, and what reverses versus what never does.","lesson":[{"h":"Two sets of books, one legitimate reason","p":["Companies compute income twice: BOOK income under GAAP (for investors) and TAXABLE income under the tax code (for the IRS). They differ because the two systems have different goals — GAAP measures economic performance; the tax code pursues policy and revenue timing.","The accounting question is: when book and tax differ, does the difference eventually REVERSE? If yes, it's TEMPORARY and creates a deferred tax item. If it never reverses, it's PERMANENT and creates none."]},{"h":"Permanent vs. temporary","p":["PERMANENT differences never appear on the other set of books. Tax-exempt municipal bond interest is income for books but never taxed. Fines and penalties are expenses for books but never deductible. Because they never reverse, they have NO deferred tax effect — they just make the effective tax rate differ from the statutory rate.","TEMPORARY differences are timing differences: the same item hits both books, just in different periods. Depreciation is the classic case — accelerated for tax, straight-line for books. Total depreciation over the asset's life is identical; only the timing differs. That timing difference reverses, so it creates deferred tax."]},{"h":"DTLs and DTAs — the direction logic","p":["A Deferred Tax LIABILITY arises when you pay LESS tax now but will pay MORE later. Example: accelerated tax depreciation lowers taxable income early, so you defer tax into the future — a future obligation = DTL.","A Deferred Tax ASSET arises when you pay MORE tax now but will pay LESS later. Example: warranty expense is booked now (reduces book income) but is deductible for tax only when actually paid — so you overpay tax now and get the deduction later = a future benefit = DTA. Net operating loss carryforwards are also DTAs.","Plain-language test: 'Will this difference make my future tax bill higher or lower than my future book expense suggests?' Higher future tax = DTL; lower = DTA."]},{"h":"Putting it together + valuation allowance","p":["Income tax expense (the book number) = current tax payable (on the actual return) + increase in DTL − increase in DTA. The deferred piece reconciles the book expense to the cash tax.","A DTA is only worth something if you'll have future income to use it against. If it is 'more likely than not' (>50%) that some of the DTA won't be realized, you record a VALUATION ALLOWANCE that reduces the DTA to its realizable amount.","Worked: book income $500,000 includes $20,000 muni interest (permanent) and $30,000 warranty expense not yet deductible (temporary). Tax rate 25%. Taxable = 500,000 − 20,000 + 30,000 = $510,000. Current tax = 510,000 × 25% = $127,500. The $30,000 temporary difference → DTA = 30,000 × 25% = $7,500. Entry: Dr Income Tax Expense 120,000 / Dr DTA 7,500 / Cr Income Tax Payable 127,500."]},{"h":"Net operating loss carryforwards","p":["When a company loses money, that loss can offset future taxable income, creating a deferred tax asset today. Example: a $100,000 NOL with a 25% rate creates a $25,000 DTA — the future tax savings the loss will generate.","Under current rules, NOLs generally carry forward indefinitely but can offset only a portion (commonly 80%) of taxable income in a future year. The core idea for a course exam: a loss today becomes a DTA because it will reduce taxes later."]},{"h":"Valuation allowances","p":["A deferred tax asset is only worth something if the company will have future profits to use it against. If it's more likely than not that some portion won't be realized, you record a valuation allowance — a contra-account that reduces the DTA to its realizable amount.","Example: a $25,000 DTA, but management judges only 60% is likely to be used. You record a $10,000 valuation allowance (40% × $25,000), netting the DTA down to $15,000. The allowance is re-evaluated each period as expectations change."]},{"h":"Changes in enacted tax rates","p":["Deferred tax balances are measured at the rate expected to apply when the difference reverses. When a new tax rate is enacted, you must remeasure existing DTAs and DTLs at the new rate, with the adjustment hitting income tax expense in the period of enactment.","Example: a $100,000 temporary difference produced a $30,000 DTL at the old 30% rate. A new 25% rate is enacted, so the DTL is remeasured to $25,000 — a $5,000 reduction that decreases income tax expense this period."]}],"keyFormulas":["Taxable income = book income − permanent items + temporary additions − temporary subtractions","Income tax expense = current tax payable + ΔDTL − ΔDTA","DTA or DTL = temporary difference × enacted future tax rate","Valuation allowance reduces DTA when realization is < 'more likely than not'"],"bank":[{"d":"easy","q":"Using accelerated depreciation for tax but straight-line for books creates a:","choices":["Deferred tax asset","Deferred tax liability","Permanent difference","Valuation allowance"],"answer":1,"why":["A DTA arises when you pay more now, less later — the opposite here.","Correct: more tax depreciation now lowers current taxable income but raises it later → pay more later → DTL.","Depreciation timing reverses, so it is temporary, not permanent.","A valuation allowance is unrelated to this."]},{"d":"easy","q":"Interest earned on tax-exempt municipal bonds is a:","choices":["Temporary difference creating a DTA","Temporary difference creating a DTL","Permanent difference","Current tax expense"],"answer":2,"why":["It never reverses, so no DTA.","It never reverses, so no DTL.","Correct: muni interest is never taxable — it never appears on the tax return — so it is a permanent difference with no deferred tax.","It reduces taxes but is not itself current tax expense."]},{"d":"easy","q":"A deferred tax asset represents:","choices":["Taxes you will pay more of in the future","A future tax benefit (you'll pay less later)","A permanent reduction in tax","Cash held for taxes"],"answer":1,"why":["That describes a DTL.","Correct: a DTA is a future deductible amount — a benefit that lowers future tax.","It is a timing benefit, not a permanent reduction.","It is not cash; it is a tax attribute."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Book income is $400,000, including $50,000 of warranty expense accrued but not yet paid/deductible, and $10,000 of nondeductible fines. Tax rate 30%. Taxable income is:","choices":["$360,000","$460,000","$440,000","$400,000"],"answer":1,"why":["$360,000 wrongly subtracts items that should be added back.","Correct: add back the warranty temporary difference (+50,000) and the nondeductible fine (+10,000): 400,000 + 50,000 + 10,000 = $460,000.","$440,000 omits one of the two adjustments.","$400,000 ignores both adjustments."]},{"d":"medium","q":"In the prior question, the warranty difference creates a deferred tax ___ of ___:","choices":["liability; $15,000","asset; $15,000","asset; $3,000","liability; $3,000"],"answer":1,"why":["It is an asset, not a liability (deduction comes later).","Correct: warranty deductible later = DTA = 50,000 × 30% = $15,000.","$3,000 uses the wrong base.","It is a DTA, and the amount is $15,000."]},{"d":"medium","q":"A valuation allowance against a deferred tax asset is required when:","choices":["The DTA is certain to be realized","It is more likely than not that some of the DTA will NOT be realized","The tax rate decreases","The company has taxable income"],"answer":1,"why":["Certainty of realization means no allowance.","Correct: if realization is < 50% likely (more likely than not it won't be realized), reduce the DTA via a valuation allowance.","A rate change adjusts the DTA balance but is not the allowance trigger.","Having taxable income supports realization, reducing the need for an allowance."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A company has a DTL of $40,000 measured at a 40% rate. Tax law changes the enacted future rate to 25%. The effect on income tax expense in the year of enactment is:","choices":["Increase of $15,000","Decrease of $15,000","No effect","Decrease of $40,000"],"answer":1,"why":["A reduced DTL lowers tax expense; it does not increase it.","Correct: the DTL is remeasured to 40,000 × (25/40) = $25,000; the $15,000 reduction in the liability decreases income tax expense in the enactment year.","Rate changes are recognized immediately in the period of enactment, so there is an effect.","The DTL falls by $15,000 (from 40,000 to 25,000), not by the full $40,000."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A net operating loss CARRYFORWARD is recognized as:","choices":["A deferred tax liability","A deferred tax asset (subject to valuation allowance)","A permanent difference","Current tax payable"],"answer":1,"why":["It is a future benefit, not an obligation.","Correct: an NOL carryforward reduces future taxes → a DTA, which may need a valuation allowance if future income is doubtful.","It reverses (it gets used), so it is not permanent.","It reduces future tax, it is not currently payable."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Income tax expense for the year is best described as:","choices":["The cash paid to the government","Current tax payable plus the change in deferred tax balances","Taxable income times the rate","Always equal to book income times the rate"],"answer":1,"why":["Cash paid is the current portion only.","Correct: total income tax expense = current tax payable + ΔDTL − ΔDTA, blending the current and deferred pieces.","That formula gives only the current tax payable.","Permanent differences make this untrue."]},{"d":"easy","q":"A difference between book and taxable income that will REVERSE in future periods is called a:","choices":["Permanent difference","Temporary difference","Valuation allowance","Tax credit"],"answer":1,"why":["Permanent differences never reverse.","Correct: a temporary (timing) difference reverses in future periods and creates deferred tax.","A valuation allowance reduces a DTA; it is not the difference itself.","A tax credit directly reduces tax owed, unrelated to this definition."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Rent collected in advance is taxed when received but recognized for books only when earned. This creates a:","choices":["Deferred tax liability","Deferred tax asset","Permanent difference","Tax credit"],"answer":1,"why":["A DTL arises when you pay tax LATER, not earlier.","Correct: you pay tax now on the cash received but recognize the book revenue later — you've prepaid tax, a future benefit → deferred tax ASSET.","It reverses when the rent is earned, so it is temporary, not permanent.","It is not a credit."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Income tax expense reported on the income statement differs from income tax PAYABLE because of:","choices":["Permanent differences only","The change in deferred tax balances","Interest on late payments","The dividend received deduction"],"answer":1,"why":["Permanent differences affect the rate but the gap between expense and payable is the deferred piece.","Correct: income tax expense = current tax payable + ΔDTL − ΔDTA; the deferred change is exactly what makes them differ.","Penalties are unrelated to this distinction.","That is a specific permanent item, not the general reason."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A company with large deferred tax assets has three consecutive years of losses and an uncertain outlook. The most likely accounting consequence is:","choices":["The DTAs are written off permanently","A valuation allowance is recorded against the DTAs","The DTAs are reclassified as liabilities","No effect on the financial statements"],"answer":1,"why":["DTAs are not permanently written off; they are reduced by an allowance that can later reverse.","Correct: a history of losses makes realization doubtful, so a valuation allowance reduces the DTAs to the amount more likely than not to be realized.","DTAs cannot become liabilities.","There is a significant effect — the allowance reduces net income and the net DTA."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Book income is $800,000, including $20,000 tax-exempt muni interest (permanent). Tax depreciation exceeds book by $60,000; warranty expense of $40,000 is not yet deductible. Rate 25%. Taxable income is:","choices":["$760,000","$800,000","$720,000","$880,000"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: 800,000 − 20,000 muni − 60,000 extra tax depreciation + 40,000 nondeductible-yet warranty = $760,000.","$800,000 ignores all three adjustments.","$720,000 mishandles the warranty add-back.","$880,000 adds the depreciation instead of subtracting it."]},{"d":"hard","q":"From the prior fact pattern (extra tax depreciation $60,000 → DTL; warranty $40,000 → DTA; rate 25%), the net effect on deferred taxes is:","choices":["DTL $15,000 and DTA $10,000 recorded separately","A single net DTA of $25,000","A single net DTL of $25,000","No deferred tax"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: the depreciation difference creates a $15,000 DTL (60,000 × 25%) and the warranty creates a $10,000 DTA (40,000 × 25%); they are tracked separately though often netted for presentation.","They are not combined into one DTA — they have opposite directions.","Likewise not a single DTL.","Both are temporary differences, so deferred tax exists."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Continuing: current tax payable is $190,000 (760,000 × 25%). Income tax EXPENSE equals current tax + ΔDTL − ΔDTA =","choices":["$185,000","$195,000","$190,000","$215,000"],"answer":1,"why":["$185,000 reverses the deferred adjustment.","Correct: 190,000 + 15,000 DTL − 10,000 DTA = $195,000.","$190,000 ignores the deferred items.","$215,000 adds both instead of netting."]},{"d":"medium","q":"A company has a $100,000 net operating loss and a 25% tax rate. This creates:","choices":["A $25,000 deferred tax asset","A $25,000 deferred tax liability","A $100,000 DTA","No deferred tax effect"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: the loss will reduce future taxes, so it's a DTA of $100,000 × 25% = $25,000.","It's an asset (future savings), not a liability.","The DTA is the tax effect ($25,000), not the full loss.","An NOL carryforward does create a deferred tax asset."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A $25,000 DTA exists, but only 60% is judged more likely than not to be realized. The valuation allowance is:","choices":["$10,000","$15,000","$25,000","$0"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: the allowance covers the unrealizable 40% — $25,000 × 40% = $10,000, netting the DTA to $15,000.","$15,000 is the net realizable DTA, not the allowance.","$25,000 would write off the entire asset.","Since some is doubtful, an allowance is required."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A $30,000 DTL was recorded at a 30% rate. A new 25% rate is enacted. The remeasurement:","choices":["Reduces the DTL to $25,000 and lowers tax expense by $5,000","Leaves the DTL unchanged","Increases the DTL to $36,000","Is recorded in OCI"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: remeasure at the new rate — the $100,000 difference × 25% = $25,000, a $5,000 reduction that decreases income tax expense.","Enacted rate changes require remeasurement.","A lower rate reduces, not increases, the DTL.","The adjustment hits income tax expense, not OCI."]}],"worked":{"title":"Reconciling book income to taxable income and recording deferred tax","setup":"A company reports $500,000 of book (GAAP) income. Two things differ from the tax return: it earned $20,000 of interest on municipal bonds (never taxable), and it recorded $30,000 of warranty expense that it can't deduct on the tax return until it actually pays the claims later. The tax rate is 25%. We need taxable income, the tax owed, and the journal entry.","steps":[{"do":"Start with book income: $500,000. This is the GAAP number that will appear on the income statement.","why":"Book and tax income are computed under different rulebooks. We start from the book number and make adjustments to reach the taxable number the IRS cares about."},{"do":"Handle the municipal interest. Remove $20,000: $500,000 − $20,000 = $480,000.","why":"Muni bond interest is income for books but the tax code NEVER taxes it. Because it never shows up on a tax return — not this year, not ever — it's a PERMANENT difference. We subtract it out and it creates no deferred tax."},{"do":"Handle the warranty expense. Add back $30,000: $480,000 + $30,000 = $510,000 taxable income.","why":"For books, you expensed $30,000 now (matching it to the sale). But the tax code won't let you deduct warranty costs until you actually pay them later. So for THIS year's tax return, that deduction isn't allowed yet — add it back. This is a TEMPORARY difference: you'll get the deduction in a future year when you pay the claims."},{"do":"Compute current tax payable = taxable income × rate = $510,000 × 25% = $127,500.","why":"This is the real cash tax owed to the government this year, based on the tax return's taxable income — not the book income."},{"do":"Compute the deferred tax asset (DTA) from the temporary difference = $30,000 × 25% = $7,500.","why":"You overpaid tax this year (you couldn't deduct the warranty yet), but you'll get that deduction later, lowering a future tax bill. A future tax saving is an ASSET. Its value = the temporary difference × the tax rate = $7,500."},{"do":"Find income tax EXPENSE (the book number) = current tax payable − increase in DTA = $127,500 − $7,500 = $120,000.","why":"The income statement should show tax based on book logic, not just the cash owed. You'll pay $127,500 in cash now, but $7,500 of that is really a prepayment you'll recover later through the DTA. So the true cost matched against this year's book income is $120,000."},{"do":"Record the entry. Dr Income Tax Expense $120,000; Dr Deferred Tax Asset $7,500; Cr Income Tax Payable $127,500.","why":"The debits ($120,000 + $7,500 = $127,500) equal the credit, so it balances. Income Tax Payable is the cash you owe now; the DTA sits on the balance sheet as a future benefit; Income Tax Expense is what hits the income statement."}],"takeaway":"Permanent differences (like muni interest) never reverse — adjust them out, no deferred tax. Temporary differences (like warranty timing) reverse later — they create deferred tax. A deduction you'll get LATER means you overpay now = a deferred tax ASSET. Income tax expense = current tax payable adjusted for the change in deferred balances."},"worked2":{"title":"A net operating loss (NOL) carryforward creates a deferred tax asset","setup":"A startup has a rough first year and reports a tax loss (negative taxable income) of $80,000. Tax law lets it carry that loss forward to offset future taxable income. The tax rate is 25%. We'll record the benefit.","steps":[{"do":"Recognize that a loss this year can shelter PROFITS in future years from tax.","why":"A net operating loss carryforward is valuable: when the company turns profitable, it can deduct this $80,000 against future taxable income, reducing future tax bills. A future tax saving is an asset."},{"do":"Compute the deferred tax asset = NOL × tax rate = 80,000 × 25% = $20,000.","why":"The benefit isn't the full $80,000 — it's the TAX you'll avoid, which is the loss times the rate. That $20,000 is the future tax saving, recorded now as a deferred tax asset."},{"do":"Record the benefit. Dr Deferred Tax Asset 20,000; Cr Income Tax Benefit 20,000.","why":"Even though there's no cash refund, GAAP lets you book the future benefit now (it reduces the current-year loss on the income statement via the tax 'benefit')."},{"do":"Check whether a valuation allowance is needed. If it's 'more likely than not' (>50%) that the company WON'T have enough future income to use the NOL, reduce the DTA.","why":"A DTA is only worth something if you'll have future profits to apply it against. For a shaky startup, auditors often require a valuation allowance. If, say, only half is expected to be realized: Dr Income Tax Expense 10,000 / Cr Valuation Allowance 10,000, cutting the net DTA to $10,000."}],"takeaway":"A net operating loss carryforward is a deferred tax ASSET equal to the loss × the tax rate, because it will reduce future taxes. But if future profits are doubtful, a valuation allowance reduces the DTA to the amount 'more likely than not' to be realized."},"practice":[{"prompt":"Book income is $600,000. It includes $40,000 of tax-exempt municipal interest. Also, tax depreciation exceeds book depreciation by $50,000 this year. Tax rate is 21%. Find (a) taxable income, (b) current tax payable, (c) the deferred tax item (asset or liability) and its amount.","solution":["(a) Start at 600,000. Remove muni interest (permanent, never taxed): −40,000. Tax depreciation exceeds book by 50,000, so taxable income is LOWER by 50,000: −50,000. Taxable income = 600,000 − 40,000 − 50,000 = $510,000.","(b) Current tax payable = 510,000 × 21% = $107,100.","(c) The extra tax depreciation is a temporary difference: you deduct more now, less later → you'll pay MORE tax later → deferred tax LIABILITY = 50,000 × 21% = $10,500.","(The $40,000 muni interest is permanent and creates no deferred tax.)"]},{"prompt":"Book income is $300,000, including $25,000 of warranty expense accrued but not yet deductible for tax (deductible only when paid later). Tax rate 25%. Find taxable income, current tax payable, and the deferred tax item.","solution":["Taxable income = 300,000 + 25,000 (add back the not-yet-deductible warranty) = $325,000.","Current tax payable = 325,000 × 25% = $81,250.","The warranty is deductible LATER → you overpay now, save later → deferred tax ASSET = 25,000 × 25% = $6,250.","Entry: Dr Income Tax Expense 75,000 / Dr Deferred Tax Asset 6,250 / Cr Income Tax Payable 81,250."]},{"prompt":"A company has a deferred tax LIABILITY of $60,000 measured at the old 30% rate. New tax law lowers the enacted future rate to 21%. Compute the remeasured DTL and the effect on income tax expense in the year of enactment.","solution":["The DTL must be remeasured at the new rate. Original temporary difference = 60,000 ÷ 30% = $200,000. New DTL = 200,000 × 21% = $42,000.","The DTL falls from 60,000 to 42,000, a decrease of $18,000.","A smaller liability means less future tax owed, so income tax expense DECREASES by $18,000 in the year of enactment. Entry: Dr Deferred Tax Liability 18,000 / Cr Income Tax Expense 18,000."]}],"entries":{"w1":[{"caption":"Record income tax for the year (current plus deferred)","lines":[{"account":"Income Tax Expense","dr":120000},{"account":"Deferred Tax Asset","dr":7500},{"account":"Income Tax Payable","cr":127500}]}],"w2":[{"caption":"Recognize the benefit of the net operating loss carryforward","lines":[{"account":"Deferred Tax Asset","dr":20000},{"account":"Income Tax Benefit","cr":20000}]},{"caption":"If realization is doubtful — record a valuation allowance (example: half)","lines":[{"account":"Income Tax Expense","dr":10000},{"account":"Valuation Allowance","cr":10000}]}]},"comptable":null,"aids":{"mnemonics":[{"tag":"DTA vs DTL direction","text":"\"Pay more NOW = Asset; pay more LATER = Liability.\" Prepaid tax (overpay now, benefit later) = DTA. Deferred tax (save now, owe later) = DTL."},{"tag":"Permanent vs temporary","text":"\"Temporary Turns around; Permanent is Permanent.\" If the difference reverses → temporary → deferred tax. If it never reverses (muni interest, fines) → permanent → no deferred tax."},{"tag":"The bridge","text":"\"Expense = Payable ± Deferred.\" Income tax EXPENSE = current tax PAYABLE + ΔDTL − ΔDTA."}],"watch":["Permanent differences (muni interest, fines, the like) create NO deferred tax — they only shift the effective rate.","A warranty/bad-debt accrual deductible LATER is a DTA; accelerated tax depreciation is a DTL. Test by asking which way the FUTURE tax bill moves.","When the tax RATE changes, remeasure existing DTA/DTL at the new rate in the period of enactment — the adjustment hits income tax expense.","A valuation allowance applies only to DTAs (you can't 'not realize' a liability), when realization is < more-likely-than-not."],"cheat":"Forget memorizing DTA vs DTL. Just ask: 'Did I pay the government more or less THIS year than my book income suggests, and will it reverse?' Paid more now, get it back later → you have a receivable-like ASSET. Paid less now, owe it later → you have a LIABILITY. The tax rate just converts the timing difference into dollars."},"plain":{"plain":"Companies do their math twice: one set of books for investors (the 'book' numbers) and one for the tax authorities (the 'tax' numbers). They don't always match. Some gaps are permanent — like income the government simply never taxes — and those just are what they are. Other gaps are only about timing: you'll pay the tax, just earlier or later than the books show. Those timing gaps get parked on the balance sheet. If you've prepaid tax you'll recover later, that's an asset. If you've put off tax you'll owe later, that's a liability. That's the whole game: sorting differences into 'permanent' versus 'just a timing thing.'","terms":[{"term":"Book income","plain":"the profit you report to investors (GAAP rules)"},{"term":"Taxable income","plain":"the profit the tax authority charges you on"},{"term":"Permanent difference","plain":"a gap between the two that never goes away (e.g., income never taxed)"},{"term":"Temporary difference","plain":"a gap that's only about timing — it reverses later"},{"term":"Deferred tax asset (DTA)","plain":"tax you've effectively prepaid and will get back later"},{"term":"Deferred tax liability (DTL)","plain":"tax you've put off and will owe later"},{"term":"Valuation allowance","plain":"a haircut on a DTA when you might not actually get to use it"}]},"lecture":[{"beat":"The core idea: two scorekeepers","text":"Every company keeps score twice. One scorecard follows GAAP, for investors — that's book income. The other follows the tax code, for the government — that's taxable income. They're built for different purposes, so they rarely agree. Accounting for income taxes is entirely about handling the gaps between those two numbers."},{"beat":"Two kinds of gaps","text":"Some gaps are permanent — they exist on one scorecard and will never appear on the other. Interest on municipal bonds is real income to investors but the government never taxes it; a fine is a real expense but never deductible. Those differences just sit there forever and never create deferred tax. Other gaps are purely about timing: the same item hits both scorecards, just in different years. Depreciation is the classic — you might deduct more for tax early and less later, but over the asset's life the totals match. Timing gaps reverse, and that's what creates deferred tax."},{"beat":"Which way does a timing gap lean?","text":"Here's the intuition that replaces memorization. Ask: did this timing difference make me pay the government more or less THIS year than my book profit suggests — and will it flip later? If you paid more now and will pay less later, you've essentially prepaid tax; that future saving is an asset, a deferred tax asset. If you paid less now and will owe more later, you've put tax off; that future bill is a liability, a deferred tax liability. Warranty costs you expense now but deduct later create a DTA; accelerated tax depreciation creates a DTL."},{"beat":"Tying it into one number","text":"Finally, the income tax expense on the income statement isn't just the cash you owe this year. It's the current tax payable, adjusted for the change in those deferred balances — plus any deferred liability, minus any deferred asset. And one guardrail: if it's doubtful you'll ever have enough future profit to actually use a deferred tax asset, you write it down with a valuation allowance, because an asset you can't use isn't worth full value."}],"advanced":{"intro":"Advanced tax: intraperiod allocation, the NOL 80% limitation, and applying different enacted rates to differences reversing in different years.","lesson":[{"h":"Intraperiod tax allocation","p":["Total income tax expense must be allocated among the income-statement components that gave rise to it: continuing operations, discontinued operations, and items in OCI. This is intraperiod allocation — 'let the tax follow the income.'","Why it matters: a company with a $200,000 profit from continuing operations and a $50,000 loss from discontinued operations doesn't report one blended tax number. Each component carries its own tax, so readers see the after-tax result of each piece separately."]},{"h":"NOL carryforward limitations and graduated reversals","p":["Two refinements appear in advanced problems. First, the 80% limitation: post-2017 NOLs can offset only up to 80% of a future year's taxable income. If future income is $100,000, at most $80,000 of NOL can be used that year, with the rest carried further forward.","Second, when temporary differences reverse in different future years that have different enacted tax rates, you measure each year's reversal at THAT year's enacted rate — not a single blended rate. A difference reversing in a year with a scheduled 21% rate is measured at 21%, even if today's rate is 25%."]}],"bank":[{"d":"hard","q":"Intraperiod tax allocation requires that income tax expense be:","choices":["Allocated among continuing operations, discontinued operations, and OCI","Reported as a single line only","Deferred entirely","Ignored for discontinued operations"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: the tax is split so each income-statement component shows its own tax effect ('let the tax follow the income').","A single blended line would obscure each component's after-tax result.","Allocation is about presentation, not deferral.","Discontinued operations get their own allocated tax."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A company has an NOL carryforward of $90,000 and future taxable income of $100,000 (post-2017 rules). The maximum NOL it can use this year is:","choices":["$80,000","$90,000","$100,000","$72,000"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: the 80% limitation caps usage at 80% × $100,000 = $80,000; the remaining $10,000 carries forward.","The full $90,000 can't be used due to the 80% cap.","$100,000 exceeds both the NOL and the limit.","$72,000 applies 80% to the NOL instead of to taxable income."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A temporary difference will reverse in a future year for which a 21% rate has been enacted, though the current rate is 25%. The deferred tax should be measured at:","choices":["21% — the rate enacted for the reversal year","25% — the current rate","A blend of 21% and 25%","Whichever is higher"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: deferred taxes use the enacted rate expected to apply WHEN the difference reverses — here, 21%.","The current rate doesn't apply if a different rate is enacted for the reversal period.","You use the specific enacted rate, not a blend.","It's the enacted reversal-year rate, not the higher one."]}]}},{"id":"eps","name":"Earnings Per Share & Dilution","icon":"📊","group":"Equity","overview":"How to compute basic EPS with mid-year share changes, and why diluted EPS asks 'what if every convertible and option turned into stock?'","lesson":[{"h":"Basic EPS: income available to COMMON, per common share","p":["EPS answers: how much profit belongs to each common share? The numerator is income available to common shareholders = net income − preferred dividends (preferred holders get paid first, so their claim is removed).","The denominator is the WEIGHTED-AVERAGE number of common shares — weighted because shares issued mid-year were only outstanding part of the year and shouldn't count as if present all year."]},{"h":"Weighting shares the right way","p":["Weight each block by the fraction of the year it was outstanding. 100,000 shares all year = 100,000. An additional 40,000 issued on July 1 (out 6 months) = 40,000 × 6/12 = 20,000. Weighted average = 120,000.","Share buybacks (treasury purchases) reduce the weighted average for the months they're gone. Stock dividends and splits are applied RETROACTIVELY to the beginning of the period (and to all prior periods presented) because they don't change the underlying ownership — they just re-slice it."]},{"h":"Diluted EPS: the 'what if' worst case","p":["Some securities can become common stock: convertible bonds, convertible preferred, options, and warrants. If they converted, more shares would exist and EPS would drop. Diluted EPS shows that more conservative figure, so investors see the potential dilution.","OPTIONS/WARRANTS use the treasury stock method: assume they're exercised, the company receives the exercise proceeds, and uses that cash to buy back shares at the average market price. Only the NET new shares (issued minus repurchased) increase the denominator.","CONVERTIBLES use the if-converted method: assume conversion at the start of the year. For convertible bonds, add the after-tax interest back to the numerator (you'd no longer pay it) and add the new shares to the denominator. For convertible preferred, add back the preferred dividend to the numerator."]},{"h":"The anti-dilution guardrail","p":["A security is only included in diluted EPS if it actually DECREASES EPS (is dilutive). If including it would RAISE EPS — anti-dilutive — it is excluded, because diluted EPS can never be higher than basic.","You test securities from most dilutive to least, adding each only while EPS keeps falling. This prevents a misleadingly high 'diluted' figure."]},{"h":"The treasury-stock method for options and warrants","p":["Dilutive options and warrants add shares to the diluted EPS denominator using the treasury-stock method. The assumption: the company uses the cash from exercising the options to buy back shares at the average market price. Only the net new shares are added.","Example: 10,000 options with a $20 exercise price when the average market price is $25. Exercise brings in $200,000, which buys back $200,000 ÷ $25 = 8,000 shares. Net incremental shares = 10,000 − 8,000 = 2,000 added to the denominator. Options are only dilutive when the market price exceeds the exercise price."]},{"h":"Multiple potential securities and the ordering rule","p":["When a company has several convertible securities, you test them from most dilutive to least dilutive, adding each only if it continues to reduce EPS. The moment a security would start to raise EPS, it (and any after it) is anti-dilutive and excluded.","This sequencing matters: a security that looks dilutive alone might become anti-dilutive once others are included, so the order of testing affects the final diluted figure."]}],"keyFormulas":["Basic EPS = (Net income − preferred dividends) ÷ weighted-average common shares","Weighted shares: each block × (months outstanding ÷ 12)","Treasury stock method (options): net new shares = issued − (proceeds ÷ avg market price)","If-converted (bonds): numerator += after-tax interest; denominator += conversion shares"],"bank":[{"d":"easy","q":"The numerator of basic EPS is:","choices":["Net income","Net income − preferred dividends","Net income − common dividends","Operating income"],"answer":1,"why":["Preferred dividends must be removed first.","Correct: income available to common = net income − preferred dividends.","Common dividends are NOT subtracted; they are a distribution of the earnings being measured.","EPS uses net income, not operating income."]},{"d":"easy","q":"Diluted EPS compared with basic EPS is always:","choices":["Higher","Equal or lower","Exactly half","Unrelated"],"answer":1,"why":["Dilution cannot raise EPS.","Correct: diluted EPS is equal to or lower than basic; anti-dilutive securities are excluded to enforce this.","There is no fixed ratio.","They are directly related — diluted starts from basic."]},{"d":"easy","q":"A stock split during the year is applied to the weighted-average share count:","choices":["From the split date forward","Retroactively to the beginning of the period","Only in the next year","Not at all"],"answer":1,"why":["Splits are not applied prospectively.","Correct: splits and stock dividends are applied retroactively to the start of the period (and prior periods shown), since ownership proportions are unchanged.","It affects the current period immediately, retroactively.","It must be reflected."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Net income $500,000; preferred dividends $50,000. Shares: 200,000 on Jan 1; issued 60,000 on Oct 1. Basic EPS is approximately:","choices":["$2.09","$2.25","$2.50","$1.96"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: weighted shares = 200,000 + 60,000 × 3/12 = 215,000; numerator = 500,000 − 50,000 = 450,000; EPS = 450,000 / 215,000 ≈ $2.09.","$2.25 forgets to weight the new shares.","$2.50 ignores the new shares entirely.","$1.96 counts the new shares for the full year."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Under the treasury stock method, options to buy 10,000 shares at $20 exist; the average market price is $25. The net increase in diluted shares is:","choices":["10,000","8,000","2,000","0"],"answer":2,"why":["10,000 ignores the assumed buyback.","8,000 is the repurchased amount, not the net.","Correct: proceeds = 10,000 × $20 = $200,000; shares repurchased = 200,000 / 25 = 8,000; net = 10,000 − 8,000 = 2,000.","There is dilution because market price exceeds the exercise price."]},{"d":"medium","q":"For convertible bonds under the if-converted method, the numerator is adjusted by adding back:","choices":["The face value of the bonds","The after-tax interest expense on the bonds","The pre-tax interest expense","Preferred dividends"],"answer":1,"why":["Face value affects shares assumed issued, not the numerator income.","Correct: if converted, the company would not pay the bond interest, so add it back NET OF TAX to income available to common.","It must be after-tax, since interest is tax-deductible.","Preferred dividends relate to convertible preferred, not bonds."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A convertible bond adds $40,000 after-tax interest to the numerator and 20,000 shares to the denominator. Basic EPS is $3.00 (450,000 income / 150,000 shares). Including the bond, is it dilutive?","choices":["Yes; incremental EPS $2.00 < $3.00 → dilutive","No; incremental EPS $2.00 > basic","Yes; incremental EPS $4.00","Cannot tell"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: incremental EPS of the bond = 40,000 / 20,000 = $2.00, which is below basic $3.00, so it pulls EPS down → dilutive, include it.","$2.00 is less than $3.00, so it IS dilutive.","Incremental EPS is 40,000/20,000 = $2.00, not $4.00.","It can be determined via the incremental-EPS test."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A company reports a net LOSS. Its outstanding stock options are:","choices":["Always included in diluted EPS","Excluded, because they would be anti-dilutive","Included only if exercise price < market","Converted to preferred"],"answer":1,"why":["With a loss, adding shares would shrink the loss per share (raise EPS) — anti-dilutive.","Correct: in a loss year, potential common shares are anti-dilutive (they reduce the loss per share), so they are excluded; diluted EPS = basic.","Even in-the-money options are excluded in a loss year for EPS purposes.","Options do not become preferred."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Order of testing potentially dilutive securities for inclusion in diluted EPS:","choices":["Largest face value first","Most dilutive (lowest incremental EPS) first","Alphabetical","Least dilutive first"],"answer":1,"why":["Face value is irrelevant to the ordering.","Correct: rank by incremental EPS, adding the most dilutive first and continuing only while EPS keeps falling.","Ordering is by dilutive effect, not name.","Starting with the least dilutive can wrongly exclude truly dilutive securities."]},{"d":"easy","q":"A company has only common stock (no preferred, no convertibles, no options). Its diluted EPS:","choices":["Is higher than basic EPS","Equals basic EPS","Cannot be computed","Is always zero"],"answer":1,"why":["Diluted can never exceed basic.","Correct: with no dilutive securities, there's nothing to dilute, so diluted EPS equals basic EPS.","It can be computed — it just equals basic.","It equals basic EPS, which is not zero if there's income."]},{"d":"medium","q":"A 2-for-1 stock split occurs on July 1. In computing weighted-average shares for the year, the split is applied:","choices":["Only from July 1 forward","Retroactively to January 1","In the following year","Only to new shares"],"answer":1,"why":["Splits are not applied prospectively.","Correct: splits and stock dividends are applied retroactively to the beginning of the period (and all prior periods presented) since ownership proportions don't change.","It affects the current period retroactively.","It applies to all shares, retroactively."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Options are 'in the money' (market price above exercise price). Under the treasury stock method they are:","choices":["Anti-dilutive and excluded","Dilutive and included","Ignored","Treated as preferred stock"],"answer":1,"why":["In-the-money options are dilutive, not anti-dilutive.","Correct: when market price exceeds the exercise price, assumed exercise adds more shares than the proceeds can buy back → net new shares → dilutive, so included.","They are not ignored when dilutive.","Options are not preferred stock."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A convertible preferred stock has an incremental EPS effect of $4.50, while basic EPS is $4.00. The convertible preferred is:","choices":["Dilutive; include it","Anti-dilutive; exclude it","Always included regardless","Converted to common automatically"],"answer":1,"why":["Including it would RAISE EPS, which is anti-dilutive.","Correct: incremental EPS ($4.50) exceeds basic ($4.00), so including it would increase EPS — anti-dilutive, so it's excluded from diluted EPS.","Anti-dilutive securities are never included.","Whether it converts is hypothetical and only assumed if dilutive."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Using the treasury-stock method: 10,000 options with a $20 exercise price, average market price $25. Incremental shares added to diluted EPS:","choices":["2,000","10,000","8,000","0"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: $200,000 exercise proceeds buy back 8,000 shares at $25; net = 10,000 − 8,000 = 2,000.","10,000 ignores the assumed buyback.","8,000 is the shares repurchased, not the net addition.","They're dilutive here since market ($25) exceeds exercise ($20)."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Options are dilutive (included in diluted EPS) only when:","choices":["The market price exceeds the exercise price","The exercise price exceeds the market price","They are always included","The company is profitable"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: only 'in the money' options (market > exercise) add net shares and dilute.","That's anti-dilutive — excluded.","Anti-dilutive options are excluded.","Profitability matters for net-loss anti-dilution, but the key test here is market vs exercise price."]}],"worked":{"title":"Computing basic EPS with a mid-year share issuance","setup":"A company had 100,000 common shares outstanding all year. On July 1, it issued 40,000 more shares. For the year, net income was $440,000, and it owes $40,000 in preferred dividends. We need basic earnings per share (EPS).","steps":[{"do":"Build the numerator: net income − preferred dividends = $440,000 − $40,000 = $400,000.","why":"EPS measures profit available to COMMON shareholders. Preferred shareholders get paid first, so their $40,000 claim is removed. What's left, $400,000, belongs to the common shareholders."},{"do":"Weight the original shares: 100,000 shares × 12/12 = 100,000.","why":"These shares were outstanding the entire year, so they count fully. The weighting is 'fraction of the year the shares existed' — here, all 12 months."},{"do":"Weight the new shares: 40,000 shares × 6/12 = 20,000.","why":"The 40,000 new shares only existed from July 1 — that's 6 months, or half the year. It would be unfair to count them as if they'd been around all year, so we count them at half weight: 20,000 'equivalent' full-year shares."},{"do":"Add to get weighted-average shares: 100,000 + 20,000 = 120,000.","why":"This is the average number of common shares that were effectively at work earning profit during the year. Using a simple year-end count of 140,000 would understate EPS; using the weighted 120,000 is correct."},{"do":"Divide: Basic EPS = $400,000 ÷ 120,000 = $3.33 per share.","why":"Each common share earned about $3.33 of profit this year. That's the figure investors compare across companies and across years."}],"takeaway":"Basic EPS = (net income − preferred dividends) ÷ weighted-average common shares. Always strip out preferred dividends from the top, and always weight shares by the fraction of the year they were outstanding — new shares issued mid-year count only for the months they existed."},"worked2":{"title":"Computing DILUTED EPS with a convertible bond (if-converted method)","setup":"A company has net income of $500,000 and 100,000 common shares outstanding all year (so basic EPS = $5.00). It also has $1,000,000 of 6% convertible bonds that could convert into 20,000 common shares. The tax rate is 25%. We'll test whether the bonds dilute EPS.","steps":[{"do":"Compute basic EPS first: 500,000 ÷ 100,000 = $5.00.","why":"Diluted EPS always starts from basic, then asks 'what if the convertibles became stock?' We need the baseline to compare against."},{"do":"Imagine the bonds convert. Then the company would NOT pay the bond interest. Annual interest = 1,000,000 × 6% = $60,000.","why":"Under the if-converted method, you pretend conversion happened at the start of the year. If the bonds were shares, there'd be no interest expense on them — so that expense gets added back to income."},{"do":"Add back the interest NET OF TAX: 60,000 × (1 − 0.25) = $45,000 added to the numerator.","why":"Interest is tax-deductible, so removing it would also remove its tax shield. The true income effect is the after-tax interest, $45,000. The numerator becomes 500,000 + 45,000 = $545,000."},{"do":"Add the conversion shares to the denominator: 100,000 + 20,000 = 120,000.","why":"If the bonds convert, 20,000 new shares exist. The denominator grows to reflect them."},{"do":"Test dilution with incremental EPS = after-tax interest ÷ new shares = 45,000 ÷ 20,000 = $2.25.","why":"Compare this $2.25 to basic EPS of $5.00. Because $2.25 is BELOW $5.00, including the bond pulls EPS down — it's DILUTIVE, so we include it."},{"do":"Compute diluted EPS = 545,000 ÷ 120,000 = $4.54.","why":"Diluted EPS ($4.54) is lower than basic ($5.00), confirming the bonds dilute. This is the more conservative figure investors use to gauge worst-case ownership dilution."}],"takeaway":"The if-converted method tests convertibles by adding back their after-tax interest to the numerator and the conversion shares to the denominator. If the resulting incremental EPS is below basic EPS, the security is dilutive and is included; diluted EPS ends up lower than basic."},"practice":[{"prompt":"Net income is $600,000; preferred dividends are $60,000. The company had 150,000 common shares on Jan 1 and issued 30,000 more on April 1. Compute basic EPS.","solution":["Numerator = net income − preferred dividends = 600,000 − 60,000 = $540,000.","Weight shares: original 150,000 × 12/12 = 150,000. New shares 30,000 × 9/12 = 22,500 (out from April 1 = 9 months).","Weighted-average shares = 150,000 + 22,500 = 172,500.","Basic EPS = 540,000 ÷ 172,500 = $3.13."]},{"prompt":"Net income is $800,000 with no preferred stock. The company had 200,000 shares outstanding all year, but bought back 24,000 shares as treasury stock on October 1. Compute basic EPS.","solution":["A buyback REDUCES the weighted average for the months the shares are gone. The 24,000 shares were repurchased Oct 1, so they're absent for 3 months (Oct–Dec).","Weighted reduction = 24,000 × 3/12 = 6,000. Weighted-average shares = 200,000 − 6,000 = 194,000.","Basic EPS = 800,000 ÷ 194,000 = $4.12."]},{"prompt":"Using the treasury stock method: there are options to buy 12,000 shares at an exercise price of $15. The average market price during the year is $20. Compute the net new shares added to the diluted EPS denominator.","solution":["Assume the options are exercised: proceeds = 12,000 × $15 = $180,000.","Assume those proceeds buy back shares at the average market price: 180,000 ÷ 20 = 9,000 shares repurchased.","Net new shares = shares issued − shares repurchased = 12,000 − 9,000 = 3,000 added to the diluted denominator.","(The options are dilutive because the $20 market price exceeds the $15 exercise price.)"]}],"entries":{"w1":[],"w2":[]},"comptable":{"w1":{"title":"Basic EPS computation","rows":[["Net income","$440,000"],["Less: preferred dividends","(40,000)"],["Income available to common (numerator)","$400,000"],["Shares outstanding Jan 1: 100,000 × 12/12","100,000"],["Shares issued Jul 1: 40,000 × 6/12","20,000"],["Weighted-average shares (denominator)","120,000"],["Basic EPS = 400,000 ÷ 120,000","$3.33"]],"highlightLast":true},"w2":{"title":"Diluted EPS — if-converted method","rows":[["Basic EPS (500,000 ÷ 100,000)","$5.00"],["Add back after-tax bond interest: 60,000 × (1 − 0.25)","+$45,000"],["Adjusted numerator","$545,000"],["Add conversion shares","+20,000"],["Adjusted denominator","120,000"],["Incremental EPS (45,000 ÷ 20,000) = $2.25 < $5.00 → dilutive","include"],["Diluted EPS = 545,000 ÷ 120,000","$4.54"]],"highlightLast":true}},"aids":{"mnemonics":[{"tag":"Basic EPS","text":"\"Income to common, over weighted shares.\" Numerator = net income − preferred dividends; denominator = weighted-average shares."},{"tag":"Two dilution methods","text":"\"Options → Treasury; Convertibles → If-converted.\" Options/warrants use the treasury-stock method; convertible bonds/preferred use if-converted."},{"tag":"Weighting","text":"\"Months in, months out.\" Weight every share block by the fraction of the year it was actually outstanding."}],"watch":["Subtract PREFERRED dividends from the numerator — never common dividends.","Splits and stock dividends are applied RETROACTIVELY to the start of the year (and prior years shown), not from their date.","Diluted EPS can never exceed basic — anti-dilutive securities are excluded. In a NET LOSS year, all potential shares are anti-dilutive, so diluted = basic.","If-converted bonds add back interest NET OF TAX to the numerator; convertible preferred adds back the preferred dividend."],"cheat":"Diluted EPS is a 'worst-case ownership' question: 'If everyone who COULD become a shareholder did, how thin would each slice of profit get?' You only count a conversion if it actually makes the slice thinner (dilutive). Anything that would make EPS go UP is ignored, because that's not a worst case."},"plain":{"plain":"Earnings per share answers a simple question: of all the profit, how much belongs to each share of stock? You take the profit that's left for common shareholders (after the preferred shareholders get their cut) and divide by how many shares there were. The one wrinkle: if shares came or went during the year, you count them only for the slice of the year they existed. 'Diluted' EPS asks a what-if: if everybody who could turn their options or convertible bonds into stock did, how much thinner would each slice get? You only count those if they'd actually make the slice smaller.","terms":[{"term":"Basic EPS","plain":"profit for common shareholders ÷ number of shares"},{"term":"Weighted-average shares","plain":"share count adjusted for how long each batch was around"},{"term":"Preferred dividends","plain":"the cut that preferred shareholders get before common ones"},{"term":"Diluted EPS","plain":"the worst-case 'what if everyone converted to shares' version"},{"term":"Anti-dilutive","plain":"a what-if that would make EPS go up — so we ignore it"}]},"lecture":[{"beat":"What the number means","text":"Earnings per share takes a giant, abstract profit figure and makes it personal to one share of stock. If you own a slice of the company, EPS tells you how much profit sits behind your slice. That's why investors obsess over it — it's profit scaled down to the unit they actually buy."},{"beat":"Building basic EPS","text":"The numerator is the profit that belongs to common shareholders — so you start with net income and remove the preferred shareholders' cut, since they get paid first. The denominator is the number of common shares. But there's a subtlety: shares come and go during the year, so you weight them by how long each batch was actually outstanding. A block of shares issued halfway through the year only counts for half a year. That weighted-average share count is what you divide by."},{"beat":"The 'what if everyone converted' question","text":"Some securities aren't shares yet but could become shares — stock options, convertible bonds, convertible preferred. If they all turned into stock, there'd be more shares splitting the same profit, and each slice would get thinner. Diluted EPS shows that more cautious, worst-case picture so investors aren't caught off guard. Options use the treasury-stock method; convertibles use the if-converted method, where you add back the interest or dividends you'd no longer pay and add the new shares."},{"beat":"The guardrail","text":"There's one firm rule: diluted EPS can never come out higher than basic. If pretending a security converted would actually raise EPS, that security is 'anti-dilutive' and you simply exclude it. Diluted is meant to be the conservative figure, so anything that would flatter the number gets left out — and in a year with a net loss, all those potential shares are anti-dilutive, so diluted equals basic."}]},{"id":"pensions","name":"Pensions & Postretirement Benefits","icon":"👵","group":"Liabilities","overview":"Why a pension promise is a present-value liability that grows with service and interest, and how five moving parts net into a single pension expense.","lesson":[{"h":"What the employer actually promised","p":["In a DEFINED-BENEFIT plan, the employer promises a specific future benefit (e.g., 2% of final salary per year of service) and bears all the investment and longevity risk. Contrast this with a defined-CONTRIBUTION plan (like a 401k), where the employer just deposits cash and the employee bears the risk — there the accounting is trivial (expense = the contribution).","The hard accounting is for defined-benefit plans, because the obligation is a present value of payments that may not be made for decades, and it depends on estimates: future salaries, employee turnover, life expectancy, and a discount rate."]},{"h":"The PBO — the obligation being measured","p":["The Projected Benefit Obligation (PBO) is the actuarial present value of all benefits employees have earned to date, measured using FUTURE (projected) salary levels. The 'projected' part matters: because most formulas tie benefits to final pay, the obligation reflects expected raises, not just today's salary.","Two events grow the PBO each year: SERVICE COST (employees earned one more year of benefits) and INTEREST COST (the obligation is one year closer to payment, so the discount unwinds). Interest cost = beginning PBO × the discount rate."]},{"h":"Plan assets and funded status","p":["The employer funds the promise by contributing cash to a separate pension trust, which invests it. The fair value of those plan assets grows with actual returns and contributions and shrinks as benefits are paid out.","FUNDED STATUS = fair value of plan assets − PBO. If assets exceed the PBO, the plan is overfunded (a net asset). If the PBO exceeds assets — the common case — it's underfunded, and the company reports a net pension LIABILITY on its balance sheet for the shortfall."]},{"h":"Pension expense: the SIR-AG components","p":["Annual pension expense nets five pieces. Mnemonic SIR-AG: Service cost (+), Interest cost (+), Return on plan assets (− expected return, which offsets cost), Amortization of prior service cost (+, from plan amendments granting credit for past service), and Gains/losses (±, from actuarial changes and differences between expected and actual returns).","The expected return is subtracted because investment earnings reduce the net cost of funding the promise. Expected return = beginning plan assets × expected long-term rate.","Worked: beginning PBO $1,000,000, plan assets $900,000, service cost $80,000, discount rate 6%, expected return 8%, no amortization or gains/losses. Interest cost = 1,000,000 × 6% = $60,000. Expected return = 900,000 × 8% = $72,000. Pension expense = 80,000 + 60,000 − 72,000 = $68,000. Funded status = 900,000 − 1,000,000 = −$100,000 (underfunded → $100,000 net liability)."]},{"h":"Expected vs actual return, and deferred gains/losses","p":["Pension expense uses the EXPECTED return on plan assets, not the actual. Why? Actual returns swing wildly year to year, and running that volatility straight through expense would whipsaw earnings. So the difference between expected and actual return is deferred as an unrecognized gain or loss.","Example: expected return is $32,000 (8% on $400,000 of assets) but actual return is only $25,000. The $7,000 shortfall isn't expensed now — it's deferred and may be amortized in later via the corridor."]},{"h":"The corridor method for amortizing gains and losses","p":["Deferred gains and losses can pile up. The corridor approach says: only amortize the portion of accumulated unrecognized gain/loss that exceeds 10% of the greater of the PBO or plan assets. The excess is spread over the employees' average remaining service period.","Example: PBO $500,000, assets $400,000, accumulated unrecognized loss $70,000, average remaining service 10 years. The corridor is 10% × $500,000 = $50,000. Excess = $70,000 − $50,000 = $20,000. Amortization = $20,000 ÷ 10 = $2,000 added to this year's pension expense."]},{"h":"Prior service cost from plan amendments","p":["When a company amends its plan to grant more generous benefits (often for past service), it creates prior service cost. This isn't expensed all at once — it's recognized in other comprehensive income at adoption, then amortized into pension expense over the employees' remaining service years.","The intuition: the company got a benefit (employee goodwill, retention) that it'll enjoy over future years, so the cost is matched to those future years rather than dumped into one period."]}],"keyFormulas":["Funded status = fair value of plan assets − PBO","Interest cost = beginning PBO × discount rate","Expected return = beginning plan assets × expected long-term rate","Pension expense = Service + Interest − Expected return + PSC amortization ± Gains/losses"],"bank":[{"d":"easy","q":"In a defined-benefit plan, who bears the investment risk?","choices":["The employee","The employer","The pension trustee personally","The government"],"answer":1,"why":["In a defined-contribution plan the employee bears the risk, not here.","Correct: the employer promises a fixed benefit and must make up any shortfall, so it bears the investment and longevity risk.","The trustee administers but does not personally guarantee the benefit.","The government does not bear ordinary plan investment risk."]},{"d":"easy","q":"Which component DECREASES net pension expense?","choices":["Service cost","Interest cost","Expected return on plan assets","Prior service cost amortization"],"answer":2,"why":["Service cost increases expense.","Interest cost increases expense.","Correct: expected return on plan assets offsets the cost of funding, reducing net pension expense.","PSC amortization increases expense."]},{"d":"easy","q":"Interest cost on the PBO is computed as:","choices":["Ending PBO × expected return rate","Beginning PBO × discount rate","Plan assets × discount rate","Service cost × discount rate"],"answer":1,"why":["It uses the discount rate on the beginning PBO, not the return rate on the ending balance.","Correct: interest cost = beginning PBO × the discount (settlement) rate, reflecting the unwinding of the discount.","That would be a return-on-assets style calc, not interest cost.","Service cost is a separate component, not the base for interest."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Plan assets are $750,000 and the PBO is $900,000. The balance sheet reports:","choices":["A net pension asset of $150,000","A net pension liability of $150,000","Nothing until benefits are paid","A net liability of $900,000"],"answer":1,"why":["An asset arises only when assets exceed the PBO.","Correct: funded status = 750,000 − 900,000 = −$150,000 underfunded → a net pension liability of $150,000.","The funded status is recognized immediately, not deferred.","Only the shortfall ($150,000), not the gross PBO, is the net liability."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Beginning plan assets are $600,000 with an expected long-term return of 7%. The expected return component of pension expense is:","choices":["$42,000 (reduces expense)","$42,000 (increases expense)","$60,000 (reduces expense)","Cannot be computed"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: 600,000 × 7% = $42,000, and expected return REDUCES net pension expense.","Expected return reduces, not increases, expense.","$60,000 uses the wrong rate.","It can be computed directly from the given figures."]},{"d":"medium","q":"A plan amendment grants employees credit for past service, increasing the PBO. This creates:","choices":["An immediate expense for the full amount","Prior service cost, amortized over future service","A permanent reduction in plan assets","Interest cost"],"answer":1,"why":["It is not expensed all at once under U.S. GAAP.","Correct: the increase is prior service cost (PSC), recognized in OCI and amortized to pension expense over employees' remaining service.","It affects the obligation, not plan assets directly.","Interest cost is the unwinding of the discount, unrelated to amendments."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Beginning PBO $2,000,000; service cost $150,000; discount rate 5%; benefits paid $90,000; no actuarial gains/losses. The ending PBO is:","choices":["$2,160,000","$2,150,000","$2,250,000","$2,060,000"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: ending PBO = 2,000,000 + service 150,000 + interest (2,000,000 × 5% = 100,000) − benefits paid 90,000 = $2,160,000.","$2,150,000 omits one component.","$2,250,000 ignores benefits paid.","$2,060,000 omits service cost."]},{"d":"hard","q":"The expected return on plan assets is $80,000 but the ACTUAL return is $95,000. The $15,000 difference is:","choices":["Immediately added to pension expense","An asset gain deferred in OCI (then amortized via the corridor)","Ignored entirely","Recorded as interest income"],"answer":1,"why":["U.S. GAAP uses expected return in expense; the difference is not expensed immediately.","Correct: the gap between actual and expected return is an unrecognized gain/loss deferred in OCI, subject to corridor amortization.","It is tracked, not ignored.","It is not interest income; it is an actuarial gain on assets."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Under U.S. GAAP corridor amortization, net gains/losses are amortized only when they exceed:","choices":["Zero","10% of the greater of beginning PBO or plan assets","5% of plan assets","The service cost"],"answer":1,"why":["Small gains/losses within the corridor are not amortized, so the threshold is not zero.","Correct: amortization begins only on the excess over 10% of the greater of beginning PBO or plan assets — the 'corridor.'","The corridor is 10%, not 5%, and uses the greater of PBO or assets.","Service cost is unrelated to the corridor threshold."]},{"d":"easy","q":"In a DEFINED-CONTRIBUTION plan, the employer's pension expense each period equals:","choices":["The change in the PBO","The agreed contribution amount","The expected return on assets","Service cost plus interest cost"],"answer":1,"why":["There is no PBO in a defined-contribution plan.","Correct: in a defined-contribution plan the employer simply expenses the contribution it agreed to make — the accounting is straightforward.","Expected return is a defined-BENEFIT concept.","SIR-AG components apply to defined-benefit plans, not defined-contribution."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Service cost in a defined-benefit plan represents:","choices":["Interest on the existing obligation","The present value of benefits employees earned by working this period","The return on plan assets","Benefits paid to retirees"],"answer":1,"why":["That describes interest cost, a separate component.","Correct: service cost is the PV of the additional benefits employees earned through this period's service.","Return on assets is a different component.","Benefits paid reduce the PBO and plan assets; they are not service cost."]},{"d":"medium","q":"If plan assets ($1,200,000) exceed the PBO ($1,000,000), the balance sheet reports:","choices":["A net pension liability of $200,000","A net pension asset of $200,000","Nothing","A liability of $1,000,000"],"answer":1,"why":["A liability arises only when the PBO exceeds assets.","Correct: funded status = 1,200,000 − 1,000,000 = +$200,000 overfunded → a net pension ASSET of $200,000.","An overfunded plan is recognized as an asset.","The net position is $200,000, not the gross PBO."]},{"d":"hard","q":"An increase in the discount rate used to measure the PBO will, all else equal:","choices":["Increase the PBO","Decrease the PBO","Have no effect on the PBO","Eliminate service cost"],"answer":1,"why":["A higher discount rate lowers present values, so the PBO falls.","Correct: the PBO is a present value; raising the discount rate discounts future benefits more heavily, decreasing the PBO.","It does affect the PBO — present values are rate-sensitive.","Service cost still exists; it is measured using the new rate."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Pension expense includes the EXPECTED return on plan assets rather than the actual return because:","choices":["It smooths the volatility of actual investment returns","Actual returns are unknown","GAAP prohibits using actual returns ever","Expected returns are always higher"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: using expected return smooths year-to-year market swings; the expected-vs-actual difference is deferred.","Actual returns are known but deliberately not run straight through expense.","Actual return does enter the deferred gain/loss; it's just not in expense directly.","Expected isn't always higher — it can be above or below actual."]},{"d":"hard","q":"PBO is $500,000, plan assets $400,000, accumulated unrecognized loss $70,000, average remaining service 10 years. Corridor amortization this year is:","choices":["$2,000","$7,000","$5,000","$0"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: corridor = 10% × greater of PBO/assets = $50,000; excess = $70,000 − $50,000 = $20,000; ÷ 10 years = $2,000.","$7,000 ignores the corridor threshold.","$5,000 uses the wrong base.","There is an excess above the corridor, so amortization isn't zero."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Prior service cost from a plan amendment is:","choices":["Recognized in OCI, then amortized to pension expense over remaining service","Expensed entirely in the year of amendment","Never recognized","Recorded as an asset"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: it goes to OCI at adoption and amortizes into expense over employees' remaining service years.","It's spread out, not expensed all at once.","It is recognized, just gradually.","It increases the obligation, not an asset."]}],"worked":{"title":"Computing pension expense and funded status for one year","setup":"At the start of the year, a company's pension obligation (PBO) is $1,000,000 and its pension plan assets are worth $900,000. During the year, employees earn $80,000 of new benefits (service cost). The discount rate is 6% and the expected return on plan assets is 8%. There's no prior service cost or gains/losses this year. We need pension expense and the funded status.","steps":[{"do":"Start with service cost: $80,000. This is one of the components that ADDS to pension expense.","why":"Service cost is the present value of the new retirement benefits employees earned by working this year. The more they work, the bigger the promise grows — so it's a cost of this year's operations."},{"do":"Add interest cost = beginning PBO × discount rate = $1,000,000 × 6% = $60,000.","why":"The pension obligation is a present value. Each year that passes, the future payments get one year closer, so the discount 'unwinds' and the obligation grows. That growth is interest cost, and it adds to expense."},{"do":"Subtract expected return on plan assets = beginning assets × expected rate = $900,000 × 8% = $72,000.","why":"The money set aside in the pension fund is invested and earns returns. Those earnings help pay for the promise, so they REDUCE the net cost to the company. This is the one big component that lowers pension expense."},{"do":"Net the components: $80,000 + $60,000 − $72,000 = $68,000 pension expense.","why":"Pension expense is a single net number built from several moving parts (the SIR-AG components). Here: service + interest − expected return = $68,000. That's what hits the income statement."},{"do":"Compute funded status = plan assets − PBO = $900,000 − $1,000,000 = −$100,000.","why":"Funded status compares what you've saved (assets) to what you owe (PBO). A negative number means the promise is bigger than the savings — the plan is UNDERFUNDED."},{"do":"Report a net pension liability of $100,000 on the balance sheet.","why":"Because the plan is underfunded by $100,000, the company must show that shortfall as a liability — it represents benefits promised but not yet funded."}],"takeaway":"Pension expense nets several pieces: service cost and interest cost add to it, while the expected return on plan assets subtracts. Funded status = plan assets − PBO; if assets fall short, you report a net pension liability for the gap."},"worked2":{"title":"Pension expense WITH prior service cost amortization","setup":"This year a company has service cost $50,000, interest cost $30,000, expected return on assets $40,000, AND $10,000 of prior service cost amortization (from a plan amendment in a prior year that granted extra benefits for past service). We'll fold in that fifth component.","steps":[{"do":"Service cost = 50,000 (adds to expense).","why":"The value of new benefits earned by employees working this year — always increases pension expense."},{"do":"Interest cost = 30,000 (adds).","why":"The unwinding of the discount on the PBO as the obligation moves one year closer to payment."},{"do":"Expected return on plan assets = 40,000 (subtracts).","why":"Investment earnings on the pension fund offset the cost of funding the promise — the one component that lowers expense."},{"do":"Prior service cost amortization = 10,000 (adds).","why":"When a company amends its plan to grant employees credit for PAST service, the obligation jumps. That cost isn't expensed all at once — it's parked in OCI and amortized into pension expense over employees' remaining working years. This year's slice is $10,000."},{"do":"Net it all: 50,000 + 30,000 − 40,000 + 10,000 = $50,000 pension expense.","why":"This shows the full SIR-AG formula in action: Service + Interest − Expected return + PSC Amortization (± Gains/losses, which are zero here). All five components net into one expense figure."}],"takeaway":"Pension expense combines up to five components (SIR-AG): service cost, interest cost, and prior-service-cost amortization all ADD; expected return SUBTRACTS; gains/losses adjust either way. Prior service cost from plan amendments is amortized in gradually rather than expensed immediately."},"practice":[{"prompt":"Beginning PBO is $800,000; beginning plan assets are $700,000. Service cost is $60,000, the discount rate is 5%, and the expected return on assets is 7%. No prior service cost or gains/losses. Compute (a) pension expense and (b) funded status.","solution":["(a) Service cost = 60,000 (adds). Interest cost = 800,000 × 5% = 40,000 (adds). Expected return = 700,000 × 7% = 49,000 (subtracts). Pension expense = 60,000 + 40,000 − 49,000 = $51,000.","(b) Funded status = plan assets − PBO = 700,000 − 800,000 = −$100,000 → underfunded → report a $100,000 net pension liability."]},{"prompt":"Beginning PBO is $1,500,000. During the year: service cost $100,000, discount rate 6%, benefits paid to retirees $70,000, no actuarial gains/losses. Compute the ending PBO.","solution":["Ending PBO = beginning PBO + service cost + interest cost − benefits paid.","Interest cost = 1,500,000 × 6% = $90,000.","Ending PBO = 1,500,000 + 100,000 + 90,000 − 70,000 = $1,620,000.","(Service cost and interest grow the obligation; benefits paid reduce it because that promise has been settled.)"]},{"prompt":"The expected return on plan assets was $50,000, but the actual return turned out to be $58,000. (a) What is the $8,000 difference called? (b) How is it treated under U.S. GAAP?","solution":["(a) The $8,000 (actual 58,000 − expected 50,000) is an asset GAIN — an unexpected favorable return.","(b) Under U.S. GAAP, pension expense uses the EXPECTED return, so this $8,000 difference is not expensed immediately. It's deferred as an unrecognized gain in OCI and later amortized into expense via the 'corridor' method (only the portion exceeding 10% of the greater of PBO or plan assets)."]}],"entries":{"w1":[{"caption":"Record annual pension expense; company contributes $60,000 cash to the plan","lines":[{"account":"Pension Expense","dr":68000},{"account":"Cash (contribution to plan)","cr":60000},{"account":"Pension Liability","cr":8000}]}],"w2":[{"caption":"Record annual pension expense including prior service cost amortization; $50,000 contributed","lines":[{"account":"Pension Expense","dr":50000},{"account":"Cash (contribution to plan)","cr":50000}]}]},"comptable":null,"aids":{"mnemonics":[{"tag":"Pension expense — \"SIR-AG\"","text":"Service cost (+) · Interest cost (+) · Return on assets (− expected) · Amortization of prior service cost (+) · Gains/losses (±). Only the expected Return SUBTRACTS."},{"tag":"Funded status","text":"\"Assets minus Promise.\" Funded status = plan Assets − PBO. Negative = underfunded = net liability."},{"tag":"Interest cost base","text":"\"Beginning balance, discount rate.\" Interest cost = BEGINNING PBO × discount rate (not ending, not the return rate)."}],"watch":["Expected return REDUCES expense; the actual return does not hit expense directly — the difference is a deferred gain/loss in OCI.","Interest cost uses the DISCOUNT rate on the BEGINNING PBO; expected return uses the EXPECTED rate on BEGINNING assets. Don't cross the rates.","Prior service cost (from a plan amendment) is amortized in gradually — NOT expensed all at once.","A higher discount rate LOWERS the PBO (it's a present value)."],"cheat":"Picture two buckets: the PBO (what you owe retirees) and plan assets (what you've saved). Expense is just 'what made the gap between them grow this year' — new benefits earned (service) and the obligation aging (interest) widen the gap; investment earnings (expected return) narrow it. SIR-AG is just those forces with signs."},"plain":{"plain":"A pension is a promise: work for us, and we'll pay you in retirement. That promise is a debt the company owes its future retirees, and it's huge and far off, so it has to be estimated. The company sets money aside in a fund and invests it to cover the promise. Each year the debt grows (people earn more benefits, and the clock ticks toward payout) while the invested money hopefully grows too. The yearly 'pension expense' is basically how much the gap between the promise and the savings widened this year. If the savings fall short of the promise, that shortfall sits on the books as something owed.","terms":[{"term":"Defined-benefit plan","plain":"the company promises a set retirement payout and bears the risk"},{"term":"PBO (projected benefit obligation)","plain":"the estimated total the company owes future retirees, in today's dollars"},{"term":"Plan assets","plain":"the money set aside and invested to pay the pensions"},{"term":"Funded status","plain":"savings minus the promise — negative means you're behind"},{"term":"Service cost","plain":"the new chunk of promise employees earned by working this year"},{"term":"Interest cost","plain":"the promise getting bigger simply because payout is one year closer"}]},"lecture":[{"beat":"The promise at the heart of it","text":"A defined-benefit pension is a promise: work for us now, and we'll pay you a defined amount every year in retirement. That promise is a genuine debt the company owes its future retirees. The catch is that it's enormous, decades away, and depends on guesses — how long people live, future salaries, turnover. So the accounting is really an exercise in measuring a fuzzy, far-off obligation and tracking how it changes."},{"beat":"The two big numbers","text":"On one side is the obligation — the projected benefit obligation, or PBO — the estimated total owed to retirees, expressed in today's dollars. On the other side are the plan assets — money the company set aside in a fund and invests to cover the promise. Compare the two and you get the funded status: assets minus obligation. If the fund falls short of the promise, that shortfall is a liability on the balance sheet."},{"beat":"Why the obligation grows each year","text":"Two forces swell the PBO annually. Service cost is the fresh chunk of pension employees earned just by working another year. Interest cost is the obligation getting bigger simply because the payout is now one year closer — the discount unwinds. Meanwhile, the plan assets are expected to grow by their investment return, which works in the opposite direction."},{"beat":"What pension expense really is","text":"Put those forces together and pension expense is essentially 'how much did the gap between the promise and the savings widen this year.' Service cost and interest cost push it up; the expected return on plan assets pulls it down; and amortized items like prior service cost adjust it. The mnemonic SIR-AG lists the pieces, but the underlying idea is just the net change in that gap."}],"advanced":{"intro":"The full pension worksheet: reconciling the PBO and plan assets through every moving part, including actuarial gains/losses and the actual-vs-expected return interplay.","lesson":[{"h":"Reconciling the PBO roll-forward","p":["The PBO moves each year through a predictable roll-forward: beginning PBO + service cost + interest cost ± actuarial gains/losses − benefits paid = ending PBO. Each piece has a distinct cause.","Example: beginning PBO $600,000 + service cost $70,000 + interest cost ($600,000 × 6% = $36,000) + actuarial loss $15,000 − benefits paid $40,000 = ending PBO of $681,000. An actuarial loss (e.g., from raising life-expectancy assumptions) increases the obligation."]},{"h":"The plan-assets roll-forward and net funded status","p":["Plan assets roll forward separately: beginning assets + actual return + contributions − benefits paid = ending assets. Note this uses the ACTUAL return, even though pension expense used the expected return — the gap becomes a deferred gain/loss.","Example: beginning assets $500,000 + actual return $45,000 + contributions $60,000 − benefits paid $40,000 = $565,000. Ending funded status = $565,000 − $681,000 = −$116,000, a net pension liability. Tracking both roll-forwards is how you reconcile the entire pension footnote."]}],"bank":[{"d":"hard","q":"Beginning PBO $600,000; service cost $70,000; discount rate 6%; actuarial LOSS $15,000; benefits paid $40,000. Ending PBO is:","choices":["$681,000","$666,000","$651,000","$721,000"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: 600,000 + 70,000 + (600,000 × 6% = 36,000) + 15,000 actuarial loss − 40,000 benefits = $681,000.","$666,000 omits the actuarial loss.","$651,000 omits both the actuarial loss and miscounts.","$721,000 fails to subtract benefits paid."]},{"d":"hard","q":"The plan-assets roll-forward uses which return figure?","choices":["The ACTUAL return on plan assets","The expected return","The discount rate","Zero"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: plan assets roll forward using the ACTUAL return (plus contributions, minus benefits); expected return is only used in computing pension expense.","Expected return is for the expense calculation, not the asset roll-forward.","The discount rate applies to the PBO, not plan assets.","Assets do earn a return."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Ending plan assets are $565,000 and the ending PBO is $681,000. The balance sheet shows:","choices":["A net pension liability of $116,000","A net pension asset of $116,000","Nothing","A liability of $681,000"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: funded status = 565,000 − 681,000 = −$116,000 → a net pension liability of $116,000.","Assets are below the PBO, so it's a liability, not an asset.","Funded status is recognized on the balance sheet.","Only the $116,000 shortfall is the net liability, not the gross PBO."]}]}},{"id":"equity","name":"Stockholders' Equity","icon":"🏛️","group":"Equity","overview":"How capital flows into and out of a corporation — issuance, treasury stock, dividends, and splits — and why par value is mostly a legal artifact.","lesson":[{"h":"The architecture of equity","p":["Stockholders' equity has two big buckets. CONTRIBUTED CAPITAL is what owners paid in: common stock and preferred stock (recorded at par or stated value) plus Additional Paid-In Capital (APIC, the excess over par). EARNED CAPITAL is retained earnings — cumulative profits not yet distributed.","Treasury stock (shares the company bought back) is a CONTRA-equity account that reduces total equity. So: Equity = contributed capital + retained earnings − treasury stock ± accumulated OCI."]},{"h":"Issuing stock and the role of par","p":["Par value is a legal minimum, largely a historical artifact, but it drives the bookkeeping split. When stock is issued, par goes to the stock account and everything above par goes to APIC.","Issue 10,000 shares of $2 par at $18: Cash 180,000 (Dr); Common Stock 20,000 (Cr, = 10,000 × $2 par); APIC 160,000 (Cr, the rest). The total credited to equity is the full $180,000 — par just determines how it's labeled."]},{"h":"Treasury stock (cost method)","p":["Buying back shares: Dr Treasury Stock at cost, Cr Cash. This reduces equity — the company is returning capital to shareholders, not earning income (a company can never report a gain or loss on its own stock through the income statement).","Reissuing treasury stock ABOVE cost: the excess credits APIC–Treasury Stock. Reissuing BELOW cost: the shortfall first reduces any APIC–Treasury balance, then reduces Retained Earnings. Gains and losses on treasury never touch net income."]},{"h":"Dividends and splits","p":["Cash dividends have three dates. DECLARATION: Dr Retained Earnings, Cr Dividends Payable (the liability is now legally owed). RECORD date: no entry. PAYMENT: Dr Dividends Payable, Cr Cash.","Stock dividends distribute additional shares instead of cash, transferring value from retained earnings to contributed capital (small stock dividends, <20–25%, are recorded at fair value). A stock SPLIT (e.g., 2-for-1) doubles shares and halves par with NO journal entry — only a memo — because no value moves; the pie is just cut into more slices.","Cumulative preferred: any unpaid (in-arrears) preferred dividends accumulate and must be paid, along with the current preferred dividend, before common shareholders receive anything."]},{"h":"Stock dividends in detail: small vs large","p":["A small stock dividend (under 20–25%) is capitalized at fair value. Example: a 10% stock dividend on 100,000 shares = 10,000 new shares; at a $15 market price you reduce Retained Earnings by $150,000, increase Common Stock by par (10,000 × $1 = $10,000), and the $140,000 remainder goes to additional paid-in capital.","A large stock dividend (over 20–25%) is capitalized at par value instead, because issuing that many shares is expected to drop the market price materially. Either way, total equity is unchanged — value just shifts from retained earnings into contributed capital."]},{"h":"Property dividends and liquidating dividends","p":["A property dividend distributes a non-cash asset. First you remeasure the asset to fair value (recognizing a gain or loss), then record the dividend at that fair value. Example: distributing an investment with a $40,000 book value but $60,000 fair value triggers a $20,000 gain, and the dividend is recorded at $60,000.","A liquidating dividend is a return of contributed capital rather than a distribution of earnings — it happens when dividends exceed retained earnings. The excess reduces additional paid-in capital, signaling shareholders are getting their investment back, not profits."]}],"keyFormulas":["Issuance: Cash = shares × price; Stock = shares × par; APIC = the excess","Equity = contributed capital + retained earnings − treasury stock ± accumulated OCI","Treasury reissued below cost: reduce APIC–Treasury first, then Retained Earnings","Stock split: no entry (memo only); cumulative preferred gets arrears + current before common"],"bank":[{"d":"easy","q":"Stock with a $1 par is issued at $30 per share. Per share, the amount credited to APIC is:","choices":["$30","$1","$29","$0"],"answer":2,"why":["$30 is the total proceeds, not the APIC portion.","$1 goes to the Common Stock account (par).","Correct: APIC = price − par = 30 − 1 = $29 per share.","There is APIC because the price exceeds par."]},{"d":"easy","q":"Treasury stock is reported in the financial statements as:","choices":["An asset","A contra-equity account reducing total equity","A liability","Revenue"],"answer":1,"why":["A company's own repurchased shares are never an asset.","Correct: treasury stock is contra-equity — it reduces total stockholders' equity.","It is not a liability.","Buying back stock never produces revenue."]},{"d":"easy","q":"A 3-for-1 stock split requires what journal entry?","choices":["Dr Retained Earnings, Cr Common Stock","Dr Common Stock, Cr APIC","No journal entry — a memo only","Dr Cash, Cr Common Stock"],"answer":2,"why":["That describes a stock dividend, not a split.","A split does not reclassify between equity accounts.","Correct: a split changes share count and par per share but moves no dollars, so only a memorandum entry is made.","No cash is received in a split."]},{"d":"medium","q":"On the DECLARATION date of a cash dividend, the entry is:","choices":["Dr Dividends Payable, Cr Cash","Dr Retained Earnings, Cr Dividends Payable","No entry","Dr Cash, Cr Retained Earnings"],"answer":1,"why":["That is the PAYMENT-date entry.","Correct: declaration creates the legal obligation — Dr Retained Earnings (reduces equity), Cr Dividends Payable (a liability).","An entry is required at declaration because a liability arises.","Cash is not received; it will be paid out."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Treasury stock costing $50 per share is reissued at $60. The $10 per-share excess is:","choices":["A gain on the income statement","Credited to APIC–Treasury Stock","Credited to Retained Earnings","Ignored"],"answer":1,"why":["A company never reports gains on its own stock in income.","Correct: reissuing above cost credits APIC–Treasury Stock, an equity account.","Retained Earnings is only affected when reissuing BELOW cost and APIC–Treasury is insufficient.","It is recorded, in equity."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Cumulative preferred stock has $30,000 of dividends in arrears plus a $20,000 current-year preferred dividend. Before common shareholders receive any dividend, the company must pay:","choices":["$20,000","$30,000","$50,000","$10,000"],"answer":2,"why":["$20,000 ignores the arrears.","$30,000 ignores the current preferred dividend.","Correct: cumulative preferred requires arrears ($30,000) plus the current preferred dividend ($20,000) = $50,000 before any common dividend.","$10,000 is unrelated to the figures."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Treasury stock costing $40/share is reissued at $35/share, and there is NO existing APIC–Treasury balance. The $5 per-share shortfall is:","choices":["A loss on the income statement","Debited to Retained Earnings","Credited to APIC","Added to treasury stock"],"answer":1,"why":["Losses on treasury never hit the income statement.","Correct: with no APIC–Treasury to absorb it, the $5 shortfall reduces Retained Earnings directly.","It is a shortfall, not a credit to APIC.","Treasury is removed at cost when reissued, not increased."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A small stock dividend (10%) is declared when the market price is $25 and par is $1. Per share distributed, Retained Earnings is reduced by:","choices":["$1 (par)","$25 (fair value)","$24","$0"],"answer":1,"why":["Par is the amount for a LARGE stock dividend, not a small one.","Correct: small stock dividends (<20–25%) are capitalized at FAIR VALUE, so Retained Earnings is reduced by $25 per share (with $1 to Common Stock and $24 to APIC).","$24 is the APIC portion, not the total RE reduction.","Retained Earnings is reduced for a stock dividend."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Which transaction has NO effect on total stockholders' equity?","choices":["Issuing common stock for cash","Declaring a cash dividend","A 2-for-1 stock split","Buying treasury stock"],"answer":2,"why":["Issuing stock increases equity.","Declaring a cash dividend reduces equity (retained earnings).","Correct: a stock split changes only share count and par per share; total equity is unchanged.","Buying treasury stock reduces equity."]},{"d":"easy","q":"Retained earnings is best described as:","choices":["Cash held by the company","Cumulative net income not distributed as dividends","Amounts paid in by shareholders","A type of treasury stock"],"answer":1,"why":["Retained earnings is not a cash account; it's an equity claim.","Correct: retained earnings is cumulative profits earned over time and not paid out as dividends.","That describes contributed capital, not retained earnings.","It is unrelated to treasury stock."]},{"d":"medium","q":"A LARGE stock dividend (e.g., 30%) is capitalized from retained earnings at:","choices":["Fair (market) value","Par or stated value","Zero","Twice par"],"answer":1,"why":["Fair value is used for SMALL stock dividends (<20–25%).","Correct: large stock dividends are recorded at par/stated value (they resemble a split), transferring less from retained earnings than a small one.","A value is transferred; it is not zero.","There is no 'twice par' rule."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Declaring and paying a cash dividend has what effect on total stockholders' equity?","choices":["Increases it","Decreases it","No effect","Converts it to debt"],"answer":1,"why":["Paying assets out cannot increase equity.","Correct: a cash dividend reduces retained earnings and ultimately pays out assets, decreasing total equity.","Unlike a stock dividend, a cash dividend does reduce equity.","Dividends do not create debt (beyond the brief payable at declaration)."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A company reissues treasury stock (cost $25/share) at $18/share. There is a $4,000 balance in APIC–Treasury Stock. The $7/share shortfall is absorbed by:","choices":["Reducing APIC–Treasury first, then Retained Earnings","Reducing Retained Earnings only","An income-statement loss","Increasing Common Stock"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: reissuing below cost first reduces any APIC–Treasury balance, then charges the remainder to Retained Earnings — never the income statement.","APIC–Treasury is used first, before Retained Earnings.","Losses on treasury stock never hit the income statement.","Common Stock is unaffected by treasury reissuance."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A 10% stock dividend is declared on 100,000 shares ($1 par) when the market price is $15. Retained earnings is reduced by:","choices":["$150,000","$10,000","$140,000","$15,000"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: a small stock dividend is capitalized at fair value — 10,000 new shares × $15 = $150,000 out of retained earnings.","$10,000 is the par portion to Common Stock.","$140,000 is the APIC portion.","$15,000 uses only one share's price."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A property dividend distributes an investment with a $40,000 book value and $60,000 fair value. Before recording the dividend, the company:","choices":["Recognizes a $20,000 gain and records the dividend at $60,000","Records the dividend at $40,000 book value","Recognizes a $20,000 loss","Records no gain or loss"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: the asset is remeasured to fair value first (a $20,000 gain), then the dividend is recorded at the $60,000 fair value.","It's recorded at fair value, not book.","Fair value exceeds book, so it's a gain.","Remeasurement to fair value does produce a gain here."]},{"d":"medium","q":"A liquidating dividend (dividends exceeding retained earnings) represents:","choices":["A return of contributed capital, reducing additional paid-in capital","Extra profit distribution","An expense","An increase in retained earnings"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: the portion beyond retained earnings is a return of capital and reduces APIC.","It's specifically not from earnings — that's the point.","Dividends are never an expense.","It reduces equity, it doesn't raise retained earnings."]}],"worked":{"title":"Issuing stock above par, then buying back treasury shares","setup":"A company issues 10,000 shares of common stock. Each share has a $2 par value (a legal minimum printed on the share), and investors pay $18 per share. Later, the company buys back 1,000 of those shares from the market at $20 each. We need the journal entries.","steps":[{"do":"Compute total cash from the issuance: 10,000 shares × $18 = $180,000.","why":"This is simply what investors handed over. All $180,000 increases stockholders' equity — par value only affects how we LABEL the pieces, not the total."},{"do":"Split the proceeds. Par portion: 10,000 × $2 = $20,000 goes to Common Stock. The rest, $180,000 − $20,000 = $160,000, goes to Additional Paid-In Capital (APIC).","why":"Accounting rules require the stock account to hold only the par amount; everything paid above par is 'extra' contributed capital, parked in APIC. Par is mostly a legal formality, but it drives this split."},{"do":"Record the issuance. Dr Cash $180,000; Cr Common Stock $20,000; Cr APIC $160,000.","why":"Cash (an asset) goes up by the full amount received. The two credits together ($20,000 + $160,000) equal $180,000, so equity rises by the full proceeds and the entry balances."},{"do":"Now the buyback: 1,000 shares × $20 = $20,000 paid.","why":"The company is repurchasing its own shares. This is NOT an investment and NOT an expense — it's returning capital to shareholders, which reduces equity."},{"do":"Record the treasury purchase (cost method). Dr Treasury Stock $20,000; Cr Cash $20,000.","why":"Treasury Stock is a contra-equity account — it's recorded at the $20,000 cost paid and reduces total stockholders' equity. Notice we do NOT touch the original Common Stock or APIC accounts, and there's no gain or loss: a company can never make a profit trading in its own shares."},{"do":"See the equity effect: issuance raised equity by $180,000; the buyback lowered it by $20,000; net equity from these events = $160,000.","why":"Buying treasury stock parks $20,000 of value in a contra account that subtracts from equity. The shares still legally exist but are no longer 'outstanding' in the hands of investors."}],"takeaway":"When stock is issued above par, par goes to Common Stock and the excess to APIC — but the total equity increase is just the cash received. Buying treasury stock (cost method) records the full cost in a contra-equity account that reduces equity, with no gain or loss ever flowing through income."},"worked2":{"title":"Recording a small stock dividend (capitalized at fair value)","setup":"A company with 50,000 common shares outstanding ($1 par) declares a 10% stock dividend when the market price is $20 per share. Because it's small (under 20–25%), it's recorded at fair value. We'll see how value shifts WITHIN equity.","steps":[{"do":"Compute shares issued: 50,000 × 10% = 5,000 new shares.","why":"A 10% stock dividend gives each holder 1 new share for every 10 owned. No cash leaves the company — shareholders just get more paper."},{"do":"Because it's a SMALL stock dividend, capitalize it at FAIR VALUE: 5,000 × $20 market price = $100,000 transferred out of Retained Earnings.","why":"Accounting rules treat small stock dividends (<20–25%) as if shares were issued at market value, so Retained Earnings is reduced by the full fair value of the new shares. (Large stock dividends use par instead.)"},{"do":"Split the $100,000 into par and excess. Common Stock = 5,000 × $1 par = $5,000. APIC = 100,000 − 5,000 = $95,000.","why":"Just like a regular issuance, par goes to Common Stock and the rest to APIC — but no cash is involved; the value comes out of Retained Earnings instead."},{"do":"Record it. Dr Retained Earnings 100,000; Cr Common Stock 5,000; Cr APIC 95,000.","why":"Notice TOTAL equity is unchanged — $100,000 just moved from earned capital (Retained Earnings) to contributed capital (Common Stock + APIC). The company is no richer or poorer; it has reclassified equity."},{"do":"Confirm the effect: total stockholders' equity is exactly the same before and after.","why":"A stock dividend never changes total equity or company value — it just divides ownership into more shares. This is why it differs from a cash dividend, which actually reduces equity by paying assets out."}],"takeaway":"A small stock dividend (<20–25%) is capitalized at fair value: Retained Earnings drops by market value, with par going to Common Stock and the rest to APIC. Total equity is unchanged — value simply shifts from earned to contributed capital."},"practice":[{"prompt":"A company issues 20,000 shares of common stock with a $1 par value at a price of $25 per share. Give the journal entry.","solution":["Cash received = 20,000 × $25 = $500,000.","Common Stock (par) = 20,000 × $1 = $20,000. APIC = 500,000 − 20,000 = $480,000.","Entry: Dr Cash 500,000 / Cr Common Stock 20,000 / Cr APIC 480,000."]},{"prompt":"A company buys 2,000 treasury shares at $30 each, then later reissues all 2,000 at $34 each. Give both journal entries (cost method).","solution":["Buyback: cost = 2,000 × $30 = $60,000. Entry: Dr Treasury Stock 60,000 / Cr Cash 60,000.","Reissue above cost: proceeds = 2,000 × $34 = $68,000. The $8,000 excess over cost goes to APIC–Treasury Stock (never to income).","Entry: Dr Cash 68,000 / Cr Treasury Stock 60,000 / Cr APIC–Treasury Stock 8,000."]},{"prompt":"A company has cumulative preferred stock with $25,000 of dividends in arrears and a $15,000 current-year preferred dividend requirement. How much must be paid to preferred shareholders before common shareholders can receive any dividend?","solution":["Cumulative preferred requires ALL unpaid (arrears) dividends plus the current-year preferred dividend before any common dividend.","Total to preferred first = arrears 25,000 + current 15,000 = $40,000.","Only after paying that $40,000 can common shareholders receive anything."]}],"entries":{"w1":[{"caption":"Issue 10,000 shares of $2 par common stock at $18","lines":[{"account":"Cash","dr":180000},{"account":"Common Stock","cr":20000},{"account":"Additional Paid-In Capital","cr":160000}]},{"caption":"Buy back 1,000 shares as treasury stock at $20 (cost method)","lines":[{"account":"Treasury Stock","dr":20000},{"account":"Cash","cr":20000}]}],"w2":[{"caption":"Declare and distribute a small (10%) stock dividend, capitalized at fair value","lines":[{"account":"Retained Earnings","dr":100000},{"account":"Common Stock","cr":5000},{"account":"Additional Paid-In Capital","cr":95000}]}]},"comptable":null,"aids":{"mnemonics":[{"tag":"Issuance split","text":"\"Par to stock, rest to APIC.\" Always: par value → Common/Preferred Stock; everything above par → Additional Paid-In Capital."},{"tag":"Stock dividend size","text":"\"Small = market, Large = par.\" Small stock dividends (<20–25%) are capitalized at fair value; large ones at par."},{"tag":"Treasury reissue below cost","text":"\"APIC-Treasury first, then RE.\" A reissue below cost drains APIC–Treasury before touching Retained Earnings — never the income statement."}],"watch":["A company NEVER reports a gain or loss on its own stock through the income statement — treasury differences go through equity (APIC–Treasury, then RE).","A stock SPLIT has NO journal entry (memo only); a stock DIVIDEND does. Don't confuse them.","Cumulative preferred must receive arrears PLUS the current preferred dividend before common gets anything.","Declaring a CASH dividend reduces equity; a STOCK dividend or split does not change total equity at all."],"cheat":"Two questions answer almost every equity problem: (1) Did cash/value actually LEAVE the company? (cash dividend = yes, stock dividend/split = no) and (2) Is this the company trading its OWN stock? (if yes, gains/losses can't touch income — they live in equity). Par value is just a labeling rule, never a value."},"plain":{"plain":"Equity is the owners' slice of the company — what's left after debts. It comes from two places: money owners put in (by buying stock) and profits the company kept instead of paying out. When a company sells stock, the cash comes in and gets sorted into a couple of labeled buckets. When it buys its own stock back, that's just returning money to owners — never a profit or loss for the company. Dividends are the company handing profit back to shareholders: cash dividends actually shrink the company's worth, while stock dividends and splits just cut the same pie into more slices without changing its size.","terms":[{"term":"Par value","plain":"a tiny legal sticker price on a share — mostly a formality"},{"term":"Additional paid-in capital (APIC)","plain":"everything investors paid above that token par value"},{"term":"Retained earnings","plain":"profits the company kept rather than paying out"},{"term":"Treasury stock","plain":"shares the company bought back from the public"},{"term":"Cumulative preferred","plain":"preferred stock that must be fully caught up before common gets a dime"}]},"lecture":[{"beat":"Whose money is this?","text":"Equity is the owners' leftover claim — everything the company has minus everything it owes. It builds up from two sources: capital the owners put in by buying stock, and profits the company earned and chose to keep rather than pay out. Almost every equity transaction is just money flowing into, around, or out of those two buckets."},{"beat":"Putting money in","text":"When a company issues stock, cash comes in and gets sorted. A tiny 'par value' — really just a legal formality — goes into the common stock account, and everything paid above that goes into additional paid-in capital. The split is pure bookkeeping; what matters is that total equity rises by the full cash received."},{"beat":"The company trading its own stock","text":"When a company buys back its own shares — treasury stock — it's not investing and not earning; it's simply returning capital to shareholders, so equity shrinks. And here's a rule worth burning in: a company can never report a profit or loss on dealing in its own stock. Any difference when it resells treasury shares flows through equity accounts, never the income statement. A business can't make earnings by trading itself."},{"beat":"Handing money back","text":"Dividends return profit to shareholders. A cash dividend actually reduces the company's worth — assets leave. But a stock dividend or a stock split hands out more shares without any cash moving; the pie is simply cut into more slices, so total equity doesn't change at all. And if there's cumulative preferred stock, those holders must be made fully whole — including any skipped past dividends — before common shareholders see a cent."}]},{"id":"revenue","name":"Revenue Recognition (ASC 606)","icon":"💳","group":"Income","overview":"The five-step model that replaced industry-specific rules with one principle: recognize revenue when you transfer control of what you promised.","lesson":[{"h":"Why one model replaced hundreds of rules","p":["Before ASC 606, revenue rules were a patchwork of industry-specific guidance that produced inconsistent results for economically similar transactions. ASC 606 (and its IFRS twin, IFRS 15) replaced all of it with a single principle-based model built on one idea: recognize revenue to depict the transfer of promised goods or services in the amount you expect to be entitled to.","The mechanism is five steps. The hardest judgment usually lives in steps 2 (what did I actually promise?) and 5 (when does control transfer?)."]},{"h":"Steps 1–3: contract, obligations, price","p":["STEP 1 — Identify the contract: an agreement with commercial substance, approved by both parties, with identifiable rights and payment terms, where collection is probable.","STEP 2 — Identify the performance obligations: each DISTINCT good or service you promised. 'Distinct' means the customer can benefit from it on its own and it's separately identifiable. A software license plus separate support are typically two obligations; installation that's integral to the product may be bundled into one.","STEP 3 — Determine the transaction price: the amount you expect to receive, including estimates of VARIABLE consideration (discounts, rebates, performance bonuses) — but variable amounts are included only to the extent it's highly probable a significant reversal won't later occur (the 'constraint')."]},{"h":"Steps 4–5: allocate and recognize","p":["STEP 4 — Allocate the transaction price to each performance obligation based on their relative STANDALONE selling prices (what you'd charge for each separately).","STEP 5 — Recognize revenue when (or as) each obligation is satisfied, i.e., when CONTROL transfers to the customer. This can be at a POINT IN TIME (most goods — control passes on delivery) or OVER TIME.","Recognize OVER TIME if any one holds: (a) the customer simultaneously receives and consumes the benefits as you perform (e.g., a cleaning service), (b) your work creates or enhances an asset the customer controls, or (c) the asset has no alternative use to you AND you have an enforceable right to payment for work completed to date."]},{"h":"Worked allocation","p":["A vendor sells a software license bundled with one year of support for $120,000. Standalone, the license sells for $100,000 and support for $50,000.","Two performance obligations. Total standalone = 150,000. License allocation = 120,000 × (100,000/150,000) = $80,000, recognized at the point the license transfers. Support allocation = 120,000 × (50,000/150,000) = $40,000, recognized over time (straight-line ≈ $3,333/month).","Notice the customer pays $120,000 but the standalone values total $150,000 — the bundle discount is spread proportionally across both obligations via relative standalone pricing."]},{"h":"Long-term contracts: recognizing revenue over time","p":["For long contracts (construction, custom projects), revenue is often recognized over time as the work progresses, commonly measured by the percentage-of-completion: costs incurred to date ÷ total estimated costs.","Example: a $1,000,000 contract with $300,000 of costs incurred against $750,000 total estimated costs is 40% complete. You recognize 40% × $1,000,000 = $400,000 of revenue and $300,000 of cost, for $100,000 gross profit to date.","If a contract doesn't qualify for over-time recognition, you wait and recognize everything at completion. And if total costs are ever expected to exceed the contract price, the entire expected loss is recognized immediately."]},{"h":"Variable consideration and rights of return","p":["When a price includes variable amounts — discounts, rebates, performance bonuses — you estimate it but only include the amount for which a significant reversal is highly improbable (the 'constraint'). This keeps companies from booking optimistic revenue that later evaporates.","A right of return is a form of variable consideration: if you sell $100,000 of goods and expect 5% returns, you recognize $95,000 of revenue, a refund liability of $5,000, and an asset for the inventory you expect back."]}],"keyFormulas":["5 steps: contract → performance obligations → transaction price → allocate → recognize","Allocation = total price × (obligation's standalone price ÷ sum of standalone prices)","Recognize when CONTROL transfers (point in time) or AS it transfers (over time)","Variable consideration included only if a significant reversal is highly improbable"],"bank":[{"d":"easy","q":"The first step of the ASC 606 model is to:","choices":["Determine the transaction price","Identify the contract with the customer","Recognize revenue","Allocate the price"],"answer":1,"why":["Price comes in step 3.","Correct: step 1 is identifying the contract; obligations, price, allocation, and recognition follow.","Recognition is the final step (5).","Allocation is step 4."]},{"d":"easy","q":"Revenue is recognized when:","choices":["Cash is collected","The contract is signed","Control of the good or service transfers to the customer","The invoice is mailed"],"answer":2,"why":["Cash timing does not drive recognition.","Signing creates the contract but does not by itself transfer control.","Correct: recognition tracks the transfer of CONTROL to the customer.","Invoicing is an administrative act, not the recognition trigger."]},{"d":"easy","q":"The transaction price is allocated to performance obligations based on:","choices":["An equal split","Relative standalone selling prices","The order of delivery","The cost of each obligation"],"answer":1,"why":["Equal splitting ignores differing values.","Correct: allocation uses each obligation's relative standalone selling price.","Delivery order does not determine allocation.","Cost is not the allocation basis; standalone selling price is."]},{"d":"medium","q":"A performance obligation is 'distinct' if:","choices":["It is the most expensive item","The customer can benefit from it on its own and it is separately identifiable","It is delivered first","It exceeds $10,000"],"answer":1,"why":["Price size does not define distinctness.","Correct: distinct requires that the customer can benefit from the good/service on its own (or with readily available resources) AND it is separately identifiable in the contract.","Delivery order is irrelevant.","There is no dollar threshold for distinctness."]},{"d":"medium","q":"A contract bundles a product ($800 standalone) and a service ($200 standalone) for $900 total. Revenue allocated to the service is:","choices":["$200","$180","$100","$225"],"answer":1,"why":["$200 is the standalone price, not the allocated amount of the discounted bundle.","Correct: 900 × (200/1,000) = $180.","$100 uses the wrong ratio.","$225 over-allocates to the service."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Variable consideration (e.g., a performance bonus) is included in the transaction price only to the extent that:","choices":["The customer requests it","It is highly probable a significant reversal will NOT occur","It exceeds 50% of the contract","It has been invoiced"],"answer":1,"why":["The customer's request is not the test.","Correct: the 'constraint' includes variable consideration only when a significant revenue reversal is highly improbable.","There is no 50% rule.","Invoicing is not the criterion."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A construction firm builds a custom asset with no alternative use and has an enforceable right to payment for work done to date. Revenue should be recognized:","choices":["At completion only","Over time as the asset is built","When cash is received","When the contract is signed"],"answer":1,"why":["Point-in-time (completion) does not apply when the over-time criteria are met.","Correct: no alternative use + enforceable right to payment for progress satisfies the over-time criterion → recognize over time (e.g., by cost-to-cost percentage of completion).","Cash receipt does not drive recognition.","Signing only establishes the contract."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Under the cost-to-cost method of over-time recognition, percent complete is measured as:","choices":["Cash collected ÷ contract price","Costs incurred to date ÷ total estimated costs","Time elapsed ÷ total time","Billings ÷ contract price"],"answer":1,"why":["Cash collected measures financing, not progress.","Correct: cost-to-cost uses costs incurred to date ÷ total estimated costs as the input measure of progress.","Time elapsed is a different (output/time-based) measure, not cost-to-cost.","Billings reflect invoicing, not performance."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A company receives $12,000 upfront on Oct 1 for a one-year service. By Dec 31, the amount recognized as revenue and the amount still a liability are:","choices":["$12,000 revenue; $0 liability","$3,000 revenue; $9,000 liability","$9,000 revenue; $3,000 liability","$0 revenue; $12,000 liability"],"answer":1,"why":["Only the portion earned can be recognized.","Correct: 3 of 12 months performed → 12,000 × 3/12 = $3,000 revenue; the remaining $9,000 is unearned (a contract liability).","This reverses the earned and unearned amounts.","Some revenue is earned by year-end, so it is not zero."]},{"d":"easy","q":"A 'performance obligation' is:","choices":["A promise to transfer a distinct good or service to the customer","The total contract price","A penalty for late delivery","The customer's obligation to pay"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: a performance obligation is each distinct promised good or service the seller must transfer.","That's the transaction price, a different step.","A penalty is variable consideration, not an obligation.","It refers to the SELLER's promise, not the customer's payment duty."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Revenue from a one-year gym membership paid upfront is recognized:","choices":["All at the start when cash is received","Evenly over the year as the service is provided","All at year-end","Only when the member attends"],"answer":1,"why":["Cash receipt doesn't trigger recognition.","Correct: the customer consumes the benefit over time, so revenue is recognized ratably over the membership year.","Recognition tracks performance throughout, not just at the end.","It's based on the passage of the service period, not individual visits."]},{"d":"medium","q":"When control of a good transfers at a single moment (e.g., a retail sale), revenue is recognized:","choices":["Over time","At that point in time","Only after the return period","When the supplier is paid"],"answer":1,"why":["Over-time applies when benefits transfer continuously.","Correct: when control passes at one moment (delivery at the register), revenue is recognized at that point in time.","Return rights affect variable consideration estimates, not the basic timing.","Paying the supplier is unrelated to revenue recognition."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A software vendor sells a license plus a year of updates that are highly integrated and not separately usable. These should likely be treated as:","choices":["Two separate performance obligations","A single combined performance obligation","Not a contract","Variable consideration"],"answer":1,"why":["If not separately usable, they aren't distinct, so not two obligations.","Correct: when goods/services are highly interdependent and not distinct on their own, they're combined into a single performance obligation.","A valid contract still exists.","This is about identifying obligations, not variable pricing."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A $1,000,000 contract has $300,000 of costs incurred against $750,000 total estimated costs. Under percentage-of-completion, revenue recognized to date is:","choices":["$400,000","$300,000","$1,000,000","$250,000"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: 300,000 ÷ 750,000 = 40% complete; 40% × $1,000,000 = $400,000 revenue.","$300,000 is cost, not revenue.","Full revenue is only at completion.","$250,000 uses the wrong ratio."]},{"d":"medium","q":"When a contract is expected to result in an overall LOSS, the loss is:","choices":["Recognized immediately, in full","Spread over the remaining term","Ignored until completion","Recognized only if over 10% of the contract"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: expected contract losses are recognized immediately and in full, regardless of completion stage — a conservatism rule.","Losses aren't spread; they're taken at once.","They can't wait until completion.","There's no percentage threshold."]},{"d":"medium","q":"You sell $100,000 of goods and expect 5% to be returned. Revenue recognized is:","choices":["$95,000, with a $5,000 refund liability","$100,000","$5,000","$90,000"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: estimated returns are constrained out — recognize $95,000 revenue and a $5,000 refund liability.","$100,000 ignores expected returns.","$5,000 is the refund liability, not revenue.","$90,000 over-reserves."]}],"worked":{"title":"Allocating a bundled contract under the 5-step model","setup":"A software company signs a $120,000 contract that bundles two things: a perpetual software license and one year of technical support. If sold separately, the license would go for $100,000 and the support for $50,000. We need to determine how much revenue to recognize, and when.","steps":[{"do":"Step 1 — Identify the contract. There's a signed agreement for $120,000 with clear terms and a customer who can pay. A valid contract exists.","why":"ASC 606 only applies once a real contract exists: approved by both sides, with identifiable rights and payment terms, and collection is probable. This one qualifies."},{"do":"Step 2 — Identify the performance obligations. There are TWO: (a) the software license, and (b) the one year of support.","why":"A performance obligation is each DISTINCT promise. The customer can benefit from the license on its own, and support is a separate service — so they're two separate obligations, each earning revenue on its own timeline."},{"do":"Step 3 — Determine the transaction price: $120,000.","why":"This is the total the customer agreed to pay. (If there were discounts or bonuses tied to performance, we'd estimate that 'variable consideration' here, but this contract is a fixed $120,000.)"},{"do":"Step 4 — Allocate the $120,000 using relative standalone selling prices. Standalone total = $100,000 + $50,000 = $150,000. License gets 120,000 × (100,000/150,000) = $80,000. Support gets 120,000 × (50,000/150,000) = $40,000.","why":"The customer got a bundle discount ($120,000 for things worth $150,000 separately). We spread that discount proportionally based on each item's standalone value, so each obligation carries its fair share. The two pieces add back to $120,000."},{"do":"Step 5 — Recognize revenue as each obligation is satisfied. License: recognize the full $80,000 when the software is delivered and the customer controls it (a point in time). Support: recognize $40,000 evenly over the year = $40,000 ÷ 12 ≈ $3,333 per month (over time).","why":"Revenue is earned when CONTROL transfers. The license transfers all at once on delivery, so its $80,000 is recognized immediately. Support is consumed continuously over 12 months, so its $40,000 is spread across the year."},{"do":"Record at delivery (assume cash received up front). Dr Cash $120,000; Cr Revenue $80,000 (license); Cr Unearned/Contract Revenue $40,000 (support).","why":"You collected all $120,000, but you've only EARNED the $80,000 license portion so far. The $40,000 of support not yet delivered is a liability (unearned revenue) you'll convert to revenue month by month as you provide support."}],"takeaway":"Walk the five steps: contract → obligations → price → allocate by relative standalone prices → recognize as control transfers. A bundle gets split by each item's standalone value; pieces delivered at a point in time are recognized immediately, while services delivered over time are spread out, with the undelivered portion sitting as unearned revenue."},"worked2":{"title":"Variable consideration and the 'constraint'","setup":"A consulting firm signs a contract for a fixed fee of $100,000, plus a $20,000 bonus if the project finishes early. The firm estimates an 80% chance of earning the bonus. We'll decide how much revenue to include in the transaction price.","steps":[{"do":"Identify the variable consideration: the $20,000 early-completion bonus is uncertain — it depends on a future outcome.","why":"Step 3 of the model (determine the transaction price) requires ESTIMATING variable amounts like bonuses, rebates, and penalties — not just the fixed fee."},{"do":"Apply the 'constraint' test: include variable consideration only to the extent it's HIGHLY PROBABLE that a significant revenue reversal won't later occur.","why":"ASC 606 is conservative about uncertain amounts. You can't book revenue you might have to claw back. 'Highly probable' is a high bar — an 80% chance may NOT clear it, depending on the firm's track record and how binary the outcome is."},{"do":"If the bonus does NOT meet the constraint: transaction price = $100,000 (fixed fee only). The bonus is excluded until it becomes highly probable.","why":"When in doubt, you leave the uncertain amount out. You'd recognize the $20,000 later, once early completion becomes highly probable or actually happens."},{"do":"If the bonus DOES meet the constraint (strong track record, near-certain): transaction price = $120,000, and the bonus is allocated and recognized along with the base fee.","why":"The decision hinges entirely on the constraint judgment. The same 80% estimate could go either way depending on the firm's history of hitting deadlines — this is where professional judgment enters."}],"takeaway":"Variable consideration (bonuses, rebates, penalties) is estimated in step 3, but only INCLUDED to the extent a significant reversal is highly improbable — the 'constraint.' Uncertain amounts are excluded until they clear that bar, keeping revenue from being recognized prematurely."},"practice":[{"prompt":"A contract bundles a product and its installation for a total price of $90,000. Standalone, the product sells for $70,000 and installation for $30,000 (and they are two distinct performance obligations). Allocate the transaction price to each.","solution":["Standalone total = 70,000 + 30,000 = $100,000.","Product allocation = 90,000 × (70,000 / 100,000) = $63,000.","Installation allocation = 90,000 × (30,000 / 100,000) = $27,000.","The two allocated amounts sum back to the $90,000 transaction price; the bundle discount is spread proportionally by relative standalone value."]},{"prompt":"A customer pays $24,000 upfront for a 2-year service contract. After 9 months, how much revenue has been recognized, and how much remains as a contract liability (unearned revenue)?","solution":["The service is delivered evenly over 24 months, so recognize revenue ratably: 24,000 × (9 / 24) = $9,000 recognized.","Unearned (contract liability) remaining = 24,000 − 9,000 = $15,000.","Revenue is recognized AS control transfers over time, not when the cash was collected upfront."]},{"prompt":"A construction contract has a total price of $500,000 and total estimated costs of $400,000. By year-end, $100,000 of costs have been incurred. Using the cost-to-cost method, compute percent complete and revenue recognized this year.","solution":["Percent complete = costs incurred to date ÷ total estimated costs = 100,000 ÷ 400,000 = 25%.","Revenue recognized = total contract price × percent complete = 500,000 × 25% = $125,000.","(Cost-to-cost uses inputs — costs — as the measure of progress toward satisfying the over-time performance obligation.)"]}],"entries":{"w1":[{"caption":"Collect $120,000 cash; recognize the license now, defer the support","lines":[{"account":"Cash","dr":120000},{"account":"Revenue — Software License","cr":80000},{"account":"Contract Liability (Unearned Support)","cr":40000}]},{"caption":"End of each month — recognize one month of support revenue","lines":[{"account":"Contract Liability (Unearned Support)","dr":3333},{"account":"Revenue — Support","cr":3333}]}],"w2":[{"caption":"If the bonus is constrained out — recognize only the fixed fee as earned","lines":[{"account":"Accounts Receivable","dr":100000},{"account":"Service Revenue","cr":100000}]}]},"comptable":null,"aids":{"mnemonics":[{"tag":"The 5 steps — \"ID the POPs, Allocate, Recognize\"","text":"1 Identify the contract · 2 Identify Performance Obligations · 3 determine the Price · 4 Allocate price · 5 Recognize as satisfied. (Contract → Obligations → Price → Allocate → Recognize)."},{"tag":"Timing","text":"\"Control = the trigger.\" Recognize when CONTROL transfers — at a point in time (most goods) or over time (services, custom work)."},{"tag":"Allocation","text":"\"Split by standalone.\" Allocate the bundle price by each item's RELATIVE standalone selling price."}],"watch":["Recognition follows CONTROL, not cash, not invoicing, not contract signing.","Variable consideration (bonuses, rebates) is included only up to the amount where a significant reversal is highly improbable — the 'constraint.'","Allocation uses relative STANDALONE prices, never an equal split and never cost.","Cash collected upfront for undelivered service is a CONTRACT LIABILITY (unearned revenue), not revenue yet."],"cheat":"Strip every revenue problem to one question: 'What did I promise, and has the customer GOT it yet?' Each distinct promise earns its money only as the customer gains control of it. The five steps are just the disciplined way of listing the promises, pricing them, and checking which ones have been delivered."},"plain":{"plain":"Revenue recognition answers one question: when do you get to say 'we earned this money'? The answer isn't 'when cash arrives' — it's 'when we actually gave the customer what we promised.' So you list out each separate thing you promised, figure out the price of each, and count the money only as you hand each piece over. Sell a product and a year of support together? You earn the product part right away, but you earn the support bit by bit across the year. Cash you've collected for stuff you haven't delivered yet isn't earnings — it's a promise you still owe.","terms":[{"term":"Performance obligation","plain":"a separate thing you promised to give the customer"},{"term":"Transaction price","plain":"the total the customer agreed to pay"},{"term":"Control transfers","plain":"the moment the customer actually gets the thing — when you earn the money"},{"term":"Standalone selling price","plain":"what each piece would cost on its own"},{"term":"Contract liability (unearned revenue)","plain":"cash you've taken for something you still owe the customer"}]},"lecture":[{"beat":"The deceptively simple question","text":"Revenue recognition answers one question that sounds obvious but isn't: when do you get to say you earned the money? The wrong answers are 'when the cash arrives' and 'when we signed the contract.' The right answer is 'when we actually delivered what we promised to the customer.' Everything in the five-step model exists to pin down that moment precisely."},{"beat":"Listing and pricing the promises","text":"First you confirm there's a real contract. Then you break it into the distinct promises inside it — sell someone a phone plus a year of service, and that's two separate promises. You figure out the total price, then split that price across the promises based on what each would sell for on its own. Now each promise carries its fair share of the money."},{"beat":"Earning it as control transfers","text":"The payoff step: you recognize revenue for each promise as the customer gains control of it. Some things transfer all at once — hand over the phone, earn that piece immediately. Others transfer gradually — the year of service is earned bit by bit across the year. The trigger is always the transfer of control, not the calendar and not the cash."},{"beat":"What unearned cash really is","text":"This reframes prepayments. If a customer pays you up front for something you haven't delivered yet, that cash isn't revenue — it's a liability, a promise you still owe. You convert it into revenue only as you deliver. So collecting cash early doesn't make you richer on the income statement; it just creates an obligation you work off over time."}]},{"id":"cash-flows","name":"Statement of Cash Flows","icon":"🌊","group":"Income","overview":"How to translate accrual net income back into actual cash, sort every flow into operating/investing/financing, and why the indirect method dominates practice.","lesson":[{"h":"Why a third statement exists","p":["The income statement uses accrual accounting: it records revenue when earned and expenses when incurred, regardless of cash. That's better for measuring performance, but it means net income ≠ cash generated. A profitable company can run out of cash; a cash-rich one can report a loss.","The statement of cash flows bridges that gap, sorting every cash movement into three buckets: OPERATING (the core business), INVESTING (buying/selling long-term assets and investments), and FINANCING (transactions with owners and lenders)."]},{"h":"The three sections","p":["OPERATING: cash effects of the transactions that determine net income — collections from customers, payments to suppliers and employees, interest paid/received, taxes paid.","INVESTING: purchasing or selling PP&E, acquiring or disposing of investments and other businesses, making and collecting loans.","FINANCING: issuing or repurchasing stock, paying dividends, borrowing and repaying debt principal. (Note a U.S. GAAP quirk: interest PAID is operating, but dividends PAID are financing.)"]},{"h":"Indirect method: net income → cash","p":["The indirect method (used by the vast majority of companies) starts with net income and reverses the accruals. Three adjustment types: (1) add back non-cash expenses like depreciation and amortization; (2) remove gains/losses that belong in investing/financing (e.g., subtract a gain on equipment sale, since the whole proceeds go in investing); (3) adjust for changes in working capital.","Working-capital rule (memorize the direction): an INCREASE in a current ASSET uses cash → subtract (e.g., A/R rose = sales not yet collected). An INCREASE in a current LIABILITY provides cash → add (e.g., A/P rose = expenses not yet paid). Decreases flip the sign.","Worked: net income $90,000; depreciation $15,000; A/R increased $8,000; A/P increased $5,000. Operating cash = 90,000 + 15,000 − 8,000 + 5,000 = $102,000."]},{"h":"Direct method: the actual receipts and payments","p":["The direct method lists actual operating cash flows: cash collected from customers, cash paid to suppliers, to employees, for interest, for taxes. It's more intuitive but requires more data, so few companies use it. Both methods produce the IDENTICAL operating cash total — only the presentation differs.","Conversions: Cash from customers = sales − increase in A/R (or + decrease). Cash paid to suppliers = COGS + increase in inventory − increase in A/P (with signs flipping for decreases)."]},{"h":"Handling gains and losses on asset sales","p":["Selling a long-term asset creates a trap in the operating section. The full cash proceeds belong in investing, but any gain or loss already sits inside net income. So under the indirect method you must reverse it: subtract gains and add back losses in operating, so the cash isn't counted twice.","Example: equipment costing $50,000 with $30,000 accumulated depreciation (book value $20,000) sold for $25,000 gives a $5,000 gain. In operating, subtract the $5,000 gain from net income; in investing, show the full $25,000 inflow."]},{"h":"Reconstructing the investing and financing sections","p":["Beyond operating, you build the other two sections by analyzing the changes in long-term accounts. Investing: changes in plant assets and investments (purchases are outflows, sales are inflows). Financing: changes in debt and equity and dividends paid.","Example financing section: issue stock +$100,000, pay dividends −$30,000, repay loan principal −$50,000 → net cash provided by financing = +$20,000. Remember the GAAP quirks: interest paid is operating, but dividends paid are financing."]}],"keyFormulas":["Indirect operating = net income + non-cash expenses ± gains/losses ± working-capital changes","Current asset ↑ → subtract cash; current liability ↑ → add cash","Direct: cash from customers = sales − increase in A/R","Interest paid = operating; dividends paid = financing (U.S. GAAP)"],"bank":[{"d":"easy","q":"Under the indirect method, depreciation expense is:","choices":["Subtracted from net income","Added back to net income","Ignored","Reported as investing"],"answer":1,"why":["Subtracting would double-count the non-cash charge.","Correct: depreciation reduced net income but used no cash, so it is added back.","It must be adjusted, not ignored.","Depreciation itself is not an investing cash flow."]},{"d":"easy","q":"Purchasing equipment for cash is reported in which section?","choices":["Operating","Investing","Financing","Not reported"],"answer":1,"why":["Operating covers the core business cycle, not asset purchases.","Correct: buying long-term productive assets is an investing outflow.","Financing covers owners/lenders, not equipment.","It is a cash flow and must be reported."]},{"d":"easy","q":"Paying a cash dividend to shareholders is classified as:","choices":["Operating","Investing","Financing","A non-cash item"],"answer":2,"why":["Dividends are not part of operating activities.","They are not an investing activity.","Correct: dividends paid are transactions with owners → financing (under U.S. GAAP).","Cash actually leaves the company, so it is not non-cash."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Accounts receivable increased by $12,000 during the year. In the indirect operating section this is:","choices":["Added $12,000","Subtracted $12,000","Ignored","Reported in investing"],"answer":1,"why":["Adding would overstate cash.","Correct: a current-asset increase means revenue was recorded but cash not yet collected → subtract $12,000.","Working-capital changes must be adjusted.","A/R changes are operating, not investing."]},{"d":"medium","q":"A company reports a $5,000 gain on the sale of equipment. In the indirect operating section, the gain is:","choices":["Added to net income","Subtracted from net income","Ignored","Doubled"],"answer":1,"why":["Adding double-counts it, since the full proceeds go to investing.","Correct: the gain is subtracted from net income in operating because the entire sale proceeds (including the gain) are reported in investing.","It must be removed to avoid double counting.","There is no doubling."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Issuing bonds for cash appears in which section, and as an inflow or outflow?","choices":["Operating inflow","Investing outflow","Financing inflow","Financing outflow"],"answer":2,"why":["Borrowing is not operating.","It is not investing.","Correct: issuing debt raises cash from lenders → a financing inflow.","Issuing (not repaying) bonds is an inflow."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Sales were $500,000 and accounts receivable DECREASED by $20,000. Under the direct method, cash collected from customers is:","choices":["$480,000","$520,000","$500,000","$460,000"],"answer":1,"why":["$480,000 would apply if A/R increased.","Correct: a decrease in A/R means you collected more than you sold → 500,000 + 20,000 = $520,000.","$500,000 ignores the A/R change.","$460,000 double-subtracts."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Equipment costing $40,000 with $25,000 accumulated depreciation is sold for $18,000. The investing section reports:","choices":["$18,000 inflow","$15,000 inflow","$3,000 inflow","$40,000 inflow"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: the investing section reports the actual cash proceeds of $18,000, regardless of book value or gain.","$15,000 is the book value, not the cash received.","$3,000 is the gain, not the cash flow.","$40,000 is original cost, not proceeds."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Which of these is a NON-CASH financing/investing activity that appears only in supplemental disclosure, not the main statement?","choices":["Paying cash for equipment","Acquiring land by issuing common stock","Collecting an account receivable","Paying interest"],"answer":1,"why":["Paying cash for equipment is a normal investing outflow.","Correct: acquiring an asset by issuing stock involves no cash, so it is disclosed separately as a non-cash transaction.","Collecting A/R is an operating cash inflow.","Paying interest is an operating cash outflow."]},{"d":"easy","q":"Receiving cash from issuing common stock appears in which section?","choices":["Operating","Investing","Financing","Non-cash"],"answer":2,"why":["Operating relates to the core business cycle.","Investing relates to assets.","Correct: issuing stock raises capital from owners → financing inflow.","Cash is actually received, so it's not a non-cash item."]},{"d":"medium","q":"A DECREASE in accounts payable during the year affects operating cash flow how (indirect method)?","choices":["Add it","Subtract it","No effect","Report in financing"],"answer":1,"why":["Adding would overstate cash.","Correct: a current-liability DECREASE means you paid down what you owed, using cash → subtract from operating cash flow.","Working-capital changes must be adjusted.","A/P changes are operating, not financing."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Under the indirect method, a LOSS on the sale of equipment is:","choices":["Subtracted from net income","Added back to net income","Ignored","Reported as financing"],"answer":1,"why":["Subtracting would double-count it.","Correct: a loss reduced net income but isn't an operating cash item (the sale's cash is in investing), so it's added back in operating.","It must be adjusted for.","The sale proceeds go in investing, but the add-back of the loss is in operating."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Collecting the principal on a loan the company had made to another party is classified as:","choices":["Operating inflow","Investing inflow","Financing inflow","Non-cash"],"answer":1,"why":["Lending and collecting principal isn't part of core operations.","Correct: making and collecting loans to others are investing activities, so collecting the principal is an investing inflow. (Interest received is operating.)","Financing covers the company's OWN borrowing, not loans it made to others.","Cash is received, so it's not non-cash."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Under the DIRECT method, cash paid to suppliers = COGS adjusted for inventory and A/P changes. If COGS is $300,000, inventory rose $15,000, and accounts payable fell $8,000, cash paid to suppliers is:","choices":["$277,000","$323,000","$307,000","$293,000"],"answer":1,"why":["$277,000 reverses both adjustments.","Correct: COGS 300,000 + inventory increase 15,000 (bought more than sold) + A/P decrease 8,000 (paid down what you owed) = $323,000.","$307,000 omits one adjustment.","$293,000 nets the adjustments the wrong way."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Under the DIRECT method, 'cash paid to suppliers' is derived from which income-statement line?","choices":["Sales revenue","Cost of goods sold","Depreciation","Interest expense"],"answer":1,"why":["Sales drives cash from customers, not payments to suppliers.","Correct: cash paid to suppliers starts from COGS, then adjusts for changes in inventory and accounts payable.","Depreciation is non-cash and excluded from the direct method entirely.","Interest is a separate direct-method line."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A key difference between the direct and indirect methods is that the DIRECT method:","choices":["Reports a different operating cash total","Lists actual operating receipts and payments instead of reconciling from net income","Is required by U.S. GAAP","Excludes the investing section"],"answer":1,"why":["Both methods produce the identical operating cash total.","Correct: the direct method lists actual cash receipts/payments (from customers, to suppliers, etc.); the indirect method reconciles from net income.","Neither method is required; the indirect method is most common and a reconciliation is required if direct is used.","The investing and financing sections are identical under both methods."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Under the DIRECT method, depreciation expense appears:","choices":["As a cash outflow","Added to operating cash","Not at all in the operating section","In financing"],"answer":2,"why":["Depreciation is non-cash; it is never a cash flow.","The direct method does not add back non-cash items — that is the indirect method's approach.","Correct: because the direct method lists only actual cash flows, non-cash depreciation simply does not appear in the operating section.","Depreciation is not a financing item."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Equipment (cost $50,000, accumulated depreciation $30,000) is sold for $25,000. Under the indirect method, you:","choices":["Subtract the $5,000 gain in operating and show $25,000 inflow in investing","Add $25,000 to operating","Subtract $20,000 in operating","Show $5,000 in investing"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: book value is $20,000, so the $5,000 gain is removed from operating (it's not operating cash), and the full $25,000 proceeds go to investing.","The proceeds are investing, not operating.","$20,000 is book value, not the adjustment.","The full $25,000 proceeds — not just the gain — go to investing."]},{"d":"medium","q":"A company issues stock for $100,000, pays $30,000 dividends, and repays $50,000 of loan principal. Net cash from financing is:","choices":["$20,000","$80,000","$150,000","$(80,000)"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: +100,000 − 30,000 − 50,000 = +$20,000.","$80,000 omits the dividends.","$150,000 adds the outflows.","The net is positive $20,000."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Under U.S. GAAP, dividends PAID are classified as:","choices":["Financing activities","Operating activities","Investing activities","Not reported"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: dividends paid are financing. (Note the trap: interest paid is operating.)","Interest paid is operating, but dividends paid are financing.","Dividends aren't investing.","They are reported, in financing."]}],"worked":{"title":"Building the operating section using the indirect method","setup":"A company reports net income of $90,000. During the year: depreciation expense was $15,000; accounts receivable increased by $8,000; and accounts payable increased by $5,000. We need cash from operating activities using the indirect method.","steps":[{"do":"Start with net income: $90,000.","why":"The indirect method begins with the accrual-based net income and then 'undoes' the parts of it that didn't involve cash, to arrive at actual operating cash. We're reconciling profit back to cash."},{"do":"Add back depreciation: +$15,000. Running total: $105,000.","why":"Depreciation reduced net income but no cash left the company for it (the cash went out years ago when the asset was bought). It's a non-cash expense, so we add it back to cancel its effect on the cash figure."},{"do":"Adjust for the increase in accounts receivable: −$8,000. Running total: $97,000.","why":"A/R went UP by $8,000, meaning $8,000 of this year's sales were recorded in income but the cash hasn't been collected yet. That revenue is in net income but not in the bank, so we subtract it. Rule: a current asset increase USES cash → subtract."},{"do":"Adjust for the increase in accounts payable: +$5,000. Running total: $102,000.","why":"A/P went UP by $5,000, meaning $5,000 of expenses were recorded but NOT yet paid in cash. Net income was reduced by these expenses, but the cash is still on hand, so we add it back. Rule: a current liability increase PROVIDES cash → add."},{"do":"Conclude: cash from operating activities = $102,000.","why":"After adding back the non-cash depreciation and adjusting for the timing differences in receivables and payables, the company actually generated $102,000 of cash from operations — more than its $90,000 of reported profit, mostly because depreciation isn't a cash drain."}],"takeaway":"The indirect method starts at net income, adds back non-cash expenses like depreciation, then adjusts for working-capital changes. Memorize the direction: an increase in a current asset subtracts cash; an increase in a current liability adds cash. Decreases flip the sign."},"worked2":{"title":"Building the FINANCING section","setup":"During the year a company: issued common stock for $100,000 cash, paid $30,000 in cash dividends, and repaid $50,000 of loan principal. We'll assemble the financing section and note what does NOT belong there.","steps":[{"do":"Issuing stock for cash: +$100,000 financing inflow.","why":"Raising money from owners (issuing shares) is a classic financing activity — cash coming in from the company's capital providers."},{"do":"Paying cash dividends: −$30,000 financing outflow.","why":"Dividends are a distribution to owners, so under U.S. GAAP they're a financing OUTFLOW. (Watch the quirk: dividends PAID are financing, but interest PAID is operating.)"},{"do":"Repaying loan principal: −$50,000 financing outflow.","why":"Repaying the PRINCIPAL of debt is financing (returning borrowed capital to lenders). Note only principal goes here — any interest portion is operating."},{"do":"Net cash from financing = 100,000 − 30,000 − 50,000 = +$20,000.","why":"The three financing flows net to a $20,000 inflow. This section captures all transactions with owners (stock, dividends) and lenders (debt principal)."},{"do":"Flag what does NOT belong: interest paid (operating), buying equipment (investing), and collecting receivables (operating) are all excluded from financing.","why":"A common exam trap is misclassifying interest or asset purchases. Financing is strictly capital transactions with owners and lenders — principal and equity, not the operating costs of using that capital."}],"takeaway":"The financing section captures cash from owners and lenders: issuing or buying back stock, paying dividends, and borrowing or repaying debt principal. Watch the U.S. GAAP quirk — dividends paid are financing, but interest paid is operating."},"practice":[{"prompt":"Using the indirect method, compute cash from operating activities. Net income $120,000; depreciation $20,000; accounts receivable increased $10,000; inventory decreased $6,000; accounts payable decreased $4,000.","solution":["Start with net income: 120,000.","Add depreciation (non-cash): +20,000.","A/R increased 10,000 (current asset up → uses cash): −10,000.","Inventory decreased 6,000 (current asset down → frees cash): +6,000.","A/P decreased 4,000 (current liability down → uses cash): −4,000.","Cash from operations = 120,000 + 20,000 − 10,000 + 6,000 − 4,000 = $132,000."]},{"prompt":"A company sells equipment that cost $60,000 and has $45,000 of accumulated depreciation, for $20,000 cash. (a) Compute the gain or loss. (b) State the amount and section reported on the statement of cash flows.","solution":["(a) Book value = 60,000 − 45,000 = $15,000. Gain = proceeds − book value = 20,000 − 15,000 = $5,000 gain.","(b) The INVESTING section reports the actual cash proceeds of $20,000 (an inflow). Separately, under the indirect method the $5,000 gain is SUBTRACTED from net income in the operating section to avoid double-counting."]},{"prompt":"Sales for the year were $800,000. Accounts receivable increased by $30,000. Using the DIRECT method, compute cash collected from customers.","solution":["Cash from customers = sales − increase in A/R = 800,000 − 30,000 = $770,000.","An increase in A/R means some sales haven't been collected in cash yet, so cash collected is less than sales."]}],"entries":{"w1":[],"w2":[]},"comptable":{"w1":{"title":"Operating activities — indirect method","rows":[["Net income","$90,000"],["Add: depreciation expense","+15,000"],["Less: increase in accounts receivable","(8,000)"],["Add: increase in accounts payable","+5,000"],["Cash provided by operating activities","$102,000"]],"highlightLast":true},"w2":{"title":"Financing activities section","rows":[["Proceeds from issuing common stock","+$100,000"],["Cash dividends paid","(30,000)"],["Repayment of loan principal","(50,000)"],["Net cash provided by financing activities","$20,000"]],"highlightLast":true}},"aids":{"mnemonics":[{"tag":"Working-capital direction — \"CALSAP\"","text":"Current Asset up → subtract (uses cash); Liability up → add (provides cash). \"Assets and cash move OPPOSITE; liabilities and cash move TOGETHER.\""},{"tag":"The three buckets","text":"\"Operating runs it, Investing builds it, Financing funds it.\" Operations = core business; Investing = long-term assets; Financing = owners & lenders."},{"tag":"GAAP quirk","text":"\"Interest is Operating, Dividends paid are Financing.\" A classic trap pairing."}],"watch":["Add back NON-CASH expenses (depreciation, amortization) to net income under the indirect method.","SUBTRACT gains (and add back losses) on asset sales in operating — the full proceeds belong in investing, so leaving the gain in would double-count.","Current asset INCREASE subtracts cash; current liability INCREASE adds cash. Decreases flip the sign.","Interest PAID is operating, but dividends PAID are financing (U.S. GAAP) — and buying equipment is investing, not operating."],"cheat":"The indirect method is one idea: net income is accrual, so 'un-accrue' it back to cash. Anything that hit income but not the bank account gets reversed — non-cash expenses added back, and timing gaps (receivables, payables, inventory) adjusted. If a current asset grew, cash went somewhere other than your pocket, so subtract."},"plain":{"plain":"The income statement can say you made a profit even when your bank account shrank — because it records sales and bills before the cash actually moves. The cash flow statement fixes that by following the real money. It sorts every dollar in or out into three piles: running the business (operating), buying or selling big stuff (investing), and dealing with owners and lenders (financing). The common 'indirect' method starts with profit and then un-does all the non-cash bits to land on the actual cash. The rule of thumb: if something you're owed grew, cash hasn't shown up yet, so subtract it.","terms":[{"term":"Operating activities","plain":"cash from actually running the business day to day"},{"term":"Investing activities","plain":"cash from buying or selling long-term stuff like equipment"},{"term":"Financing activities","plain":"cash from owners and lenders — stock, loans, dividends"},{"term":"Indirect method","plain":"start with profit, then strip out the non-cash parts to find real cash"},{"term":"Non-cash expense","plain":"a cost on paper that didn't actually move any money (like depreciation)"}]},"lecture":[{"beat":"Why a third statement was needed","text":"The income statement has a blind spot. Because it records revenue when earned and expenses when incurred — not when cash moves — a company can report a healthy profit while its bank balance is actually draining away, or post a loss in a year it piled up cash. Profit and cash are not the same thing. The statement of cash flows exists to follow the actual money."},{"beat":"Three buckets","text":"Every dollar in or out lands in one of three piles. Operating is the cash from running the business day to day. Investing is cash from buying or selling long-term things like equipment and investments. Financing is cash exchanged with owners and lenders — issuing stock, paying dividends, borrowing or repaying loans. Sorting flows correctly into these three is half the battle."},{"beat":"The indirect method's logic","text":"Most companies build the operating section with the indirect method, and its whole idea is: net income is an accrual number, so let's un-accrue it back to cash. You start with profit, add back non-cash expenses like depreciation that reduced profit without touching cash, and then adjust for changes in working capital."},{"beat":"The direction rule that trips everyone","text":"Those working-capital adjustments have a direction, and it's worth locking in. If something you're owed grew — accounts receivable went up — then sales were booked but the cash hasn't arrived, so you subtract. If something you owe grew — accounts payable went up — then expenses were booked but you haven't paid the cash yet, so you add it back. Assets and cash move opposite; liabilities and cash move together. Get that reflex right and the operating section falls into place."}]},{"id":"investments","name":"Investments","icon":"📈","group":"Assets","overview":"How the accounting for an investment hinges on what KIND of security it is and how much INFLUENCE or control the investor has.","lesson":[{"h":"Two questions drive everything","p":["How you account for an investment depends on (1) whether it's a DEBT or EQUITY security, and (2) for equity, how much INFLUENCE you have over the investee. Get those two right and the method follows.","The reason influence matters: at low ownership you're just a passive investor (mark to fair value), but once you can influence or control the investee, fair value stops making sense — you report your economic share of the investee's actual results."]},{"h":"Debt securities: three classifications","p":["HELD-TO-MATURITY (HTM): debt you have the intent and ability to hold to maturity. Reported at AMORTIZED COST; market fluctuations are ignored because you'll collect face at maturity anyway.","TRADING: debt (or equity) bought to sell short-term. Reported at FAIR VALUE with unrealized gains/losses flowing through NET INCOME.","AVAILABLE-FOR-SALE (AFS): debt that's neither HTM nor trading. Reported at FAIR VALUE, but unrealized gains/losses go to OTHER COMPREHENSIVE INCOME (OCI), bypassing net income until realized."]},{"h":"Equity securities: influence determines the method","p":["Less than 20% (no significant influence): generally fair value through NET INCOME.","20%–50% (SIGNIFICANT INFLUENCE): the EQUITY METHOD. You record the investment at cost, then increase it by your share of the investee's net income and decrease it by your share of losses and by dividends received.","Over 50% (CONTROL): CONSOLIDATION — you combine the subsidiary's financials with your own line by line."]},{"h":"The equity method's key counterintuitive rule","p":["Under the equity method, dividends received REDUCE the investment account — they are NOT income. Why? Because you already recognized income when the investee earned it (your share of net income). A dividend is the investee paying out earnings you've already booked, so it's a return OF investment, reducing the carrying amount.","Worked: buy 30% of Company X for $300,000. X earns $100,000 and pays $40,000 in total dividends. Investment = 300,000 + (30% × 100,000) − (30% × 40,000) = 300,000 + 30,000 − 12,000 = $318,000. The $30,000 is investment income; the $12,000 dividend just converts part of your investment into cash."]},{"h":"Equity method with excess purchase price","p":["When you buy an equity-method stake for more than your share of the investee's book value, the excess must be accounted for. Part may relate to undervalued assets (which get amortized against your investment income), and part may be goodwill (not amortized).","Example: pay $300,000 for 30% when 30% of book value is $240,000 — a $60,000 excess. If $40,000 of it relates to an undervalued depreciable asset with an 8-year life, you reduce investment income by $40,000 ÷ 8 = $5,000 per year. The remaining $20,000 is goodwill, left alone unless impaired."]},{"h":"Fair value adjustments and reclassification on sale","p":["For securities carried at fair value, you record a fair value adjustment each period. The destination differs: trading securities run the change through net income, while available-for-sale debt securities run it through other comprehensive income (OCI).","When an AFS security is sold, the cumulative gain or loss that was sitting in OCI is reclassified into net income — realized at last. Held-to-maturity debt, by contrast, stays at amortized cost throughout and ignores interim fair value changes entirely."]}],"keyFormulas":["HTM = amortized cost; Trading = fair value → net income; AFS = fair value → OCI","Equity <20% → fair value; 20–50% → equity method; >50% → consolidate","Equity method: cost + share of NI − share of losses − dividends received","Dividends under equity method reduce the investment (return OF investment)"],"bank":[{"d":"easy","q":"An investor owns 40% of an investee and can influence its policies. Which method applies?","choices":["Fair value through net income","Equity method","Consolidation","Amortized cost"],"answer":1,"why":["Fair value applies below 20%.","Correct: 20%–50% indicates significant influence → equity method.","Consolidation requires control (>50%).","Amortized cost is for HTM debt, not equity."]},{"d":"easy","q":"Unrealized gains/losses on TRADING securities are reported in:","choices":["Net income","OCI","Retained earnings directly","Not recognized"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: trading securities are marked to fair value with unrealized gains/losses in net income.","OCI is for AFS debt, not trading.","They flow through the income statement, not directly to RE.","They are recognized each period at fair value."]},{"d":"easy","q":"Held-to-maturity debt securities are reported at:","choices":["Fair value through net income","Fair value through OCI","Amortized cost","Lower of cost or market"],"answer":2,"why":["HTM ignores fair value changes.","OCI treatment is for AFS, not HTM.","Correct: HTM debt is carried at amortized cost since the investor will hold to maturity.","Lower-of-cost-or-market is an inventory rule, not for HTM debt."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Under the equity method, dividends received from the investee are:","choices":["Recorded as dividend revenue","Added to the investment account","Subtracted from the investment account","Ignored"],"answer":2,"why":["Income was already recognized when the investee earned it.","Dividends reduce, not increase, the investment.","Correct: dividends are a return OF investment, reducing the carrying amount of the investment.","They are recorded — as a reduction of the investment."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Unrealized gains/losses on AVAILABLE-FOR-SALE debt securities are reported in:","choices":["Net income","Other comprehensive income","The investing section of cash flows","They are not recognized"],"answer":1,"why":["AFS bypasses net income for unrealized changes.","Correct: AFS unrealized gains/losses go to OCI until the security is sold (realized).","OCI is an equity/income concept, not a cash-flow section.","They are recognized in OCI each period."]},{"d":"medium","q":"An investor buys 25% of an investee for $500,000; the investee reports a $40,000 net LOSS and pays no dividends. The investment balance becomes:","choices":["$510,000","$490,000","$500,000","$460,000"],"answer":1,"why":["A loss decreases, not increases, the investment.","Correct: equity method — investment = 500,000 − (25% × 40,000) = 500,000 − 10,000 = $490,000.","The loss must be reflected.","$460,000 uses the full loss, not the 25% share."]},{"d":"hard","q":"An investor owns 30% of an investee. The investee earns $200,000 and pays $60,000 in dividends. The investor's INCOME from this investment for the year is:","choices":["$60,000","$18,000","$42,000","$200,000"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: equity-method income = 30% × net income = 30% × 200,000 = $60,000 (dividends do not affect income).","$18,000 is 30% of dividends, which is not income under the equity method.","$42,000 incorrectly nets dividends against income.","$200,000 is the full investee income, not the investor's share."]},{"d":"hard","q":"A company holds AFS debt with a $100,000 cost; year-end fair value is $94,000. The $6,000 unrealized loss:","choices":["Reduces net income by $6,000","Reduces OCI by $6,000, with the security shown at $94,000","Is ignored until sale","Increases the investment to $106,000"],"answer":1,"why":["AFS unrealized losses do not hit net income.","Correct: the security is marked to $94,000 and the $6,000 unrealized loss is recorded in OCI until realized.","It is recognized now in OCI, not deferred off-statement.","Fair value fell, so the carrying amount decreases, not increases."]},{"d":"hard","q":"Ownership exceeding 50% of an investee's voting stock generally requires:","choices":["The equity method","Fair value through net income","Consolidation of the subsidiary's financials","Amortized cost"],"answer":2,"why":["The equity method applies to significant influence (20–50%), not control.","Fair value applies to passive holdings below 20%.","Correct: control (>50%) requires consolidation — combining the subsidiary line by line with the parent.","Amortized cost is a debt-security method."]},{"d":"easy","q":"Held-to-maturity classification can be used ONLY for:","choices":["Equity securities","Debt securities the investor intends and is able to hold to maturity","Any security held over a year","Trading securities"],"answer":1,"why":["Equity has no maturity date, so it can't be HTM.","Correct: HTM applies only to debt securities the investor has the positive intent and ability to hold until they mature.","Intent and ability to hold to maturity — not just time held — is required.","Trading is a different, short-term classification."]},{"d":"medium","q":"An investor owns 15% of a company and has no board seats or influence. The investment is generally accounted for using:","choices":["The equity method","Consolidation","Fair value through net income","Amortized cost"],"answer":2,"why":["The equity method requires significant influence (usually 20–50%).","Consolidation requires control (>50%).","Correct: below 20% with no influence, an equity investment is carried at fair value with changes through net income.","Amortized cost is for HTM debt, not equity."]},{"d":"medium","q":"Under the equity method, recognizing the investor's share of the investee's net income:","choices":["Decreases the investment account","Increases the investment account","Has no effect on the investment","Is recorded only when dividends are paid"],"answer":1,"why":["Income increases, not decreases, the investment.","Correct: the investor's share of investee net income increases both investment income and the carrying value of the investment.","It does affect the investment account.","Income recognition is independent of dividends under the equity method."]},{"d":"hard","q":"An investor's stake rises from 10% (fair value method) to 30% (equity method) mid-year. Going forward, the investor should:","choices":["Ignore the change","Apply the equity method prospectively from the date significant influence is achieved","Restate all prior years to the equity method","Switch to consolidation"],"answer":1,"why":["A change in influence level requires a change in method.","Correct: once significant influence is achieved, the equity method is applied prospectively from that point.","Retroactive restatement is not required for this transition under current guidance.","30% is significant influence, not control, so consolidation doesn't apply."]},{"d":"hard","q":"You pay $300,000 for a 30% stake when 30% of the investee's book value is $240,000. $40,000 of the $60,000 excess relates to an undervalued asset with an 8-year life. Annual reduction of investment income is:","choices":["$5,000","$7,500","$60,000","$0"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: $40,000 ÷ 8 years = $5,000 amortized against investment income each year.","$7,500 would amortize the full $60,000 over 8 years, but $20,000 is goodwill (not amortized).","$60,000 is the total excess, not the annual amount.","The undervalued-asset portion must be amortized."]},{"d":"medium","q":"An unrealized gain on an AVAILABLE-FOR-SALE debt security is reported in:","choices":["Other comprehensive income (OCI)","Net income","Retained earnings directly","It isn't recorded"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: AFS unrealized gains/losses go to OCI until the security is sold.","Trading securities go to net income; AFS goes to OCI.","It flows through OCI, not straight to retained earnings.","It is recorded, in OCI."]},{"d":"medium","q":"When an available-for-sale security is sold, the cumulative gain or loss previously in OCI is:","choices":["Reclassified into net income","Left in OCI permanently","Ignored","Recorded as a liability"],"answer":0,"why":["Correct: on sale, the accumulated OCI amount is reclassified ('recycled') into net income.","It moves out of OCI at sale.","It's realized, not ignored.","It's an equity reclassification, not a liability."]}],"worked":{"title":"Accounting for a 30% investment under the equity method","setup":"On January 1, an investor buys 30% of Company X for $300,000 cash. During the year, Company X reports net income of $100,000 and pays a total of $40,000 in dividends to all its shareholders. We need the year-end balance in the investment account.","steps":[{"do":"Determine the method. 30% ownership falls in the 20%–50% range, which means SIGNIFICANT INFLUENCE → use the EQUITY METHOD.","why":"Below 20% you'd just mark the investment to fair value. But at 30% the investor can influence Company X's decisions, so fair value no longer fits — instead you report your economic SHARE of what the investee actually does."},{"do":"Record the purchase. Dr Investment in Company X $300,000; Cr Cash $300,000. Starting balance: $300,000.","why":"The investment starts at cost — what you paid. From here, the balance will move up and down with the investee's performance."},{"do":"Add your share of the investee's net income: 30% × $100,000 = $30,000. Dr Investment $30,000; Cr Investment Income $30,000. Balance: $330,000.","why":"Under the equity method, when Company X earns money, your slice of it is YOUR income too — even though you haven't received any cash. You recognize $30,000 of investment income and increase the investment account to reflect that your stake is now worth more."},{"do":"Subtract your share of dividends received: 30% × $40,000 = $12,000. Dr Cash $12,000; Cr Investment $12,000. Balance: $330,000 − $12,000 = $318,000.","why":"This is the rule that trips everyone up: dividends are NOT income here. You already counted your share of earnings in the step above. A dividend is Company X handing you cash out of those earnings — so it converts part of your investment into cash, REDUCING the investment account. It's a return OF investment, not a return ON it."},{"do":"Final investment balance = $318,000.","why":"The account grew by your $30,000 share of profits and shrank by the $12,000 of dividends you collected. The $318,000 reflects your evolving economic stake in Company X."}],"takeaway":"At 20–50% ownership you use the equity method: start at cost, ADD your share of the investee's net income (that's your income), and SUBTRACT dividends received (a return of investment, not income, because you already booked the earnings). Dividends reducing the investment account is the signature move of the equity method."},"worked2":{"title":"A TRADING security (fair value through net income)","setup":"A company buys a trading security (a stock it intends to sell short-term) for $50,000. At year-end, its fair value has risen to $56,000. The company has not sold it. We'll contrast this with the AFS treatment.","steps":[{"do":"Classify it: bought to sell short-term → TRADING security.","why":"Classification drives everything. Trading securities are actively bought and sold for short-term profit, so accounting marks them to market with the change flowing through net income."},{"do":"Record the purchase. Dr Trading Securities 50,000; Cr Cash 50,000.","why":"Standard cost basis at acquisition — same as any investment at the start."},{"do":"At year-end, mark to fair value. The security rose from 50,000 to 56,000, an unrealized gain of $6,000.","why":"Even though you haven't sold, GAAP requires marking trading securities to their current market value each reporting date."},{"do":"Record the unrealized gain IN NET INCOME. Dr Fair Value Adjustment 6,000; Cr Unrealized Gain on Trading Securities 6,000 (income statement).","why":"This is the key contrast: for TRADING securities, unrealized gains/losses hit NET INCOME immediately. (For an AFS security, the identical $6,000 gain would instead go to OCI and bypass net income until sold.)"},{"do":"Result: net income rises by $6,000 this year even though no sale occurred.","why":"Trading classification makes earnings more volatile because market swings flow straight through the income statement. The same security classified as AFS would leave net income untouched until realized."}],"takeaway":"Trading securities are marked to fair value with unrealized gains and losses flowing through NET INCOME each period. That's the crucial contrast with available-for-sale securities, whose unrealized changes go to OCI and skip net income until the security is sold."},"practice":[{"prompt":"An investor buys 25% of an investee for $400,000. The investee reports net income of $120,000 and pays total dividends of $40,000. Compute the year-end investment balance (equity method).","solution":["25% = significant influence → equity method. Start at cost: $400,000.","Add share of net income: 25% × 120,000 = +$30,000 (this is investment income).","Subtract share of dividends: 25% × 40,000 = −$10,000 (a return OF investment, not income).","Ending balance = 400,000 + 30,000 − 10,000 = $420,000."]},{"prompt":"An investor owns 40% of an investee (equity method), with an investment carrying value of $600,000. The investee reports a net LOSS of $50,000 and pays no dividends. Compute the new investment balance.","solution":["Under the equity method, the investor records its share of the investee's loss as a reduction of the investment.","Share of loss = 40% × 50,000 = $20,000.","New balance = 600,000 − 20,000 = $580,000. Entry: Dr Loss on Investment 20,000 / Cr Investment 20,000."]},{"prompt":"A company holds an available-for-sale (AFS) debt security with a cost of $80,000. At year-end its fair value is $73,000. (a) Compute the unrealized gain/loss. (b) State where it is reported and the security's balance-sheet value.","solution":["(a) Unrealized loss = fair value − cost = 73,000 − 80,000 = −$7,000.","(b) For AFS securities, the $7,000 unrealized loss is reported in OTHER COMPREHENSIVE INCOME (OCI), NOT net income. The security is shown on the balance sheet at its $73,000 fair value.","Entry: Dr Unrealized Loss–OCI 7,000 / Cr Fair Value Adjustment (AFS) 7,000."]}],"entries":{"w1":[{"caption":"Purchase 30% of Company X","lines":[{"account":"Investment in Company X","dr":300000},{"account":"Cash","cr":300000}]},{"caption":"Record share of investee net income (30% × $100,000)","lines":[{"account":"Investment in Company X","dr":30000},{"account":"Investment Income","cr":30000}]},{"caption":"Record dividends received (30% × $40,000) — reduces the investment","lines":[{"account":"Cash","dr":12000},{"account":"Investment in Company X","cr":12000}]}],"w2":[{"caption":"Purchase the trading security","lines":[{"account":"Trading Securities","dr":50000},{"account":"Cash","cr":50000}]},{"caption":"Year-end — mark to fair value; unrealized gain flows through net income","lines":[{"account":"Fair Value Adjustment — Trading","dr":6000},{"account":"Unrealized Gain on Trading Securities (P&L)","cr":6000}]}]},"comptable":null,"aids":{"mnemonics":[{"tag":"Debt classifications — \"HTM-AFS-Trading\"","text":"\"HTM = cost; Trading = income; AFS = OCI.\" Held-to-maturity → amortized cost; Trading → fair value through net income; Available-for-sale → fair value through OCI."},{"tag":"Influence ladder","text":"\"Under 20 fair value · 20–50 equity · over 50 consolidate.\" Ownership % sets the method."},{"tag":"Equity method moves","text":"\"Plus income, minus dividends.\" Investment goes UP by your share of net income, DOWN by dividends received."}],"watch":["Under the equity method, dividends received REDUCE the investment (a return OF investment) — they are NOT income.","Trading unrealized gains/losses hit NET INCOME; AFS unrealized gains/losses go to OCI. Same fair-value math, different destination.","HTM is only for DEBT (equity has no maturity), and only if you have intent AND ability to hold.","When influence rises into the 20–50% range, switch to the equity method PROSPECTIVELY."],"cheat":"Two dials decide everything: 'How much control do I have?' and 'What kind of security is it?' Low control = mark to fair value (you're a spectator). High control = report your share of what they actually earn (you're a part-owner). For equity-method dividends, remember you already booked the earnings — a dividend is just cashing some of that out, so it shrinks the investment."},"plain":{"plain":"When one company owns a piece of another, how it keeps score depends on how much of it it owns. A little bit, with no say in things? Just track what it's worth, like a stock in your portfolio. A meaningful chunk — enough to influence decisions? Then you count your share of whatever profit they make as your own. Own more than half? You basically run them, so you fold their whole books into yours. The trickiest rule: when you own a meaningful chunk, a dividend they pay you isn't income — you already counted your share of their profit, so the dividend is just them handing you some of money you'd already claimed.","terms":[{"term":"Fair value method","plain":"track the investment at its market price, like a normal stock holding"},{"term":"Equity method","plain":"count your share of the other company's profit as your own"},{"term":"Significant influence","plain":"owning enough (roughly 20–50%) to sway their decisions"},{"term":"Consolidation","plain":"owning over half, so you combine their books fully into yours"},{"term":"Held-to-maturity","plain":"a bond you plan to keep until it pays off, so you ignore price swings"}]},"lecture":[{"beat":"It all hinges on how much you own","text":"When one company holds a stake in another, the accounting depends almost entirely on a single thing: how much influence that stake gives. A sliver with no say is treated completely differently from a controlling majority. So before anything else, ask how much of the other company you own and how much sway that buys you."},{"beat":"The influence ladder","text":"Own a small piece with no real influence — under about 20% — and you just track it at market value, like any stock in a portfolio. Own enough to influence decisions — roughly 20% to 50% — and you use the equity method, counting your share of their actual profits as your own. Own more than half and you control them, so you fold their entire financials into yours through consolidation. Three rungs, set by influence."},{"beat":"The rule that trips everyone","text":"The equity method has one counterintuitive move worth dwelling on. When the company you've invested in pays you a dividend, you do NOT record income. Why? Because you already recorded your share of their profit when they earned it. A dividend is just them handing you cash out of profit you'd already claimed — so it reduces your investment balance rather than adding income. It's a return of investment, not a return on it."},{"beat":"And for bonds you hold","text":"Debt investments split by your intent. If you mean to hold a bond to maturity, you carry it at amortized cost and ignore the day-to-day price swings, since you'll collect face at the end regardless. If you're holding to trade, you mark it to market and run the swings through income. And the in-between category runs those unrealized swings through other comprehensive income instead, parking them outside net income until you actually sell."}]}];


