// ---- Curriculum: the guided learning path ----
// Ordered so prerequisites come first. Each step explains WHY it's placed here.
const PATH = [
  { id: "bonds", phase: "Foundations of Liabilities", why: "Start here. Bonds teach present value, carrying value, and effective-interest amortization — the mechanical engine that leases, pensions, and income taxes all reuse. Master this and the next three topics feel familiar." },
  { id: "leases", phase: "Foundations of Liabilities", why: "Leases are bonds in disguise: the lease liability amortizes exactly like a bond. You already know the machinery — now apply it to a new context and learn the classification tests." },
  { id: "income-tax", phase: "Foundations of Liabilities", why: "Income taxes introduce the book-vs-tax distinction and deferred tax assets/liabilities. It leans on the same timing-difference logic, and shows up inside almost every other topic." },
  { id: "pensions", phase: "Foundations of Liabilities", why: "Pensions combine present value (the obligation) with a multi-part expense. With bonds and taxes behind you, the moving pieces here will click into place." },
  { id: "equity", phase: "The Equity Side", why: "Shift from what a company owes to what it owns and owes its shareholders. Equity is more intuitive and gives your brain a breather before the dilution math." },
  { id: "eps", phase: "The Equity Side", why: "EPS builds directly on equity — you need to understand shares, preferred dividends, and convertibles first. This is one of the most heavily tested topics, so it gets prime placement after its prerequisite." },
  { id: "investments", phase: "The Equity Side", why: "Investments tie equity and debt together (you're now the one holding the securities). The fair-value vs. equity-method split echoes the classification thinking you used for leases." },
  { id: "revenue", phase: "Income & Reporting", why: "Revenue recognition is the capstone conceptual topic — the 5-step model governs the top line of every company. It's principle-heavy rather than math-heavy, a good late-stage topic." },
  { id: "cash-flows", phase: "Income & Reporting", why: "Finish here. The statement of cash flows synthesizes everything — it pulls from liabilities, equity, investments, and revenue. It only makes full sense once you've seen the other topics, so it's the natural finale." },
];

